window.LT_INDEX=[{"u":"ALI/ALI.html","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"ALI does not compute anything itself: it is a dispatch widget that chooses between two backends with no shared code — ALI_CBCT ( reinforcement-learning agents that move through a volume) and ALI_IOS (multi-view rendering and per-pixel segmentation on a mesh) — to place anatomical landmarks in LPS ."},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#situation","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"ALI is a landmark provider for almost the whole repository. Nobody calls the widget itself; both CLIs are called, though. Caller Backend How Why ALI.py:999 ALI_CBCT slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.ali_cbct, …) direct use ALI.py:1246 ALI_IOS conda run -n shapeaxi python -m ALI_IOS direct use ASO_Method/CBCT.py:515 ALI_CBCT slicer.cli.run landmarks for SEMI_ASO orientation AREG_Method/IOSCBCT.py:300 both slicer.cli.run 12 occlusal points on CBCT and on IOS, to register one onto the other GreedyReg_Method/Logic.py:493 ALI_CBCT slicer.cli.run \"Distant Registration\": landmark-based registration FlexR"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#fichiers","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs ALI/ALI.py widget, 2286 lines: dispatch, UI, weight download, conda bootstrap Slicer Python ALI_Method/CBCT.py builds ALI_CBCT's parameters same ALI_Method/IOS.py builds those for segmentation + ALI_IOS same ALI_CBCT/ALI_CBCT.py CLI, 270 lines: preprocessing then the agent loop Slicer Python , via slicer.cli.run ALI_CBCT_utils/ agent, brain, environment, preprocess same ALI_IOS/ALI_IOS.py CLI, 688 lines: a single 500-line main function conda env shapeaxi ALI_IOS_utils/ agent (cameras), render, surface, model (tables), io, orientation, segmentation same Non-obvious point"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#ali-cbct","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"3. ALI_CBCT — agents inside the volume","x":"ALI_CBCT: one agent per landmark, reused for every patient, walks voxel by voxel until it oscillates, changes scale, then averages six refinements ( Focus ). Drag to rotate · scroll to zoom Basion Ba The anterior margin of the foramen magnum, on the skull base. Sella S The centre of the sella turcica, the hollow that holds the pituitary. Nasion N The bridge of the nose, where the frontal and nasal bones meet. Replay the search walking, one voxel per step oscillated — arrived. Scale now steps to reach the landmark The real path, not a reconstruction: ali_trace.py reads it out of the agent’s own"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#ali-ios","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"4. ALI_IOS — multi-view rendering","x":"ALI_IOS: the network (2D UNet) segments each rendered view at the pixel level; everything else — camera placement, pixel→vertex mapping, scaling back to the original size — is geometric. Drag to rotate · click a tooth Run the camera pass dentalmodelseg numbers the teeth first — camera placed on tooth UNet segmenting the render of tooth landmarks placed, one camera pass per tooth The published test arch (ASO_IOS). It starts as plain surface: the numbering comes first, from dentalmodelseg , and everything depends on it — the label array is what gives each tooth’s centroid, and therefore where th"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#predit-calcule","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"5. What's predicted vs. what's computed","x":"Block Nature Where Histogram windowing, resampling computed geometric (SimpleITK / itk) CBCT CLI, Slicer Python Agent's move direction predicted DenseNet3D + MLP network, 6 classes CBCT CLI Stopping criterion, scale-up, Focus computed geometric (oscillation detection, averaging) CBCT CLI Crown segmentation predicted multi-view network (DentalModelSeg) conda shapeaxi Arch leveling (MG) computed geometric, SVD on centroids IOS CLI Camera placement computed geometric (centroids, arch tangent, MG_AIM_OFFSET prior) IOS CLI Per-pixel class map predicted monai 2D UNet network IOS CLI Pixel → face → v"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#environnement","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"6. Environment","x":"Item Value Source CBCT backend Slicer Python + itk , dicom2nifti==2.6.2 , pydicom==3.0.2 , monai ( 1.3.2 if Python ≥ 3.10, otherwise 0.7.0 ) ALI.py:875 IOS backend conda env shapeaxi , shared with AREG, ASO , FlexReg , DOCShapeAXI ALI.py:2108 Env Python 3.12 ALI.py:2110 Env creation torch>=2.8,<2.13 , ocnn==2.2.1 , SimpleITK ALI.py:2132 pytorch3d wheel chosen based on the installed torch version, PEP 503 index ImageMindAnalytics.github.io/pytorch3d-wheels install_pytorch.py shapeaxi >=2.0.2 , installed after pytorch3d install_pytorch.py:189 Windows conda via WSL ( CondaSetUpCallWsl ), checks f"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#pieges","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"7. Pitfalls and fragile spots","x":"Focus() has no safeguard : no time budget, no search_atempt check. It's the only unbounded loop in the CBCT pipeline. Move() tests its bounds wrong : if new_pos.all() > 0 and (...) — .all() returns a boolean, compared against 0 . The test checks \"no coordinate is zero,\" not \"no coordinate is negative\" ( agent.py:193 ). The int16 cast before argmax is still there on the O and C models. The comment in the MG code explains precisely why it's wrong. get_view_rasterize for non-MG rasterizes without R / T : renderer.rasterizer(meshes.clone()) ( agent.py:366 ) doesn't pass the matrices used by the re"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#litterature","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"8. Literature","x":"Both backends have their own paper, and the code matches what they describe — with one reservation for ALI_IOS. ALI_CBCT — Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography , Gillot, Baquero, Cevidanes et al., Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research , 2023. Detection is reframed as a classification problem : a virtual agent placed in the volume learns to navigate a multi-scale space toward the landmark. Verified Reported mean error 1.54 ± 0.87 mm over 32 landmarks, 4.2 s inference. That's exactly what the code does, including the two scales, 1 mm / 0.3 mm. Original repository: Ma"},{"u":"ALI/SOURCES.html","p":"ALI — sources","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"ALI's bibliography: what has been archived in this folder, what's left to read online, and the neighborhood — related work that doesn't describe the code. ALI is the best-published module in the repository: each of the two backends has its own paper, and those papers describe the code well. ALI_CBCT is covered by an open-access journal article (Orthod Craniofac Res 2023), ALI_IOS by a paywalled LNCS chapter (CLIP 2022) with an author's version in circulation. The sheet's reservation holds up: the MG path (mucogingival line) of the ALI_IOS code is not described in any publication — no article, "},{"u":"ALI/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"ALI — sources","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"Archived in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2023_Gillot_ALICBCT_landmark_identification.xml / .txt Gillot M, Miranda F, Baquero B, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Anchling L, Hutin N, Biggs E, Yatabe M, Paniagua B, Fillion-Robin JC, Allemang D, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC. Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography. Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research, 2023, 26(4), 560-567 Journal article — the ALI_CBCT paper 10.1111/ocr.12642 · PMID 36811276 · PMCID PMC10440369 · CC BY-NC-ND 2022_Baquero_ALIIOS_landmark_intraoral_scans.pdf Baquero B, Gillot M, Cevidanes L, Al Turkestani "},{"u":"ALI/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"ALI — sources","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Leclercq M, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Yatabe M, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, Styner M, Paniagua B, Prieto JC. DentalModelSeg: fully automated segmentation of upper and lower 3D intra-oral surfaces. Proc. IEEE ISBI 2023 Freely readable on Europe PMC PMC10949221 ; neither JATS nor BioC available, PDF behind Cloudflare 10.1109/ISBI53787.2023.10230397 Baquero B, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, et al. ALIDDM: Automatic Landmark Identification in Digital Dental Models. 51st AADOCR/CADR Annual Meeting, Atlanta (hybrid), March 2022, abstract 3668353 IADR Abstract Archives — IADR account required — G"},{"u":"ALI/SOURCES.html#voie-mg","p":"ALI — sources","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"What was searched for the MG path","x":"Verified The sheet claims the mucogingival line is published nowhere. Verified, and nothing contradicts it: Europe PMC: \"mucogingival\" AND AUTH:\"Cevidanes L\" (13 results, none about automatic landmark identification), \"mucogingival\" AND AUTH:\"Prieto JC\" (1 result, unrelated), \"mucogingival\" AND \"intraoral scan\" AND \"deep learning\" (3 results, all unrelated abstract collections). OpenAlex: ALIIOS , mucogingival line automatic landmark identification , gingival margin landmark detection intraoral scan neural network — nothing from the DCBIA group. The CLIP paper itself does not contain a single "},{"u":"ALI/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"ALI — sources","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"Neighborhood","x":"Related work that does not describe ALI but sheds light on the problem. Alansary et al. 2019 (archived here): a comparative evaluation of reinforcement-learning agents for anatomical landmark detection. This is the general reference for the moving-agent mechanism described in the sheet. Ghesu et al. 2016 (MICCAI) and 2019 (TPAMI) : the origin of this mechanism, an artificial agent followed by multi-scale deep reinforcement learning. This is reference 20 of the ALI_CBCT paper, the only one it cites for the agent part. Maxime Gillot has no prior work of his own on the subject : a review of his p"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"Automatic multi-structure segmentation of craniofacial CBCT scans: one binary nnU-Net v2 network per anatomical structure, assembled and merged by the CLI."},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#situation","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"1. Where it sits in the chain","x":"AMASSS is both a terminal module (the AMASSS widget) and a building block reused by three other tools , all of which call the same CLI through slicer.modules.amasss_cli . Caller File Usage AMASSS widget AMASSS.py:1638 operator-driven segmentation AREG_CBCT (semi-auto, fully-auto, oriented) CBCT.py:341 , 470 , 555 , 840 , 918 two distinct roles: registration masks ( merge=SEPARATE , genVtk=False ) before AREG, then deliverable segmentations afterwards VFACE createlistprocess.py:126 segmentation within the asymmetry-analysis chain AMASSS calls nothing else: no conda subprocess, no other module. "},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#fichiers","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs AMASSS/AMASSS.py Slicer widget, 1654 lines, of which ~450 are light/dark stylesheets Slicer's Python AMASSS_CLI/AMASSS_CLI.py all the logic: model discovery, inference, output assembly Slicer's Python, via slicer.cli.run AMASSS_CLI/AMASSS_CLI.xml declaration of the 12 positional parameters — The CLI is not launched inside a conda environment: slicer.cli.run starts it in Slicer's Python, and since the September 2026 port ( 109e1ed ) nnU-Net inference runs in this same process , no longer in a child nnUNetv2_predict . CUDA is not required: ResolveDevice() ( AMASSS_CLI.py:"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#entrees","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"3.1 File discovery main() accepts a file or a folder, walked with a recursive os.walk ( AMASSS_CLI.py:676 ) — necessary because AREG writes its registered scans to <output>/<Region>/<patient>_OutReg/ . Extensions kept: .nii , .nii.gz , .nrrd , .nrrd.gz . Two filters: any name containing MASK is skipped, and if isSegmentInput is false, any name containing _<prediction_ID>_ is skipped too — it is the output of a previous run, and re-segmenting it would produce an empty mask. Pitfall The widget, for its part, counts files with a different extension list ( .gipl included) and different exceptions "},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#modeles","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"4. The models","x":"4.1 The bundle and its discovery FindModelFolder() ( AMASSS_CLI.py:89 ) looks, for each structure code, for: <modelDirectory>/<CODE>/**/<Dataset...>__nnUNetPlans__3d_fullres/fold_0/checkpoint_final.pth A candidate is only accepted once the fold_0 checkpoint is confirmed present ; otherwise the structure is declared unavailable and the run continues with the others. Discovery happens once for the whole run . Download: the Download latest models button simply opens a browser ( AMASSS.py:738 ) on MODEL_LINK , the releases/tag/AMASSS_CBCT page — no automatic download on the AMASSS side. The archiv"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#coeur","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"5. The algorithmic core (not learned)","x":"5.1 GPU resampling EnableGpuResampling() ( AMASSS_CLI.py:150 ) is the CLI's main optimization. According to the code comment, single-core scipy resampling costs roughly seven times as much as the network. The mechanism: nnU-Net resolves its resampling functions by name , through recursive_find_resampling_fn_by_name , from the configuration dict. Rewriting the two names redirects both ends, with no monkeypatch: configuration[\"resampling_fn_data\"] = \"resample_torch_fornnunet\" configuration[\"resampling_fn_probabilities\"] = \"resample_torch_fornnunet\" configuration[f\"{key}_kwargs\"] = {\"is_seg\": Fal"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#sorties","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"6. Post-processing and outputs","x":"Naming — base = scan name without extension, ext = original extension: Mode File Content SEPARATE <base>_<prediction_ID>_<STRUCT><ext> computed binary int16 mask, one per structure MERGE <base>_<prediction_ID>_MERGED<ext> computed int16 label map per LABELS[\"LARGE\"] genVtk <same name>.vtk computed colored surface(s) With save_in_folder , everything goes into <output_folder>/<base>_<prediction_ID>_SegOut/ ; otherwise directly into <output_folder> . Confirmed bug The temporary folder is wiped and recreated at both startup and the end . nnunet_input is deleted as soon as inference finishes, befor"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#predit-calcule","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"7. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Preprocessing (resampling, CTNormalization ) computed deterministic, driven by plans.json nnU-Net, in-process Segmentation of each structure predicted 3D PlainConvUNet network, one per structure, binary nnU-Net, in-process, GPU if available Sliding window, gaussian, step 0.5 computed deterministic nnU-Net Output resampling computed deterministic, torch (GPU) or scipy nnU-Net, resampler substituted by AMASSS Binarization + label merging computed trivial geometry ( np.where ) CLI Geometry registration to the scan computed ITK, nearest neighbor, a no-op in practice CLI VTK surf"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#environnement","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"8. Environment","x":"Everything runs in Slicer's Python. Installation is triggered by the Predict button ( AMASSS.py:851 ), not when the module opens, and goes through a confirmation dialog. Package Version pinned Reason readable in the code torch , torchvision , torchaudio >=2.2.0 (CUDA wheels) download.pytorch.org/whl/cu118 or cu121 index depending on the detected CUDA version nnunetv2 2.8.0 inference backend numpy 1.26.4 ( NUMPY_PINNED_VERSION ) torch 2.2.0 is built against numpy 1.x; numpy ≥ 2 breaks every torch import with _ARRAY_API not found pydicom 3.0.2 do not downgrade : pydicom 2.x breaks dicomweb-clien"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#pieges","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug — small FOV broken highDefinition / \"small FOV\" no longer does anything it claims to. The smallFOVCheckBox switches the offered structure list to GROUPS_HD_SEG ( AMASSS.py:747 ) — mandible, maxilla, teeth, root canal, mandibular canal, three of which have no model. The param[\"highDefinition\"] flag is not declared in the XML , slicer.cli.run drops it, and the CLI no longer has any SMALL code path anyway (spacing [0.16, 0.16, 0.32] and MODELS_GROUP[\"SMALL\"] disappeared with the port). Checking the box only changes the list of checkboxes. Confirmed bug — DICOM input ignored isDCMInp"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#litterature","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"10. Literature","x":"AMASSS is published once: Gillot M., Baquero B., Le C., Deleat-Besson R., Bianchi J., Ruellas A., …, Cevidanes L., Prieto J.C. Automatic multi-anatomical skull structure segmentation of cone-beam computed tomography scans using 3D UNETR. PLOS ONE 17(10):e0275033, 2022. doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0275033 — PMID 36223330, PMC9555672 Code / paper divergence The code has diverged from the paper, completely. The paper describes a UNETR (transformer encoder + convolutional decoder, MONAI library): 128³ patches, feature_size=16 , hidden_size=768 , mlp_dim=3072 , 12 attention heads, 5% dropout, trained "},{"u":"AMASSS/SOURCES.html","p":"AMASSS — sources","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for this tool: what is deposited in this folder, what is meant to be read online, and the neighborhood. AMASSS is published once, open access (PLOS ONE 2022), but the paper describes a dead version of the module : a UNETR MONAI model with a 128³ sliding window, whereas the code that runs today is an nnU-Net v2 (one binary network per structure, PlainConvUNet architecture coming from the weights' plans.json ). The switch dates to commit 61bbb15 \"ENH: AMASSS with nnunet\" (2025-07-30); commit 109e1ed (2026-09-09) later replaced the subprocess call with nnU-Net's Python API. Code / pa"},{"u":"AMASSS/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AMASSS — sources","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2022_Gillot_AMASSS_skull_segmentation.pdf Gillot M., Baquero B., Le C., Deleat-Besson R., Bianchi J., Ruellas A., Gurgel M., Yatabe M., Al Turkestani N., Najarian K., Soroushmehr R., Pieper S., Kikinis R., Paniagua B., Gryak J., Ioshida M., Massaro C., Gomes L., Oh H., Evangelista K., Chaves Junior C.M., Garib D., Costa F., Benavides E., Soki F., Fillion-Robin J.-C., Joshi H., Cevidanes L., Prieto J.C. Automatic multi-anatomical skull structure segmentation of cone-beam computed tomography scans using 3D UNETR. PLOS ONE 17(10):e0275033, 2022, 12 p. Journal article,"},{"u":"AMASSS/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AMASSS — sources","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Isensee F., Jaeger P.F., Kohl S.A.A., Petersen J., Maier-Hein K.H. nnU-Net: a self-configuring method for deep learning-based biomedical image segmentation. Nature Methods 18(2):203-211, 2021. Reference version to cite ; only the arXiv preprint is freely redistributable. nature.com 10.1038/s41592-020-01008-z"},{"u":"AMASSS/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AMASSS — sources","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"Neighborhood","x":"These works do not describe AMASSS ; they document its environment or its building blocks. nnU-Net (Isensee et al. 2021, above): the foundation of the module's current version. It describes the self-configuring method, not the AMASSS model or its weights, whose training is documented nowhere. UNETR (Hatamizadeh et al. 2022): the architecture of the PLOS ONE paper, so of the historical version of the CLI. Unrelated to what runs today. Maxlo24/AMASSS_CBCT — the original repository cited by the paper; research code, not the Slicer module. DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools — the module's r"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Rigid T1/T2 registration of CBCT scans onto a reference bone region (cranial base, mandible, or maxilla): plain elastix , mutual information, no network — everything learned sits upstream, in the masks and the landmarks."},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#situation","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"AREG/AREG.py is a single widget that drives three modalities: CBCT ( AREG/AREG_Method/CBCT.py ), IOS ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py ) and IOS-on-CBCT ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOSCBCT.py ). It computes nothing itself: each Method.Process() returns a list of step dictionaries , and the widget runs them one after another. A step always has the following shape: {\"Process\": slicer.modules.areg_cbct, # Slicer CLI module \"Parameter\": {...}, # dict passed as-is to slicer.cli.run \"Module\": \"AREG_CBCT for Mandible\", # label shown in the UI \"ReviewId\": \"cbct_registration\", # optional review pause \"ReviewFolder\": \"."},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#fichiers","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs AREG/AREG.py widget, 3336 lines: UI, step chaining, review pauses, library installation Slicer's Python AREG/AREG_Method/CBCT.py the 3 CBCT modes, building the step lists, T1/T2 pairing same AREG/AREG_Method/Review.py review pauses: patient queue, loading, writing corrections, rollback same AREG/AREG_Method/Progress.py translation of <filter-progress> into a progress bar same AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.py the CLI: patient loop, writing outputs separate process , Slicer's python-real AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT_utils/utils.py pairing, masking, elastix, matrices same Unlike the IOS path"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#modes","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"3. The three modes and the exact chain","x":"The mode is chosen by CbModeType ; SwitchType() ( AREG.py:930 ) instantiates the matching class. All three share the same tail end — center T2, run AREG per region, final AMASSS — and only differ upstream: Mode Class Upstream T1 masks Semi-Automated Semi_CBCT ( CBCT.py:266 ) none supplied by the operator (\"T1 Masks\" field) Fully-Automated Auto_CBCT ( CBCT.py:446 ) none AMASSS CBMASK / MANDMASK / MAXMASK , written into the input T1 folder predicted Orientation & Registration Or_Auto_CBCT ( CBCT.py:731 ) PRE_ASO → ALI_CBCT → SEMI_ASO on T1, into <T1 folder>Or AMASSS on <T1 folder>Or predicted Th"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#entrees","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"4. Inputs, pairing, preprocessing","x":"Formats. .nii.gz , .nii , .nrrd , .nrrd.gz , .gipl , .gipl.gz . For DICOM input, convertdicom2nifti ( utils.py:720 ) converts a per-patient subfolder via sitk.ImageSeriesReader , falling back to dicom2nifti , into <folder>/NIFTI/<patient>.nii.gz . T1/T2 pairing — GetPatients ( utils.py:91 ). The patient identifier is whatever remains of the file name after a chain of split() calls on a fixed list of markers ( _Scan , _Or , _MAND , _CB , _lm , _T2 , _T1 , _Cl …). Then: an image file whose name contains mask , seg or pred → segmentation; otherwise → scanT1 / scanT2 ; if segmentationType is given"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#recalage","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"5. The algorithmic core — the registration","x":"Cranial base Mandible Maxilla Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom Register again 0. upstream — ALI_CBCT places the landmarks 1. read the moving T2 as itk.F 2. AMASSS predicts the mask 3. mask the fixed T1 — this is all elastix will see 4. one elastix pass: Mattes MI, 3 pyramid levels, 1500 iterations each 5. MatrixRetrieval → Euler3DTransform 6. resample T2 — on its own grid, not T1's ALI_CBCT places the landmarks the orientation needs — read the moving T2 as itk.F AMASSS predicts the mask mask the fixed T1 — this is all elastix will see one elastix pass: Mattes MI, 3 pyramid levels, 1500"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#amont","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"6. What runs upstream: exact arguments","x":"AMASSS — registration masks ( CBCT.py:456 ): {\"inputVolume\": <T1 folder>, # or <T1 folder>Or \"modelDirectory\": <model_folder_1>/AMASSS_Models, \"skullStructure\": \"CBMASK,MANDMASK,MAXMASK\", # depending on checked boxes \"merge\": \"SEPARATE\", \"genVtk\": False, \"save_in_folder\": False, \"output_folder\": <T1 folder>, # next to the scans \"vtk_smooth\": 5, \"prediction_ID\": \"seg\", \"temp_fold\": <Documents>/Slicer_temp_AMASSS, \"SegmentInput\": False, \"DCMInput\": False} The name written is <base>_seg_<STRUCT>.<ext> ( AMASSS_CLI.py:600 ) — so P1_T1_seg_MANDMASK.nii.gz , which the second pass of GetPatients find"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#sorties","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Outputs","x":"Written by AREG_CBCT.py:152 : <output>/<Cranial Base|Mandible|Maxilla>/<patient>_OutReg/ <patient>_<CB|MAND|MAX>Scan<add_name>.nii.gz # registered T2, int16 <patient>_<CB|MAND|MAX><add_name>_matrix.tfm # the transform The subfolder name comes from translate() ( utils.py:714 ): CB → Cranial Base . add_name is concatenated with no separator . Conventions of the .tfm : sitk.WriteTransform of an Euler3DTransform , so LPS and the ITK convention (the transform maps output space to input space). No flip is needed anywhere in the CLI, everything there is SimpleITK/elastix; the only place in the CBCT c"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#revue","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Review pauses and batch navigation","x":"Added by commit 2609bc4 (\"Pause VFACE at chosen steps\"… and AREG before it). In \"Review and manual adjustment,\" the operator checks the steps where the run should stop; the selection is stored in QSettings under AREG/ReviewSteps ( AREG.py:1922 ). The breakpoints available in CBCT ( getReviewSteps , CBCT.py:258 ), and what they allow ( CATALOGUE , Review.py:568 ): Id After Nature cbct_landmarks_orientation ALI_CBCT (Orientation mode) editable : points can be moved and are rewritten cbct_oriented SEMI_ASO view only cbct_masks AMASSS masks view only cbct_centered_t2 PRE_ASO T2 view only cbct_regi"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#predit-calcule","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"9. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where T1/T2 centering (PRE_ASO) computed geometric, translation of the center Slicer CLI Orientation landmarks ( ALI_CBCT ) predicted 3D DenseNet network, multi-scale RL agents Slicer CLI, CUDA if available Orientation (SEMI_ASO) computed rigid VTK ICP on landmarks Slicer CLI Reference-region masks ( AMASSS ) predicted nnU-Net v2 3d_fullres network Slicer CLI, GPU T1/T2 registration (AREG_CBCT) computed rigid elastix, mutual information Slicer CLI, CPU Final segmentations (AMASSS) predicted nnU-Net v2 network Slicer CLI, GPU Manual correction computed Slicer transform composed int"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#environnement","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"10. Environment","x":"Everything is installed into Slicer's Python by install_function ( AREG.py:86 ) when the launch button is clicked, after a confirmation dialog: itk==5.4.0 , itk-elastix==0.19.2 (the registration engine), dicom2nifti==2.6.2 , torch==2.2.0 ( cu118 wheel on Windows), monai==1.3.2 , nnunetv2>=2.8.0 and torchvision ( Linux/macOS only ), numpy<2.0.0 re-forced afterward if numpy ≥ 2 is detected, plus connected-components-3d , einops , nibabel , pandas . pydicom is pinned to ==3.0.2 , Slicer's own version: downgrading it breaks dicomweb-client and highdicom , hence every DICOM module in Slicer. Trap c"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#pieges","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"11. Traps and fragile spots","x":"The _T1 / _T2 suffixes. Commit 05d1fec flagged every site with the keyword TIMEPOINT-SUFFIX . The reference note sits above GetPatients ( utils.py:68 ). Trap The problem: the patient id is whatever remains of the name after splitting on a fixed list of markers, and the only time points on that list are _T1 and _T2 . A P001_T3.nii.gz keeps the id P001_T3 , pairs with nothing, and the pair is silently dropped — no exception. The actual time point does not come from the name but from the time_point argument, i.e. from the folder; only the id extraction is at fault. The sites, within AREG's scope:"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#litterature","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"12. Literature","x":"The reference paper is Anchling, Hutin, Huang, Barone, Roberts, Miranda, Gurgel, Al Turkestani, Tinawi, Bianchi, Yatabe, Ruellas, Prieto, Cevidanes, \"Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans,\" LNCS 14242, 2023, p. 43-58 — Springer , PMC11104011 . Reported accuracy: < 3° and < 2 mm against experts, under 5 min per case. The code diverges from the paper on three verified points: the paper describes a two-stage registration — \"VBR between the full images, then with the masked image as a refinement step,\" 10,000 iterations on the masked pass. The code now runs "},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/SOURCES.html","p":"AREG_CBCT — sources","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for AREG_CBCT: what is archived in this folder, what is still to be checked online, and the neighboring work that does not describe the code. AREG_CBCT is published only once , in a MICCAI workshop proceedings chapter (LNCS 14242, 2023) that it shares with ASO : the same text describes orientation (ASO) and registration (AREG). This chapter is paywalled at Springer, but the author manuscript is freely readable on PMC (PMC11104011); PMC, however, blocks automated PDF download, so it could not be archived here (see \"To check online\"). Trap The code has diverged from the paper on thr"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AREG_CBCT — sources","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Archived in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2024_Barone_VSP_ClasseIII_voxel_based.xml / .txt Barone S., Cevidanes L., Miranda F., Gurgel M.L., Anchling L., Hutin N., Bianchi J., Goncalves J.R., Giudice A. Enhancing skeletal stability and Class III correction through active orthodontist engagement in virtual surgical planning: A voxel-based 3-dimensional analysis. American Journal of Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopedics, 165(3):321-331, 2024. Journal article, full JATS text (Europe PMC) + plain text — CC-BY license 10.1016/j.ajodo.2023.09.016 — PMID 38010236, PMCID PMC10923113 2022_Gillot_AMASSS_skull_seg"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AREG_CBCT — sources","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"To check online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Anchling L., Hutin N., Huang Y., Barone S., Roberts S., Miranda F., Gurgel M., Al Turkestani N., Tinawi S., Bianchi J., Yatabe M., Ruellas A., Prieto J.C., Cevidanes L. Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans. Lecture Notes in Computer Science 14242, Springer, Cham, 2023, p. 43-58. Joint proceedings of CLIP 2023 (12th International Workshop on Clinical Image-Based Procedures), FAIMI 2023 and EPIMI 2023, held with MICCAI 2023, Vancouver, October 8-12, 2023. Eds. Wesarg, Puyol Antón, Baxter et al. ISBN 978-3-031-45248-2 / 978-3-031-45249-"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AREG_CBCT — sources","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighboring work","x":"These works do not describe AREG_CBCT ; they underpin or surround the method. Cevidanes 2005 (DMFR) and Nada 2011 (PLOS ONE) : voxel-based superimposition on the anterior cranial base. Note: contrary to what the AREG_CBCT.md page might suggest, the 2011 PLOS ONE paper is not from the Cevidanes lab: it is signed by Nada, Maal, Kuijpers-Jagtman et al. (Radboud UMC, Nijmegen). It is an independent validation of the method, which makes it more useful, not less. Ruellas 2016 (mandible) and Ruellas 2016 (maxilla) : the two papers that established the reference regions used by regional registration —"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/SOURCES.html#recuperation","p":"AREG_CBCT — sources","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"How the full text was retrieved","x":"The chapter is paywalled at Springer, but the author manuscript deposited with the NIH is freely accessible. Two approaches fail on it and one works — noted here to avoid retracing the whole search: Route Result on this PMCID pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/.../pdf/ anti-bot HTML BioC API pmcoa.cgi \"No result can be found\" — it only serves the open access subset eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=pmc&id=<number> full JATS, body included curl -sL \"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=pmc&id=11104011\" This is how the .xml and .txt files in this folder were obtained"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Longitudinal T1/T2 registration of Intra Oral Scans by ICP restricted to a stable region — a palatal patch predicted by a network (Butterfly) or a mucogingival band computed from predicted landmarks (MGL), processed in batches of folders. Longitudinal T1/T2 registration of Intra Oral Scans. Same principle as FlexReg.md — a rigid ICP restricted to a stable region — but the region is predicted by a network instead of being drawn by the operator, and processing runs in batches of folders instead of patient by patient. Two modes coexist in the same CLI: Butterfly — upper arch, palatal patch predic"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#situation","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"AREG_IOS is a CLI. Its only caller in the repository is the AREG module, via AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py . It doesn't call any other module itself: everything it needs (segmentation, orientation, landmarks) is produced upstream by the steps the orchestrator stacks ahead of it. Upstream preprocessing depends on the mode (Butterfly vs MGL); the CLI then produces either a palatal patch predicted by a network, or an MGL band computed , before the rigid ICP. AREG mode Steps stacked before AREG_IOS Source Auto_IOS / Butterfly CrownSegmentationcli T1 and T2 → PRE_ASO_IOS T1 and T2 AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#fichiers","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.py the CLI, 451 lines: Butterfly loop, RunMGL , writing .tfm files conda env shapeaxi AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/PredPatch.py palatal patch inference + morphological post-processing same, CUDA mandatory AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/net.py MonaiUNetHRes : PyTorch3D rendering + MONAI UNet same AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/dataset.py T1/T2 pairing, canonical orientation, normalization, tensors same AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/mgl_patch.py MGL band: spline, snap, Dijkstra via binary heap same, CPU AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/ICP.py wrapped vtkIterativeClosestPointTransform sam"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#entrees","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"3.1 T1/T2 pairing Sort() ( AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/dataset.py:98 ) and sort() ( AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/dataset.py:267 ): glob over both folders, filtered to .vtk .vtp .stl .obj (the .tfm files are explicitly excluded); files are classified Upper/Lower by substring search: [\"Lower\",\"_L\",\"L_\",\"Mandibule\",\"Md\"] and [\"Upper\",\"_U\",\"U_\",\"Maxilla\",\"Mx\"] ; pairing works by literally stripping the T1 / T2 substring from the basename and comparing what's left; matching Upper↔Lower for the same patient works by additionally stripping the words Upper/Lower and comparing. Pitfall Non-negotiable assumption"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#modele","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"4. The model — MonaiUNetHRes","x":"Weights file: a single .ckpt in the model folder, selected by getModel(..., extension=\"ckpt\") ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py:266 ). Source: AREG_model.zip , release HUTIN1/AREG v1.0.0 ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py:272 ). Loaded via torch.load(path)[\"state_dict\"] — a PyTorch Lightning checkpoint. Multi-view rendering, a per-view 2D UNet predicted , reprojection onto the mesh by voting, then a morphological closing computed to fill in the holes left by a degenerate vote (writes only to the first vertex of each triangle). 4.1 Architecture AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/net.py:61 . A LightningModule that contains"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#mgl","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"5. MGL mode — lower arch","x":"The palate gives a stable plateau up top; the mandible has none. The substitute is the mucogingival line. This mode is the CLI port of path C of FlexReg (see FlexReg.md §5.3), and it involves no AREG network at all: the palatal .ckpt isn't even loaded ( parameter_reg[\"model\"] = \"None\" , AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py:92 , and RunMGL returns before PredPatch ). Mucogingival landmarks predicted by ALI_IOS, filtering out doubtful points, then computed construction of the band via spline and geodesic growth. 5.1 The landmarks MGLProcess() ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py:40 ) stacks two ALI_IOS calls (T1 then T"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#icp","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"6. The ICP","x":"Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom 1. the two time points, still apart 2. the Butterfly patch, predicted on the palate 3. rigid ICP, on the patch alone 4. registered mean gap on the patch Register again the two time points, still apart the Butterfly patch, predicted on the palate — rigid ICP, on the patch alone registered — mean gap on the patch T1 in blue, T2 in orange, the patch in green. AREG_IOS does not register on the whole arch: it registers on a patch — here the Butterfly, predicted on the palate, 9529 of 75867 vertices , about an eighth of the mesh. That array is in the output f"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#sorties","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Post-processing and outputs","x":"WriteSurf(surf, output_folder, name, inname) ( AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/utils.py:159 ) writes <name without extension><suffix><extension> . File Content Mode <T1><suffix>.vtk T1 geometrically unchanged, with the Butterfly or Bottom_MGL array both <T2><suffix>.vtk T2 registered both <Lower_T1><suffix>.vtk lower arch T1, copied unchanged Butterfly, if a lower pair exists <Lower_T2><suffix>.vtk lower arch T2, with the upper arch's matrix applied to it Butterfly, if a lower pair exists <pid>_T1_SegOr.tfm copy of <pid>_SegOr.tfm written by PRE_ASO_IOS areg_mode == \"Auto_IOS\" only <pid>_T2_SegOr<suff"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#predit-calcule","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"8. What's predicted vs what's computed","x":"Block Nature Where Crown segmentation network multi-view ( DentalModelSeg , latest ) conda run -n shapeaxi -m CrownSegmentationcli ASO orientation (Butterfly, Auto) geometric + ICP against a gold file slicer.cli.run(pre_aso_ios) Internal canonical orientation geometric , 4 centroids, 2 rotations CLI, inside DatasetPatch 7-view rendering geometric (PyTorch3D) CLI, GPU Palatal patch network 2D MONAI UNet over the 7 views CLI, GPU Reprojection + vote geometric ( pix_to_face , argmax) CLI, GPU Morphological closing geometric , edge graph CLI, CPU MG landmarks network multi-view ( ALI_IOS , Lower_M"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#comparaison","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"9. FlexReg vs AREG_IOS — the table","x":"Same output, same arrays, same ICP. The dividing line is: who decides the patch's shape . Step FlexReg AREG_IOS Granularity one patient, interactive T1/T2 folders, batch Where the heavy computation runs Slicer's Python ( slicer.cli.run ) conda env ( python -m AREG_IOS ) Crown segmentation network, on demand if the array is missing network, a step stacked by AREG Orientation geometric, teeth 3/5/12/14, in the CLI ASO (upstream) then geometric, teeth 3/8/9/14, in the dataset Upper patch built by hand : 4 centroids, Bézier curves mirrored across the chord, GPU geodesic flood fill predicted : 7 vi"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#environnement","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"10. Environment","x":"Element Value Source Conda env shapeaxi , shared with ALI , ASO , FlexReg , DOCShapeAXI AREG/AREG.py:3104 Python 3.12 AREG/AREG.py:3106 Env creation torch>=2.8,<2.13 , ocnn==2.2.1 , SimpleITK AREG/AREG.py:3132 pytorch3d + shapeaxi installed after torch by install_pytorch.py AREG/AREG.py:3139 Slicer side (IOS) tqdm , vtk , pandas , monai==1.3.2 AREG/AREG.py:1448 Windows conda inside WSL, plus libxrender1 , libgl1 / libgl1-mesa-glx , libglx-mesa0 check_lib_wsl , AREG/AREG.py:3161 GPU torch.device(\"cuda\") hardcoded in PredPatch.__init__ AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/PredPatch.py:31 check_deps.ensure_co"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#pieges","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"11. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug isLowerUpper() tests the full path , not the basename ( AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/dataset.py:241 ; called with file as a full path from Sort and SortLower ). A folder containing _U , L_ , Md … misclassifies every arch. And the words are tested case-sensitively here, whereas Method.IsLower on the widget side ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py:141 ) lowercases the basename first: the two don't classify the same way. Confirmed bug The face→vertex vote only writes to F[:,0] , and P_faces[:, pf] += pred doesn't accumulate pixels from the same face. These are facts about the code, not conjectures"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#litterature","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"12. Literature","x":"The published foundation is AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOral Scans (Hutin, Anchling, Cevidanes et al., Lecture Notes in Computer Science , 2023, MICCAI): crown segmentation by DentalModelSeg , initial alignment via shared centroids, palatal ROI segmented by multi-view 3D shape analysis, then ICP. Two discrepancies between the paper and this repository's code: the paper describes an initial alignment by shared crown centroids ; in the code, that role is played by upstream PRE_ASO_IOS plus VTK's StartByMatchingCentroidsOn() — there is no centroid-alignment step inside AREG_IOS itsel"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/SOURCES.html","p":"AREG_IOS — sources","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for AREG_IOS: what has been deposited in this folder, what remains paywalled with no author version, and the methodological neighborhood that does not describe this code. The tool is published , once: AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOralScans (ShapeMI 2023, LNCS 14350). The paper describes the automatic palatal path. This repository's code departs from it on two points flagged on the pipeline page: the initial alignment by crown centroids is moved upstream into PRE_ASO_IOS + StartByMatchingCentroidsOn() , and the MGL path (mucogingival line) appears nowhere in the paper —"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AREG_IOS — sources","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2021_Boubolo_FlyBy_CNN_surface_segmentation.xml / .txt Boubolo L, Dumont M, Brosset S, Bianchi J, Ruellas A, Gurgel ML, Massaro C, Aliaga-Del Castillo A, Ioshida M, Yatabe MS, Benavides E, Rios H, Soki F, Neiva G, Paniagua B, Cevidanes L, Styner M, Prieto JC. « FlyBy CNN: a 3D surface segmentation framework ». Proc. SPIE 11596, Medical Imaging 2021: Image Processing , 115962B, 2021. SPIE proceedings, PMC full text (author manuscript) 10.1117/12.2582205 — PMC7983301 2023_Leclercq_DentalModelSeg_intraoral_surfaces.xml / .txt Leclercq M, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Yatabe M,"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AREG_IOS — sources","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Hutin N, Anchling L, Cevidanes L, Miranda F, Curado D, Gurgel M, Barone S, Bianchi J, Al Turkestani N, Ruellas A, Eason M, Mavani K, Prieto JC, Aliaga A. « AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOralScans ». In: Shape in Medical Imaging — ShapeMI 2023, held jointly with MICCAI 2023, Vancouver, October 8, 2023 , Lecture Notes in Computer Science vol. 14350, Springer, 2023, p. 223–235. link.springer.com — paywalled, no deposited author version 10.1007/978-3-031-46914-5_18 Eason M, Tai SK, Loh CT, Del Castillo AA, Teixeira R, Hutin N, Leroux G, Gurgel ML, Mattos CT, de Olive"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AREG_IOS — sources","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighborhood","x":"Related work that does not describe this code . Gillot M, Miranda F, Baquero B, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Anchling L, Hutin N, Biggs E, Yatabe M, Paniagua B, Fillion-Robin JC, Allemang D, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC. « Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography ». Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research 2023;26(4):560–567. 10.1111/ocr.12642 — ALI on the CBCT side. AREG_IOS does not use ALI_CBCT ; it's the volumetric counterpart of ALI_IOS. Anchling L, Hutin N, Huang Y, Barone S, Roberts S, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Tinawi S, Bianchi J, Yatabe "},{"u":"AREG_IOS/SOURCES.html#recherche-vide","p":"AREG_IOS — sources","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"Searched with nothing found","x":"Crossref, OpenAlex, Europe PMC ( AReg IOS , IntraOralScans , AUTH:Hutin N ), Unpaywall, HAL/DUMAS, arXiv, NA-MIC Project Week 36 through 43 repositories, web search of IADR/AADOCR abstracts. No published work on the MGL path nor on the \"4.2 mm vs 1.2 mm over 364 predictions\" figures cited in the code's comments. No open-access author version of AReg IOS: the only remaining place to check by hand is Deep Blue (deepblue.lib.umich.edu), inaccessible by script."},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Multimodal registration: placing an Intra Oral Scan into the frame of a CBCT from the same patient. The CLI is entirely geometric — it loads no weights at all. Nothing to do with AREG_IOS , which is longitudinal (T1 → T2, same modality): here the two point clouds come from different sensors, and there is no common \"stable patch\". Two successive matching steps, no network inside the CLI : 12 occlusal landmarks predicted on both sides by two different networks ( ALI_CBCT on the volume, ALI_IOS on the mesh) → rigid Procrustes transform ( vtkLandmarkTransform ) that serves as pre-alignment; ICP of"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#situation","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Place in the chain","x":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.py is called only by AREG/AREG_Method/IOSCBCT.py , which defines three modes. It calls nothing itself. Mode Class Steps stacked before AREG_IOSCBCT Registration Reg_IOSCBCT ( IOSCBCT.py:392 ) none : the user supplies IOS, CBCT and both landmark folders Semi-Automated Semi_IOSCBCT ( IOSCBCT.py:157 ) CrownSegmentationcli → ALI_CBCT → ALI_IOS Fully-Automated Auto_IOSCBCT ( IOSCBCT.py:469 ) 7 steps, see §2 So this is indeed an orchestrator with a thin terminal CLI . The real pipeline is the list of processes built by Process() . Full sequence of the Fully-Automated mode I"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#ou-ca-tourne","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Where it runs","x":"Element Value Execution slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.areg_ioscbct, ...) — Slicer's Python , not conda Exception the CrownSegmentationcli step goes through run_conda_tool(\"seg\") ( AREG/AREG.py:1611 ) Dependencies installed in Slicer pyvista==0.47.3 , scipy , numpy , SimpleITK ( AREG/AREG.py:1458 ) GPU not required in the CLI: no torch is imported Parameter passing via AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.xml , 5 positional arguments, this one is up to date IOS_folder CBCT_folder IOS_lm_folder CBCT_lm_folder output This is the structural difference from AREG_IOS : AREG_IOS is a conda module launched by ha"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#appariement","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"3. Matching the files","x":"getPatients() ( AREG_IOSCBCT.py:339 ) scans the four folders and builds a <patient_id>_<timepoint> key. Extraction Regex Note Timepoint [Tt]([0-2]) T0, T1, T2 only . A _T3 matches nothing → patient silently ignored Arch (?:^|_)(?:u|upper)(?=_|\\.|$) and the same for l/lower the letter must be a delimited token. An isolated u in the middle of the name no longer counts — otherwise P001_T1_L_Surface.vtk was read as \"upper\" and registered against the wrong landmarks Patient ([A-Za-z]+)[_]?([0-9]+)[_]?[Tt][0-2] letters + digits stuck together Normalization underscores removed, leading zeros stripped"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#correspondance","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"4. Matching the two modalities","x":"Lower arch Upper arch Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom 1. the CBCT surface, marching cubes at a hard-coded threshold 2. ALI places the same six occlusal points on each modality 3. paired by label, never by position 4. rigid pre-alignment, vtkLandmarkTransform 5. then ICP, inside a 1 mm window not replayed here Register again the CBCT surface, marching cubes at a hard-coded threshold of ALI places the same six occlusal points on each modality paired by label, never by position — rigid pre-alignment, vtkLandmarkTransform then ICP, inside a 1 mm window — not replayed here Two modalities w"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#sorties","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"5. Outputs","x":"save_registered_ios() ( AREG_IOSCBCT.py:272 ) and apply_matrix_and_save_landmarks() ( AREG_IOSCBCT.py:278 ), all within <output> (= <folder_output>/Registered IOS in the Semi and Auto modes): File Content <pid>_Reg_U.vtk upper IOS, landmark transform then ICP applied <pid>_Reg_L.vtk same, lower <pid>_lm_Reg_U.mrk.json the CBCT json , with each position replaced by that of the registered IOS landmark of the same label <pid>_lm_Reg_L.mrk.json same <pid> is the normalized identifier ( P1 , not P_0001 ): the output names therefore do not resemble the input names, hence _matchAcrossNaming() in AREG"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#predit-calcule","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"6. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where CBCT resampling geometric slicer.cli.run(mri2cbct_resample_cbct_mri) CBCT pre-orientation network (PreASO) slicer.cli.run(pre_aso_cbct) CBCT landmarks (×2 passes) network ( ALI_CBCT , RL agents) slicer.cli.run(ali_cbct) CBCT orientation geometric , gold file slicer.cli.run(semi_aso_cbct) IOS crown segmentation network ( DentalModelSeg ) conda run -n shapeaxi IOS orientation geometric , 4 centroids + gold ICP slicer.cli.run(pre_aso_ios) IOS landmarks network ( ALI_IOS multi-view) slicer.cli.run(ali_ios) CBCT isosurface geometric , marching cubes at 400 CLI Pre-alignment geome"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#pieges","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"The 400 threshold is hard-coded And is never exposed. On a CBCT not calibrated in HU, or with a different reconstruction, the isosurface captures something else and the ICP registers against noise. This is the parameter most likely to need changing from one center to another. run_icp_point_to_plane is point-to-point. The normals are computed and discarded. Either the name is wrong or the implementation is incomplete — either way, a maintainer counting on point-to-plane (better convergence on smooth surfaces, less tangential slippage) will be disappointed. Two modalities, one single CBCT per pa"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#litterature","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Literature","x":"Unpublished No paper covers AREG_IOSCBCT as implemented here. The lineage published by the group covers CBCT orientation and registration ( Automated Orientation and Registration of CBCT Scans, LNCS 2023 ) and longitudinal IOS registration (AReg IOS, LNCS 2023); the landmark modules it consumes each have their own ( ALI_CBCT ). The multimodal IOS↔CBCT part appears to be an internal, unpublished assembly. The methodological neighborhood in the literature — for comparison, not as a description of this code: Novel Procedure for Automatic Registration between CBCT and Intraoral Scan Data Supported"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/SOURCES.html","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — sources","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"No publication describes this module. What is published are the upstream building blocks that the orchestrator stacks in front of the CLI. Verification The sheet said so, and the verification confirms it: neither Crossref, nor OpenAlex, nor Europe PMC, nor arXiv, nor HAL, nor NA-MIC project weeks 36 through 43 contain anything about an IOS↔CBCT registration by occlusal landmarks + ICP on an isosurface signed by this group. The two NA-MIC projects titled \"Multimodal Registration\" (PW40 2024, PW41 2024) deal with MRI↔CBCT , not IOS↔CBCT. What is published are the upstream building blocks that th"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — sources","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID .xml / .txt Gillot M, Miranda F, Baquero B, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Anchling L, Hutin N, Biggs E, Yatabe M, Paniagua B, Fillion-Robin JC, Allemang D, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC. \"Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography\". Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research 2023;26(4):560–567. article, open access (CC BY-NC-ND) 10.1111/ocr.12642 — PMC10440369 .xml / .txt Boubolo L, Dumont M, Brosset S, Bianchi J, Ruellas A, Gurgel ML, Massaro C, Aliaga-Del Castillo A, Ioshida M, Yatabe MS, Benavides E, Rios H, Soki F, Neiva G, Paniagu"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — sources","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Zheng Q, Wu Y, Chen J, Wang X, Zhou M, Li H, Lin J, Zhang W, Chen X. \"Automatic multimodal registration of cone-beam computed tomography and intraoral scans: a systematic review and meta-analysis\". Clinical Oral Investigations 2025;29(2):97. — systematic review of the problem; places geometric approaches against learned approaches. Does not mention this code. link.springer.com — paywalled 10.1007/s00784-025-06183-x Elgarba BM, Fontenele RC, Ali S, Swaity A, Meeus J, Shujaat S, Jacobs R. \"Validation of a novel AI-based automated multimodal image registration of CBCT and intr"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — sources","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighborhood","x":"Work that addresses the same problem but does not describe this code . Kim 2023 ( 2023_Kim_registration_CBCT_intraoral_scan.pdf ): same objective, but with tooth segmentation on both sides , then RANSAC + ICP. Reported error 0.234 ± 0.019 mm. AREG_IOSCBCT takes the lighter path: landmarks instead of volumetric segmentation, thresholded isosurface instead of segmented teeth. Jang 2024 ( 2024_Jang_CBCT_intraoral_impressions_integration.pdf ): tooth-by-tooth identification and correction of the impression's stitching error. Fully learned pipeline, unrelated to the implementation here. Zheng 2025 "},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/SOURCES.html#rien-trouve","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — sources","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Searched with nothing found","x":"Crossref, OpenAlex, Europe PMC, arXiv, HAL, Unpaywall on every combination of AREG / IOSCBCT / IOS-CBCT / multimodal registration + Cevidanes, Prieto, Anchling, Hutin, Leroux, Baquero; listing of NA-MIC Project Week 36 → 43 projects (the two \"Multimodal Registration\" ones are MRI↔CBCT); targeted web search. Nothing on the multimodal IOS↔CBCT part of this repository."},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"Looks for a rigid rotation that brings a CBCT or an intraoral scan into the frame of a reference case (the \"gold\"), through a 3-point initialization followed by ICP — never an optimal least-squares registration."},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#situation","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"Nothing in the repository calls ASO. ASO, however, calls three tools, and AREG_IOS consumes its output directly : AREG_IOS.py:324 looks for <patient>_SegOr.tfm in the T1 and T2 folders — exactly the name written by PRE_ASO_IOS.py:422 — and composes inv(areg_matrix @ inv(matrix_aso)) to produce a single matrix from raw scan to registered. Called When How slicer.modules.pre_aso_cbct CBCT, Fully-Automated mode only slicer.cli.run slicer.modules.ali_cbct CBCT, Fully-Automated mode only slicer.cli.run slicer.modules.semi_aso_cbct CBCT, both modes slicer.cli.run CrownSegmentationcli (ShapeAXI / Dent"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#fichiers","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs ASO/ASO.py widget, 2590 lines: UI, downloads, chaining the CLIs, conda Slicer's Python ASO_Method/CBCT.py Semi_CBCT / Auto_CBCT : landmark lists, CLI parameters same ASO_Method/IOS.py Semi_IOS / Auto_IOS : segmentation bypass, parameters same PRE_ASO_CBCT.py recentering the volume CLI, Slicer's Python SEMI_ASO_CBCT.py the actual CBCT orientation same ASO_CBCT_utils/utils.py 1045 lines: json merging, landmark sorting, InitICP, ICP, resampling same PRE_ASO_IOS.py IOS orientation by tooth centroids same SEMI_ASO_IOS.py IOS orientation by supplied landmarks same ASO_IOS_uti"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#principe","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"3. The shared geometric principle","x":"The target is not an analytical frame (\" occlusal plane = z=0\") but a reference patient case , downloaded from a release: scan + fiducials for the CBCT, two meshes + two jsons for the IOS. The choice of frame is thus offloaded into the data: Occlusal and Midsagittal Plane or Frankfurt Horizontal and Midsagittal Plane ( CBCT.py:68 ) are two different golds, the same code producing two different orientations. In both backends the estimation follows the same two-stage scheme: A 3-point initialization — a translation followed by two rotations around hand-built axes, never a least-squares Procruste"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#aso-cbct","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"4. ASO_CBCT","x":"4.1 The two modes Semi-Automated Fully-Automated Input scans + fiducials .mrk.json scans alone (or DICOM) Steps SEMI_ASO_CBCT PRE_ASO_CBCT → ALI_CBCT → SEMI_ASO_CBCT Working folder the user's folder two slicer.util.tempDirectory() The automatic mode is therefore just the semi mode preceded by generating the missing landmarks ( CBCT.py:489 ). ASO_CBCT: the Fully-Automated mode only adds the recentering and landmark prediction by ALI_CBCT ( network ) ahead of an identical orientation ( geometric ). 4.2 PRE_ASO_CBCT — recentering, no more network computed Despite its name and its six arguments, t"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#aso-ios","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"5. ASO_IOS","x":"5.1 Automatic mode — crown centroids a) Segmentation bypass. __BypassCrownseg__ ( IOS.py:196 ) opens each mesh and looks for a PredictedID , UniversalID or Universal_ID array. Already-segmented meshes are copied directly into the segmentation's output folder with the suffix _Seg ; the others go to the input folder to be segmented, with exotic formats converted to .vtk along the way. b) DentalModelSeg predicted , via conda ( IOS.py:275 ): conda run -n shapeaxi python -m CrownSegmentationcli \\ None <csv> <out> 0 latest 0 Universal_ID 0 Seg <vtk_folder> <dentalmodelseg_path> Argument order: surf "},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#flexreg","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"6. Comparison with FlexReg's orientation","x":"FlexReg_Method/orientation.py and ASO_IOS_utils/pre_icp.py are the same algorithm, copy-pasted . make_vector is identical character for character; the bodies of orientation() and PrePreAso() differ only on three points: ASO_IOS PrePreAso FlexReg orientation Target the gold mesh's centroids , read with the same vtkMeanTeeth three hard-coded points, [[-0.5,-0.5,0], [0,0,0], [0.5,-0.5,0]] Teeth 3 or 4 chosen by the operator, sorted by organizeLandmark 3, 5, 12, 14 hard-coded (UR6, UR4, UL4, UL6) Return (mesh, matrix) mesh alone transformation.py is likewise duplicated between the two (same Rotati"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#predit-calcule","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"7. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where CBCT recentering geometric (translation) PRE_ASO_CBCT CBCT landmarks (auto mode) network , multi-scale search agents ALI_CBCT Pruning outlier landmarks geometric , distances + angles SEMI_ASO_CBCT Optimal triplet + rotations geometric , random search ≤ 2500 draws SEMI_ASO_CBCT / PRE_ASO_IOS Refinement geometric , rigid VTK ICP same IOS crown segmentation network , multi-view ( DentalModelSeg ) conda run -n shapeaxi Tooth centroids geometric , vertex averaging PRE_ASO_IOS IOS orientation geometric , 2 rotations + translation PrePreAso The two networks that are called are pre-"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#environnement","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"8. Environment","x":"Two distinct regimes, and this is a source of confusion. The CLIs run in Slicer's Python. install_function ( ASO.py:69 ) pip_install s whatever is missing there: itk , torch==2.2.0 , pytorch_lightning , dicom2nifti==2.6.2 , pydicom==3.0.2 and monai at 1.3.2 (Python ≥ 3.10) or 0.7.0 otherwise. Pitfall torch , pytorch_lightning and monai are only used by Net.py and by ALI_CBCT : in semi-CBCT mode and in IOS, ASO installs them for nothing, with the risk of overwriting another module's version of torch (the dialog says so itself: \"Doing it could break other modules\"). The conda environment is only"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#pieges","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug The semi-CBCT mode is broken as it stands. SEMI_ASO_CBCT.py:113 requires data[\"tfm\"] , introduced by commit 1bfb51f at the same time as the matrix output. This .tfm only exists because PRE_ASO_CBCT writes it — but the semi mode does not run PRE_ASO_CBCT and works on the user's raw folder. Result: a KeyError for any patient whose folder does not already contain a .tfm , caught and logged as \"missing required files,\" with the patient counted as a failure without interrupting the others. The mode therefore only works if a .tfm happens to sit next to the scan — typically after a prio"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#litterature","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"10. Literature","x":"The reference paper for ASO_CBCT is Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans (Anchling, Hutin, Cevidanes et al., Lecture Notes in Computer Science, 2023). Its description matches the code: ALI_CBCT identifies the landmarks regardless of the input orientation, then the orientation is obtained \"by first aligning 3 randomly chosen landmarks, then refining with ICP.\" Reported errors: < 3° and < 2 mm against experts. The paper does not mention the DenseNet pre-orienter, consistent with the fact that it is no longer connected. For ALI_CBCT itself: Automatic landm"},{"u":"ASO/SOURCES.html","p":"ASO — sources","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"ASO's bibliography: what has been deposited in this folder, what is still to be read online, and the neighborhood — the works that underpin the method without describing the code. ASO_CBCT is published , in a MICCAI workshop proceedings chapter (LNCS 14242, 2023) that it shares with AREG_CBCT : the same text describes the orientation (ASO) and the registration (AREG). The chapter is paywalled at Springer, but the author manuscript is freely readable on PMC (PMC11104011); PMC blocks automated PDF downloads, so it could not be deposited here (see \"To read online\" ). ASO_IOS has no paper of its o"},{"u":"ASO/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"ASO — sources","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2023_Gillot_ALI_CBCT_landmark_identification.xml / .txt Gillot M., Miranda F., Baquero B., Ruellas A., Gurgel M., Al Turkestani N., Anchling L., Hutin N., Biggs E., Yatabe M., Paniagua B., Fillion-Robin J.-C., Allemang D., Bianchi J., Cevidanes L., Prieto J.C. Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography. Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research, 26(4):560-567, 2023. Journal article, full JATS text (Europe PMC) + plain text — CC-BY-NC-ND 10.1111/ocr.12642 — PMID 36811276, PMCID PMC10440369 2016_Ruellas_common_coordinate_system.pdf Ruellas A.C.O., "},{"u":"ASO/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"ASO — sources","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Anchling L., Hutin N., Huang Y., Barone S., Roberts S., Miranda F., Gurgel M., Al Turkestani N., Tinawi S., Bianchi J., Yatabe M., Ruellas A., Prieto J.C., Cevidanes L. Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans. Lecture Notes in Computer Science 14242, Springer, Cham, 2023, p. 43-58. Joint proceedings of CLIP 2023 (12th International Workshop on Clinical Image-Based Procedures), FAIMI 2023 and EPIMI 2023, held with MICCAI 2023, Vancouver, October 8-12, 2023. Eds. Wesarg, Puyol Antón, Baxter et al. ISBN 978-3-031-45248-2 / 978-3-031-45249-"},{"u":"ASO/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"ASO — sources","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"Neighborhood","x":"These works do not describe ASO ; they underpin the method or show it in use. Ruellas 2016, Common 3-dimensional coordinate system . This is the article that defines the common coordinate system toward which ASO orients: it first shows that head orientation changes the displacement values measured in 3D, then proposes building a common frame from landmarks. ASO's orientation \"gold standard,\" and the reference landmarks shipped with the module, come from this convention. It speaks of neither ASO nor deep learning: everything in it is manual. Gillot 2023, ALI_CBCT . The reinforcement-learning la"},{"u":"ASO/SOURCES.html#methode","p":"ASO — sources","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"How the full text was retrieved","x":"The chapter is paywalled at Springer, but the author manuscript deposited with the NIH is open access. Two recipes fail on it and one works — noted here to avoid retracing the steps: Route Result on this PMCID pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/.../pdf/ anti-bot HTML BioC API pmcoa.cgi \"No result can be found\" — it only serves the open access subset eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=pmc&id=<number> full JATS, body included curl -sL \"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=pmc&id=11104011\" Verified This is how the .xml and .txt files in this folder were obtained. The p"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Project report — 45th NA-MIC Project Week, Boston, 2026. Category: Infrastructure. A statement of intent, not of state This report announces llama3:latest where the code hardcodes qwen3:8b , and its \"next steps\" (LLM integration, deployment inside Slicer) are already achieved in the repository. Read it as a dated statement of intent, not as a description of the module as it stands. See the pipeline page for the actual state. Investigators Name Affiliation Country Paul Dumont University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Alexandre Buisson University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Juan C"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#github","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Github Repository","x":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#financement","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Funding Source(s)","x":"R01DE024450"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#description","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Project Description","x":"Problem : The Slicer Automated Dental Tools extension provides robust craniofacial analysis, but complex module selection and parameter tuning create a steep learning curve for users. Solution : This project introduces an AI Agent and chatbot UI integrated into 3D Slicer to streamline the workflow. By allowing drag-and-drop inputs and natural language prompts, users can easily request complex tasks (e.g., segmentation, landmarking, or orientation on CBCT/IOS). The agent autonomously translates these requests into actions, selecting the right tools and automatically configuring the parameters t"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#objectif","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Objective","x":"Local LLM Integration: Uses Ollama to run a lightweight local model (currently llama3:latest ). Cross-Encoder Retrieval: the system leverages a Cross-Encoder. It directly scores the semantic relevance between the user's query and tool use cases to retrieve the top 3 most appropriate modules. Autonomous Execution: The LLM analyzes the retrieved context, selects the optimal tool, extracts the required parameters from the user's text, and automatically triggers the execution within Slicer."},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#approche","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Approach and Plan","x":"Build the core backend pipeline integrating the Cross-Encoder retrieval model and the local LLM. Deploy the pipeline within 3D Slicer and connect it to the user-facing chatbot UI. Implement an autonomous feedback loop. The model will verify if the selected Slicer tools executed successfully or encountered an error, and provide real-time feedback to the user."},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#progres","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Progress and Next Steps","x":"Current Progress The backend retrieval system is completely operational. The Cross-Encoder model reliably identifies and selects the appropriate Slicer tool from natural language input. Next Steps LLM Integration: Implement the logic for the local LLM to parse the user's prompt, auto-fill the required parameters, and trigger the tool execution. Slicer Deployment: Embed the interactive UI and connect the entire AI pipeline directly within the 3D Slicer environment."},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#illustrations","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Illustrations","x":"Media hosted offsite The screenshots and video are hosted on github.com/user-attachments . They are given as links rather than embedded, so this site stays browsable offline. What would the Slicer user interface look like? — image (504 × 934) Slicer Automated Dental Tools Overview — image (width 800) Tool demo — video Results comparing local LLMs and Cloud-based Models — screenshot from June 26, 2026 (1629 × 82)"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#references","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Background and References","x":"Github link: https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools This work was supported by the National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research (NIDCR) of the National Institutes of Health under Award Number R01DE024450 ."},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"A natural-language assistant that picks a module from the extension, extracts its parameters, builds the command line, and launches it. No medical neural network is called here — the Agent only does routing and form-filling. Everything runs locally: an 8B LLM served by Ollama, plus a small cross-encoder to preselect candidate tools. Three calls to the same model — routing, extraction, repair. The choice of binary, though, comes from the manifest."},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#situation","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"Nobody calls Agent. A grep for slicer.modules.agent turns up no caller outside the module itself. It is a terminal entry point. Outbound, there are two levels: Called How Where Agent_CLI slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.agent_cli, ...) ( Agent/Agent.py:798 ) separate CLI process, Slicer Python the target module (ALI_CBCT.py, AMASSS_CLI.py, …) subprocess.run(cli_args) ( Agent/Agent.py:962 ) child process of the widget, Slicer Python Ollama python client ollama , HTTP on 127.0.0.1:11434 local server, launched/installed by the module The inventory of targets is purely declarative: it lives in Agent_"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#fichiers","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs Agent/Agent.py widget: chat, drop zone, Ollama installation, confirmation, actual execution, repair loop Slicer Python Agent_CLI/Agent_CLI.py routing + extraction; prints JSON to stdout , executes nothing CLI process, Slicer Python Agent_CLI/Agent_CLI_utils/utils.py manifest, cross-encoder, Ollama call, path resolution, command building same parameter_extraction_improved.py building the extraction prompt, nothing else same parameter_validator.py type coercion, required / enum / range / extension checks same Agent_CLI/manifest.yaml the 23 addressable tools, their paramet"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#modeles","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"3. The models","x":"3.1 The LLM — qwen3:8b via Ollama A single constant, duplicated at both ends: DEFAULT_MODEL = \"qwen3:8b\" ( utils.py:21 ) and MODEL_NAME = \"qwen3:8b\" ( Agent.py:18 ). get_router_model() honors a ROUTER_MODEL environment variable that lets you swap in any Ollama model without touching the code. The same model serves all three calls : routing, parameter extraction, repair. No specialized model, no fine-tuning. Point Value Source Inference engine Ollama (so llama.cpp underneath), local HTTP API import ollama , port 11434 Weight format GGUF — imposed by Ollama, no transformers checkpoint inferred f"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#routage","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"4. Routing","x":"Two stages, and the LLM only steps in at the second one. Stage 1 — cross-encoder preselection. cross_encoder_retrieve_candidates(manifest, prompt, k=3) builds one document per tool: docs.append(f\"{s['name']}: {s['description']} (tags: {tags})\") scores = model.predict([(user_text, doc) for doc in docs]) ranked = sorted(zip(scores, scripts), reverse=True) return [s for _, s in ranked[:k]] 23 pairs scored, sorted in descending order, hardcoded top-3 ( k=3 is never parameterized). The manifest's priority field (values 1 to 23) is never read — the line that would read it is commented out ( utils.py"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#extraction","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"5. Parameter extraction","x":"A second LLM call, with a tool already chosen, inside extract_parameters ( utils.py:220 ). Masking technical parameters. Parameters named temp_fold , tmp_folder , temp_folder , log_path or logPath are removed from the spec before building the prompt — no point asking the model to invent a temporary folder. They are reinjected afterward with the value input.temp_folder , i.e. the slicer.util.tempDirectory() created by the widget on every click ( Agent_CLI.py:153 ), and removed from missing_required . The extraction prompt ( parameter_extraction_improved.py:40 ) lists each parameter as - name (t"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#execution","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"6. Building and executing the command","x":"Yes, the module is actually launched. Unambiguously. The CLI only builds the command. build_cli_args ( utils.py:350 ) merges the manifest's default values with the extracted parameters, then: return [sys.executable, tool_path] + cli sys.executable is the Agent_CLI process's interpreter, i.e. Slicer's Python. For cli_style: positional (21 tools out of 23), the values are concatenated in the manifest's declaration order — positional_order is supported but no entry sets it . For any other cli_style , execution falls into the --flag value branch, iterating over merged : hallucinated out-of-spec pa"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#reparation","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"7. The repair loop","x":"On a nonzero return code, runToolWithRepair ( Agent.py:943 ) asks the LLM to fix the parameters, at most MAX_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS = 2 times. build_repair_prompt ( utils.py:282 ) sends the parameter definitions, the values used, the full command, and the last 2000 characters of stderr , and asks it to return only the parameters to change. This path runs inside the Slicer process , not the CLI: _proposeRepair adds the Agent_CLI folder to sys.path and reimports Agent_CLI_utils so as not to duplicate the logic. The fix goes back through ParameterValidator then build_cli_args , an old -> new diff is dis"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#modules","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"8. Addressable modules — and their real state","x":"The 23 entries of Agent_CLI/manifest.yaml . The right-hand column compares the manifest's positional order against the target script's actual argparse signature, checked file by file. Tool (manifest) Target script Manifest / actual params Verdict ali_cbct ALI_CBCT/ALI_CBCT.py 6 / 10 missing spacing , speed_per_scale , agent_fov , spawn_radius ali_ios ALI_IOS/ALI_IOS.py 6 / 10 shift starting at position 5 ( teeth_mg missing) amasss_cli AMASSS_CLI/AMASSS_CLI.py 5 / 12 the script refuses: \"Expected 13 arguments\" areg_cbct AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.py 7 / 10 DCMInput lands in add_name areg_ios AREG_IOS/"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#modes","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"9. The two modes","x":"Mode ( comboBox ) Agent_CLI output Execution Agent (Automated) JSON {tool, tool_confidence, parameters_confidence, parameters, missing_required, command} yes, after confirmation Ask (Interactive) free text none In Ask mode, build_candidates_block(..., include_parameters=True) appends | parameters:<raw YAML list of dicts> to each line — the Python repr of the manifest's structure gets injected as-is into the system prompt of an \"expert medical image analysis consultant.\" No format=\"json\" ; the response is displayed after stripping the * and # characters. Three names for two modes The README cal"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#garde-fous","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"10. Guardrails","x":"What exists: A mandatory modal confirmation before every execution, listing all the parameters, and a second one before every repair attempt. Blocking on missing required parameters : nothing is launched while missing_required is not empty. Type coercion and choices / min / max / extension-on-folder checks. Masking of temporary folders , filled in from slicer.util.tempDirectory() rather than by the model. Bounding : MAX_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS = 2 , history truncated to MAX_HISTORY_ENTRIES = 40 turns. Global try in main() : stdout always stays parsable JSON, the traceback goes to stderr. What does not"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#environnement","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"11. Environment","x":"CheckDependencies ( Agent.py:1141 ) runs a slicer.util.pip_install inside Slicer's Python, in this order, which matters : Package Reason for the pin ollama python client only — pip does not provide the server pyyaml reading the manifest transformers<4.53.0 regression NameError: name 'nn' is not defined in integrations/accelerate.py , breaks the cross-encoder import numpy<2 numpy ≥ 2 breaks the ABI of the pip torch wheels ( RuntimeError: Numpy is not available ) sentence-transformers pulls in torch; installed last so as not to bump the pins Agent_CLI.py runs in a separate process but shares Sli"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#pieges","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"12. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"resolve_tool_path finds nothing in this repository (§8). This is blocker number one. Possible fixes: create a CLI files folder with links, set AGENT_CLI_TOOLS_DIR , or put repository-relative paths in the manifest and extend the search. 17 signatures out of 23 are wrong , and the shift is silent. batchdentalseg and clic are not CLIs. cli_style: ui_module is handled nowhere: it falls into the --flag branch, which produces a command that cannot work on a scripted Slicer module. input.folders.split(\",\") breaks any path containing a comma, both for the folder list and for the join(\",\") on the widg"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#predit-calcule","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"13. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Preselection of the 3 candidates network (cross-encoder MiniLM-L6, 22.7 M) Agent_CLI process, CPU or GPU depending on torch Final choice of tool LLM qwen3:8b, constrained JSON Ollama server Parameter extraction LLM qwen3:8b, constrained JSON Ollama server Path resolution LLM , from FOLDERS_CONTEXT Ollama server Validation / coercion deterministic , pure Python Agent_CLI Temporary folders deterministic , slicer.util.tempDirectory() widget Command building deterministic , manifest order Agent_CLI Repair after failure LLM qwen3:8b, from stderr Slicer process Environment error d"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#litterature","p":"Agent — full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"14. Literature","x":"No paper Neither on this module nor on the approach. A search for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools + LLM agent turns up only the repository's documentation and general literature on LLMs in dentistry, unrelated to this code. The only real external references are those of the two models used, both generic and not trained for this domain. Qwen3 on Ollama — routing and extraction model cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 — MS MARCO reranker used for preselection SlicerAutomatedDentalTools The module's git history fits in three commits ( aa3330d ADD: Add the new agentic module, b463312 , 2a91e33 ): it "},{"u":"Agent/SOURCES.html","p":"Agent — sources","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Unpublished. No paper covers this module or its approach. There is, however, an official record that had to be found: a NA-MIC project report. The report describes the intent, not the state A NA-MIC Project Week report (PW45, Boston, 2026) signed by its authors was retrieved here. It describes the module in its intent, not in its actual state — it announces llama3:latest where the code hardcodes qwen3:8b , and its \"next step\" (LLM integration, deployment inside Slicer) is already done in the repository. The other useful references are purely technical: the model served by Ollama , the preselec"},{"u":"Agent/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"Agent — sources","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID PW45 report Dumont P., Buisson A., Prieto J.C., Cevidanes L., Pieper S. AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools. 45th NA-MIC Project Week, Boston, 2026. Funded by NIDCR R01DE024450. NA-MIC project report projectweek.na-mic.org/PW45_2026_Boston .pdf Qwen Team. Qwen3 Technical Report. arXiv:2505.09388, 14 May 2025. The routing and extraction model ( qwen3:8b , default Ollama tag: 5.2 GB, Q4_K_M, 8.19 G parameters). Technical report / arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.09388 .pdf Wang W., Wei F., Dong L., Bao H., Yang N., Zhou M. MiniLM: Deep Self-Attention Distillation for Ta"},{"u":"Agent/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"Agent — sources","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Nothing. No paywalled work was identified for this module. Model cards, worth reading alongside this page: ollama.com/library/qwen3 (tags, sizes, quantizations) and huggingface.co/cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 ."},{"u":"Agent/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"Agent — sources","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Neighborhood","x":"None of the following works describe this module . MS MARCO (Bajaj, retrieved): the reranker was trained to rank web passages for Bing queries. Here it is fed Slicer module cards and operator prompts — a domain and a register it was not trained for, and for which no evaluation exists in the repository. Qwen3 (retrieved): the generic model's technical report. Neither the module's system prompt, nor its constrained JSON output format, nor its repair loop appear in it — and no temperature , seed , num_ctx , or think is passed by the code. Ollama and llama.cpp , the server and inference engine act"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"Batch-crops a folder of volumes to a ROI box ( .mrk.json ) by extracting indices in the original grid — no interpolation, no network, no conda."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#situation","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"1. Position in the pipeline","x":"Nothing in the repository calls AutoCrop3D, and AutoCrop3D calls nothing but Slicer. It is declared in CMakeLists.txt:39 and that's it. In practice it sits upstream of the segmenters ( AMASSS , BATCHDENTALSEG) or downstream of them: the \"generate VTK\" mode only triggers if the output file name contains seg , which presupposes it's being handed labelmaps already produced elsewhere. AutoCrop3D is called by no other module in the repository and calls nothing but Slicer; its place upstream or downstream of the segmenters is a matter of usage, not code. No step is predicted by a network — both path"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#fichiers","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs AutoCrop3D.py widget, input validation, Crop Volume path Slicer's Python AutoCrop3D_CLI.py the main path, 175 lines, all the cropping Slicer's Python ( SlicerMacroBuildScriptedCLI ) FilesType.py Search (extension glob) and ChangeKeyDict (patient→ROI pairing) same GenerateVTKfromSeg.py marching cubes on the cropped labelmap same CropCBCT.py dead code , says so itself: !!! UNUSED !!! — The CLI is a scripted CLI ( AutoCrop3D/Crop_Volumes_CLI/CMakeLists.txt ), so it's launched by slicer.cli.run inside Slicer's Python. No conda environment, unlike AMASSS or ALI."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#boite-crop","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"3. What defines the crop box","x":"A markups ROI json file ( .mrk.json ), read as follows ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:70 ): ROI = json.load(open(ROI_Path))['markups'][0] ROI_Center = np.array(ROI['center']) ROI_Size = np.array(ROI['size']) Lower = ROI_Center - ROI_Size / 2 Upper = ROI_Center + ROI_Size / 2 Three fields of the json are ignored , and that's the main source of surprises: coordinateSystem . The test file ROI.mrk.zip (in AutoCrop3D/Crop_Volumes_UI/Testing/Test_data/ ) declares \"coordinateSystem\": \"LPS\" , which is the repository's convention (every markups writer hardcodes \"LPS\" : ALI_CBCT , ASO_CBCT, AREG_CBCT , FlexReg , V"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#application","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"4. How the box is applied — index extraction, no resampling","x":"This is the key point: no interpolation . The physical → index conversion followed by the crop ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:77 ): Lower = np.array(img.TransformPhysicalPointToContinuousIndex(Lower)).astype(int) Upper = np.array(img.TransformPhysicalPointToContinuousIndex(Upper)).astype(int) for i in range(3): if Lower[i] > Upper[i]: Lower[i], Upper[i] = Upper[i], Lower[i] Lower = [max(0, l) for l in Lower] Upper = [min(img_size[i], u) for i, u in enumerate(Upper)] img_roi = img[Lower[0]:Upper[0], Lower[1]:Upper[1], Lower[2]:Upper[2]] The swap is not cosmetic. TransformPhysicalPointToContinuousIndex app"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#crop-volume","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"5. The alternative path — Slicer's Crop Volume module","x":"processCropVolume() ( AutoCrop3D.py:915 ), activated by the \"Use module 'Crop Volume' for tilted images\" checkbox (commit 9d2bbe8 ). It runs inside the widget , not in the CLI: cropVolumeLogic = slicer.modules.cropvolume.logic() parameters = slicer.vtkMRMLCropVolumeParametersNode() parameters.SetInputVolumeNodeID(inputVolume.GetID()) parameters.SetROINodeID(roiNode.GetID()) parameters.SetOutputVolumeNodeID(outputVolume.GetID()) cropVolumeLogic.Apply(parameters) Only the three IDs are set: interpolation, isotropy, and spacing scale stay at the vtkMRMLCropVolumeParametersNode 's default values. "},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#batch","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"6. Batch mode and patient ↔ ROI pairing","x":"Two regimes, discriminated by len(ROIList['.mrk.json']) > 1 ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:53 ): a single ROI → applied to every volume found; several ROIs → ChangeKeyDict builds {basename.split('_')[0]: path} and each volume looks up its key; on failure → logger.warning('No ROI for patient:') and continue . Both sides compute the key differently : Side Rule Example MA_0001_T1_Scan.nii.gz / MA_0001_ROI.mrk.json volume chain of .split() on _Scan _scan _Seg _seg _Or _OR _MAND _MD _MAX _MX _CB _lm _T2 _T1 _Cl then .split('.')[0] MA_0001 ROI basename.split('_')[0] MA A patient name containing an underscore t"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#vtk","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"7. Generating a VTK mesh from a segmentation","x":"Triggered by if \"seg\" in ScanOutPath.lower() ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:151 ) — the test is against the full output path , so a parent folder named Segmentations/ is enough to trigger it on every file. The failure is swallowed by a bare except: pass . convertNiftiToVTK ( GenerateVTKfromSeg.py:16 ): padding() : sitk.ConstantPad(img, [50,50,10], [0,0,0]) . The actual signature verified: ConstantPad(image, padLowerBound, padUpperBound, constant) — the third argument being zero means that only the lower bound gets padded . Confirmed bug The comment \"add 50 pixels around each dimension\" is wrong: a struct"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#sorties","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"8. Outputs","x":"Path Name produced Construction CLI <stem>_<suffix><ext> basename.split('.')[0] + \"_\" + suffix + key , where key is the matched search extension ( .nii.gz , …) CLI, segmentation <stem>_<suffix>_vtk.vtk same Crop Volume <basename with .nii.gz → _<suffix>.nii.gz> basename.replace('.nii.gz', f'_{suffix}.nii.gz') — hardcoded to .nii.gz Default suffix: cropped ( AutoCrop3D.ui:308 ). The input folder tree is reproduced in the output folder via os.path.relpath . No transform file is produced."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#predit-calcule","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"9. Predicted vs. computed","x":"Block Nature Where Reading the ROI computed json, no computation CLI Physical → index computed TransformPhysicalPointToContinuousIndex (ITK) CLI Cropping computed SimpleITK slicing, no interpolation CLI \"Tilted\" cropping computed resampling via cropVolumeLogic.Apply widget VTK surface computed marching cubes + laplacian smoothing CLI No network. No part of this module learns or predicts anything."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#environnement","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"10. Environment","x":"Nothing to install. The module uses only what Slicer already ships with: SimpleITK , numpy , vtk , qt . pip_install is imported in AutoCrop3D.py:11 but never called. No CUDA, no WSL, no conda."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#pieges","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"11. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Pitfall — ROI folder with a single file A ROI folder containing exactly one file → crash. ROI_dict is only built if len(ROIList) > 1 ; otherwise ROI_Path stays the folder , and json.load(open(ROI_Path)) raises IsADirectoryError . The Crop Volume path, by contrast, tests os.path.isdir and handles it fine. Pitfall — inconsistent extensions between widget and CLI The widget and the CLI don't look for the same extensions. CheckInput ( AutoCrop3D.py:808 ) only looks for .nii.gz , .nrrd.gz , .gipl.gz . A folder of uncompressed .nii or .nrrd files is rejected by the UI (\"Wrong type of patient file de"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#litterature","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"12. Literature","x":"No paper covers AutoCrop3D. It's a batch-processing utility written for the lab (Jeanne Claret, UoM/DCBIA), documented only by the README.md:336 section and by the help of Slicer's Crop Volume module, whose semantics it reuses: Crop Volume — Slicer documentation Crop Volume Sequence — Slicer documentation (cited by the README as the limitation AutoCrop3D works around: files too heavy to load as a sequence) SlicerAutomatedDentalTools The CLI's documentation-url points to https://github.com/Jeanneclre/DCBIA-code , a personal repository that is not the project's own. Full bibliography: SOURCES.ht"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/SOURCES.html","p":"AutoCrop3D — sources","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"AutoCrop3D bibliography: nothing describes it, two articles cite it in a single sentence. Unpublished. No article, abstract, poster, or Project Week report describes AutoCrop3D. Two articles cite it in passing as a tool they used, one sentence each, without describing either how it works or its parameters; they are retrieved here because they are the only published trace of the module's existence. The documentation remains that of the repository and that of Slicer's Crop Volume module, whose semantics AutoCrop3D reuses."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AutoCrop3D — sources","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2025_Barone_condylar_remodelling_class_III.xml / .txt Barone S., Cevidanes L., Bianchi J., Gonçalves J.R., Giudice A. Deep Learning-Based Three-Dimensional Analysis Reveals Distinct Patterns of Condylar Remodelling After Orthognathic Surgery in Skeletal Class III Patients. Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research 28(3):441-448, 2025. Journal article, full JATS text (Europe PMC, CC BY-NC-ND) 10.1111/ocr.12895 — PMCID PMC12056474 2025_Caleme_MR2CBCT_TMJ_case_series.xml / .txt Caleme E.D., Cevidanes L., Mattos C., Miranda F., Gurgel M., Barone S., Gaydamour A., Tulissi E."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AutoCrop3D — sources","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Nothing. No paywalled work was identified on this module."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AutoCrop3D — sources","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Neighboring resources","x":"These resources do not describe AutoCrop3D ; they document what it reuses the semantics of, or what it works around. Crop Volume — Slicer documentation : the reference for the expected behavior (ROI, spacing scale , interpolation, isotropic ). Crop Volume Sequence — Slicer documentation : cited by the repository's README as the limitation AutoCrop3D works around (files too heavy to load as a sequence). DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools : the module's repository. The CLI's documentation-url , meanwhile, points to Jeanneclre/DCBIA-code , the author's personal repository, which is not the"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"Applies one or more ITK transformations, already computed elsewhere, in batch to a folder of volumes and landmark files — no network, no conda, no GPU. Batch application of one or more ITK transformations to a folder of volumes and landmark files. No network, no conda, no GPU: sitk.ReadTransform + sitk.ResampleImageFilter , and a file ↔ matrix match by name splitting. The whole point of this page lies in the transformation conventions and in the gap between what the UI promises and what the CLI actually does."},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#situation","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"1. Position in the pipeline","x":"AutoMatrix is called by no other module in the repository. It is, however, a consumer of the .tfm files produced by the registration modules: Producer File produced Transformation type AREG_CBCT.py:157 <out>/<Region>/<patient>_OutReg/<patient>_<CB|MAND|MAX><add_name>_matrix.tfm affine / rigid SEMI_ASO_CBCT.py:176 <patient>_<add_name>_transform.tfm sitk.CompositeTransform ( utils.py:802 ) AREG_MRI.py:227 <mri>_reg_transform.tfm composite Review.py:499 and VFACE.py:2168 registration + manual nudge CompositeTransform([areg, nudge.GetInverse()]) FlexReg_CLI.py:186 <T2><suffix>.tfm affine, already "},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#fichiers","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs AutoMatrix.py widget: selectors, validation, mirror, progress bar Slicer Python applyMatrix.py Automatrix_Method : builds the CLI's parameter dict same General_tools.py search and GetPatients — a copy of the CLI's versions, used only for counting same Automatrix_CLI.py all the real work, 341 lines Slicer Python, SlicerMacroBuildScriptedCLI AutoMatrixLogic.process() ( AutoMatrix.py:1094 ) is a pass : the logic lives entirely in Automatrix_Method . No conda, no CUDA — verified by grep, no occurrence of torch , conda or .pth in AutoMatrix/ and Automatrix_CLI/."},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#formats","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"3. Transformation formats read — and those that aren't","x":"The UI ( AutoMatrix.py:1012 ) and the README advertise .npy , .h5 , .tfm , .mat , .txt . GetPatients collects all of them. The CLI then does, with no distinction ( Automatrix_CLI.py:285 ): try: tfm = sitk.ReadTransform(matrix) except Exception as e: logger.error(f\"ERROR reading transform {matrix}: {e}\") continue sitk.ReadTransform only knows ITK's TransformIO classes. Tested with Slicer 5.12's SimpleITK 2.5.5: Extension Actual result .tfm , .txt OK if and only if it's an Insight Transform File V1.0 . A text file containing a raw 4×4 matrix is rejected: ITK ERROR: Tags must be delimited .h5 , ."},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#convention","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"4. The transformation convention — the point that breaks everything","x":"4.1 Direction of the transformation ITK is fixed → moving : a sitk.Transform passed to a resampler maps the output physical point to the input physical point to sample from. The visible content therefore moves according to T⁻¹ . The code exploits this on both sides, and it is consistent: Volumes ( Automatrix_CLI.py:209 ): resampler.SetTransform(transform) — the transformation is passed as-is, the content moves by T⁻¹ . Landmarks ( Automatrix_CLI.py:176 ): tfm_inverted = transform.GetInverse() point['position'] = list(tfm_inverted.TransformPoint(point['position'])) T⁻¹ is explicitly applied to "},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#types-donnees","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"5. Data types handled","x":"GetPatients collects .vtk .vtp .stl .off .obj .nii .nii.gz .nrrd .mrk.json ( Automatrix_CLI.py:72 ). The CLI then only distinguishes two cases: is_landmark = scan.endswith(\".mrk.json\") ... image = sitk.ReadImage(scan) # everything else Confirmed bug Meshes are no longer processed. Tested: sitk.ReadImage on a vtkPolyData written as .vtk fails with sitk::ERROR: Unable to determine ImageIO reader . Same for .stl , .obj , .off , .vtp . The exception is caught ( Automatrix_CLI.py:310 ), logged as ERROR processing ... , and the file is skipped. Yet the UI still offers them, CheckGoodEntre validates "},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#reechantillonnage","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"6. Resampling and reference image","x":"resampler.SetInterpolator(sitk.sitkNearestNeighbor if is_seg else sitk.sitkLinear) resampler.SetDefaultPixelValue(0) resampler.SetReferenceImage(reference) The interpolator comes from the \"Check this box if using segmentations\" checkbox ( is_seg ). Nearest neighbor for a labelmap, linear otherwise — correct, and it's up to the user to declare it, nothing is auto-detected. The output grid is chosen as follows ( Automatrix_CLI.py:191 and :301 ), in decreasing priority: Composite transformation → the reference is guessed from the matrix 's path: matrix.replace(\"_transform.tfm\", \".nii.gz\") , falli"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#mirror","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"7. Mirror mode","x":"Mirror() ( AutoMatrix.py:304 ) downloads Mirror.zip from a personal GitHub release ( GaelleLeroux/DCBIA_Apply_matrix ), unzips it into <Documents>/SlicerDownloads/Mirror_matrix/ and fills the matrix field with Mirror/Matrix_mirror.tfm . The file's actual content: #Insight Transform File V1.0 Transform: AffineTransform_double_3_3 Parameters: -1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 FixedParameters: 0 0 0 Pitfall That is diag(-1, 1, 1) in LPS, centered at the origin: a reflection about the plane x_LPS = 0 . Since x_LPS points toward the patient's left, this is indeed a left/right swap — but about the sagittal p"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#appariement","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"8. File ↔ matrix matching","x":"GetPatients ( Automatrix_CLI.py:60 ) builds {patient_key: {'scan': [...], 'matrix': [...]}} . The key is obtained through a chain of .split() calls that is not the same on both sides : Tokens removed (in order) scan _Seg _seg _Scan _scan _Or _OR _MAND _MD _MAX _MX _CB _lm _T2 _T1 _Cl _MR , then .split('.')[0] , then _T0 … _T49 matrix _SegOr _Left _left _Right _right _Or _OR _MAND _MD _MAX _MX _CB _lm _T2 _T1 _Cl _MA _Mir _mir _Mirror _mirror _MR , then .split('.')[0] , then _T0 … _T49 GetPatients removes different tokens from the scan name and the matrix name to obtain the patient key. Here _T"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#sorties","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"9. Outputs","x":"matrix_suffix = f\"_{Path(matrix).stem}\" if args.matrix_name == \"True\" else \"\" out_suffix = f\"{args.suffix}{matrix_suffix}\" out_file = outpath.split(extension_scan)[0] + out_suffix + extension_scan The input directory tree is reproduced in the output folder ( scan.replace(input_patient, output_folder) ). Default suffix _apply , checkBoxMatrixName checked by default ( AutoMatrix.ui:342 ). Pitfall The README is stale on this point: it advertises patient1_T1_MA_apply_matrix1.nii.gz . The old version extracted the part of the matrix name that came after the patient key ( basename.split(ext)[0].spli"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#predit-calcule","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"10. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Transform reading computed ITK TransformIO CLI File ↔ matrix matching computed string splitting CLI (and a diverging copy in the widget) Volumes computed sitk.ResampleImageFilter , linear or nearest neighbor CLI Landmarks computed T⁻¹.TransformPoint per point CLI Mirror matrix computed fixed .tfm downloaded, diag(-1,1,1) GitHub release No neural network anywhere."},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#environnement","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"11. Environment","x":"Nothing to install: SimpleITK , numpy , vtk , qt are already in Slicer. No conda, no CUDA. The only network access is the mirror matrix download, and only if the Mirror checkbox is checked. Confirmed bug The progress bar is not fed by a log file despite the log_path parameter: DisplayAutomatrix.isProgress ( Progress.py:39 ) checks the mtime of log_path , but the CLI never writes to that file . Progress actually flows through the <filter-progress>0 / 2 / 0</filter-progress> sequence emitted by the CLI after each scan ( Automatrix_CLI.py:314 ) and the if kwds[\"progress\"] == 200 in isProgress — a"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#pieges","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"12. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Summary .npy does not work ( §3 ), though advertised by the UI and the README. Meshes no longer work ( §5 ), though advertised by the UI and the README. Regression from commit 7bc362b . .txt does not accept a raw 4×4 matrix ( §3 ), only ITK's text format. The landmarks' coordinateSystem is never read ( §4.2 ). No composition : N matrices for one patient give N files, not a chain ( §4.3 ). Outputs overwritten if checkBoxMatrixName is unchecked with several matrices ( §9 ). OnEndProcess divides by self.nb_scans ( AutoMatrix.py:953 ) with no guard: an empty input folder causes a ZeroDivisionError"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#litterature","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"13. Literature","x":"No paper covers AutoMatrix: it's a batch-application utility written for the lab (Gaëlle Leroux, UoM/DCBIA). The scientific content lives entirely in the modules that produce the matrices — AREG, ASO , MRI2CBCT , FlexReg — and is covered in their respective pages. For the transformation convention, the up-to-date reference is ITK / Slicer documentation rather than a paper: Transforms — Slicer user guide (fixed→moving direction, Slicer's RAS vs. ITK's LPS) Coordinate systems — Slicer developer guide SimpleITK — Transforms and Resampling SlicerAutomatedDentalTools DCBIA_Apply_matrix — the origin"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/SOURCES.html","p":"AutoMatrix — sources","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"AutoMatrix's bibliography: search done, negative result — no work describes or cites the tool. Unpublished, and nothing cites it. No paper, abstract, poster, thesis or Project Week report covers AutoMatrix, and none names it either. It's a batch-application utility written for the lab (Gaëlle Leroux, UoM/DCBIA): the scientific content is entirely in the modules that produce the matrices — AREG, ASO , MRI2CBCT , FlexReg — and is covered in their respective pages."},{"u":"AutoMatrix/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AutoMatrix — sources","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"Nothing. There is nothing to retrieve."},{"u":"AutoMatrix/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AutoMatrix — sources","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Nothing."},{"u":"AutoMatrix/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AutoMatrix — sources","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Neighborhood","x":"For the transformation convention, the up-to-date reference is documentation, not a paper. None of these resources describe AutoMatrix . Transforms — Slicer user guide — fixed→moving direction, Slicer's RAS versus ITK's LPS . It's the one thing a successor should have read before touching this module. Coordinate systems — Slicer developer guide SimpleITK — Transforms and Resampling DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools — the module's repository. GaelleLeroux/DCBIA_Apply_matrix — the original repository, which still hosts the mirror matrix and the test sets cited by the README. Searched for"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"Queue that drives nnUNetv2_predict over a batch of CT/CBCT scans, with four interchangeable weight bundles — DentalSegmentator and its three variants — plus all the guard-rail, geometry-restoration, and export work around them. Batch dento-maxillofacial segmentation of CT/CBCT scans. The module is not a network: it is a queue and an orchestrator around nnUNetv2_predict , with four interchangeable weight bundles. All the learning happens outside this repository; what is written here is the model choice, the memory guard-rails, the geometry restoration, and the exports."},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#situation","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"No other module in the repository calls BatchDentalSeg , and BatchDentalSeg does not call any other module in the repository. It is a terminal tool. It does, however, depend on an external Slicer extension , NNUNet ( SlicerNNUNetLib ), from which it uses three classes. Called Origin Role SlicerNNUNetLib.InstallLogic NNUNet extension setupPythonRequirements() : installs nnunetv2 in Slicer's Python SlicerNNUNetLib.Parameter NNUNet extension validates the model directory tree and builds the command line SlicerNNUNetLib.SegmentationLogic NNUNet extension writes the volume as NIfTI into a temporary"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#fichiers","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Size BATCHDENTALSEG.py wraps ScriptedLoadableModule , only instantiates the widget 83 lines SegmentationWidget.py everything : UI, queue, model choice, memory guard, crop, labels, exports 2940 lines Queue.py persistent queue, QueueItem / SegmentationQueue 204 lines PythonDependencyChecker.py download and freshness check for the DentalSegmentator weights 184 lines Utils.py , Signal.py , IconPath.py Qt helpers Resources/ML/ weight root; the repository ships Dataset111_453CT/.../dataset.json and plans.json Everything runs inside Slicer's Python . nnUNetv2_predict is launched as a child "},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#entrees","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"3. Inputs, folder traversal, naming","x":"3.1 What is accepted listVolumes() ( Queue.py:28 ) does a non-recursive glob over four patterns: *.nii , *.nii.gz , *.gipl , *.gipl.gz . Subfolders are not visited, and NRRD is not recognized — Slicer's default format, and a format the repository's other modules commonly produce. volumeStem() strips the compound suffix: case01.nii.gz → case01 (a Path.stem would leave case01.nii ). 3.2 The queue One entry = one scan , not one folder ( QueueItem , Queue.py:52 ): inputPath , outputDir , model , device , status , error , durationSec , autoCrop . Several folders with different models can therefore "},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#modeles","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"4. The four models","x":"4.1 What the combo box selects onApplyClickedForVolume() ( SegmentationWidget.py:1729 ) is an if/elif with four near-identical branches. Each builds the same object: Parameter(folds=\"0\", modelPath=basePath, device=self.deviceComboBox.currentText) The combo box's four entries all go through the same code; only the dataset folder and the label table change. Name in the combo box Folder under Resources/ML Weight origin DentalSegmentator Dataset111_453CT/nnUNetTrainer__nnUNetPlans__3d_fullres zip of the latest release of gaudot/SlicerDentalSegmentator , resolved via the GitHub API PediatricDentals"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#coeur","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"5. The non-learned core","x":"The only learning happens in a single step; everything else — preprocessing, reconstruction, the replay loop — is computed. 5.1 Label map reconstruction and geometry restoration _buildLabelArray() ( SegmentationWidget.py:1912 ): a single ExportSegmentsToLabelmapNode , with the explicit list of segmentId (which pins down the correspondence \"exported value i +1 ↔ segIds[i] \"), then a numpy LUT that remaps to the official values in one pass ( lut[exported] ). The earlier version rasterized the full extent of the volume once per segment: 55 rasterizations of a full-mouth CBCT for UniversalLab. 5.2"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#sorties","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"6. Outputs","x":"onInferenceFinished always writes, regardless of which formats are checked, <outputFolder>/<stem>_Segmentation.nii.gz : a label map with the values from _get_active_label_map , on the geometry (spacing, origin, IJKToRAS ) of the original volume — uncropped if a crop took place. This is the file expectedOutputPath() looks for to decide a scan has already been processed. Additional formats, checkboxes, all written into the same folder: Format Function Naming Details STL, OBJ ExportSegmentsClosedSurfaceRepresentationToFiles one file per segment smoothing 1.0, no merging VTK (per label) _exportVTK"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#predit","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"7. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Preprocessing (transposition, resampling, CTNormalization ) computed deterministic, driven by plans.json nnUNetv2_predict , child process Segmentation predicted PlainConvUNet 3D network, 1 fold, no TTA same Automatic crop computed geometric (air threshold + bounding box) widget, numpy Restoration onto the original grid computed index offset, no interpolation widget, numpy Label map reconstruction computed single export + LUT widget, VTK/numpy Mirror correction computed geometric (sagittal plane on the incisors) widget, numpy Meshes computed marching cubes + windowed-sinc + q"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#environnement","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"8. Environment","x":"Installed once per session , on the first Apply , by _runSetupThenStartQueue() ( SegmentationWidget.py:1651 ): pip install light-the-torch , then python -m light_the_torch install torch torchvision — ltt picks the CUDA wheel matching the detected driver, no version is pinned ; pip install numexpr>=2.10.2 ; pip install numpy<2.0 numexpr>=2.10.2 psutil , via PipRunner , a QProcess that returns control to the UI; SlicerNNUNetLib.InstallLogic().setupPythonRequirements() : nnunetv2 , outside this repository; PythonDependencyChecker.downloadWeightsIfNeeded() : DentalSegmentator weights only. numpy<2"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#pieges","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug — NasoMaxillaDentSeg The segment names and the label table diverge for NasoMaxillaDentSeg. _get_active_label_map gives Maxilla = 3 , Upper Teeth = 4 , Lower Teeth = 5 , Mandibular canal = 6 , while _updateSegmentationDisplay ( SegmentationWidget.py:2385 ) renames Segment_1..6 using the list [\"Upper Skull\", \"Mandible\", \"Upper Teeth\", \"Lower Teeth\", \"Mandibular canal\", \"Maxilla \"] . The first effect is visible on screen , before any question of export: the list is not in the order of the table, so the last four segments get the wrong name, the wrong color, and the wrong opacity. Se"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#litterature","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"10. Literature","x":"DentalSegmentator is published: Dot G., Chaurasia A., Dubois G., Savoldelli C., Haghighat S., Azimian S., Rahbar Taramsari A., Sivaramakrishnan G., Issa J., Dubey A., Schouman T., Gajny L. DentalSegmentator: robust open source deep learning-based CT and CBCT image segmentation. Journal of Dentistry 147:105130, 2024. doi:10.1016/j.jdent.2024.105130 — PMID 38878813 nnU-Net v2 (v2.2 per the Zenodo notice for the weights), 470 CT and CBCT scans from 7 institutions for training, 5 structures, tested hold-out on 256 scans (133 internal, 123 external): DSC 92.2 ± 6.3% internal, 94.2 ± 7.4% external. "},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/SOURCES.html","p":"BatchDentalSeg — sources","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"What is published, what is not, and what has been retrieved in this folder. The module is partially published : of the four models it bundles, only DentalSegmentator has a paper, and on that model the code and the paper agree. PediatricDentalSegmentator, UniversalLabDentalSegmentator, and NasoMaxillaDentSegmentator are not attached to any publication — a search was done, and nothing turned up (details at the end of this file). The module itself (queuing, memory management, batch UI) is described nowhere."},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"BatchDentalSeg — sources","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2024_Dot_DentalSegmentator_JDent.pdf Dot G., Chaurasia A., Dubois G., Savoldelli C., Haghighat S., Azimian S., Rahbar Taramsari A., Sivaramakrishnan G., Issa J., Dubey A., Schouman T., Gajny L. DentalSegmentator: Robust open source deep learning-based CT and CBCT image segmentation. Journal of Dentistry 147:105130, 2024. Author's accepted version deposited on HAL ( hal-05454422 , 17 p., author layout; the manuscript carries the preprint's title, \"robust deep learning-based CBCT image segmentation,\" and the HAL notice mistakenly names the journal International Journ"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"BatchDentalSeg — sources","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Isensee F., Jaeger P.F., Kohl S.A.A., Petersen J., Maier-Hein K.H. nnU-Net : a self-configuring method for deep learning-based biomedical image segmentation. Nature Methods 18(2):203-211, 2021. Reference version to cite (the one cited by the repository's README); only the arXiv preprint is freely redistributable. nature.com 10.1038/s41592-020-01008-z Mattos C.T., Mota Júnior S.L., Teodoro A.B., Lorenzoni D.C., Cury-Saramago A.A., Cevidanes L. 705 — Open-Source AI for Efficient 3D Orthodontic Workflows: Pediatric and Adult Dental Segmentation in Daily Practice. Journal of th"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/SOURCES.html#variantes-non-publiees","p":"BatchDentalSeg — sources","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"The three variants: nothing published","x":"No publication. What was searched, with no result: Europe PMC, queries PediatricDentalSegmentator , UniversalLabDentalSegmentator , NasoMaxillaDentSegmentator , pediatric dental segmentator mixed dentition CBCT nnU-Net : 0 results every time. OpenAlex on the same names: nothing, other than the EJWF conference abstract above. Web search on each model name and on \"Cevidanes / DCBIA + pediatric CBCT + primary teeth + 513\": the only hits were the repository's own README and work from other, unrelated teams (e.g. Baraka et al., Multi-Architecture deep learning for CBCT segmentation of dental hard t"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"BatchDentalSeg — sources","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"Adjacent work","x":"These works do not describe this module ; they evaluate DentalSegmentator from the outside, or provide its foundation. nnU-Net (Isensee et al. 2021): the foundation of all four models. Describes the self-configuring method, not these models or their weights. Sinard et al. 2025 (file 2025_Sinard_DL_impacted_teeth_CBCT.xml ): compares four segmentation methods, including DentalSegmentator, on CBCT scans of patients with multiple impacted teeth. An external evaluation of the original model; says nothing about the three variants nor about the batch module. The corresponding thesis is open access o"},{"u":"CLIC/2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.html","p":"Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"NA-MIC Project Week 43 project page (Montreal, 2025): it takes CLIC as an existing building block and prototypes an extension to classify the severity of root resorption in adjacent teeth. Note NA-MIC project page reproduced here as-is (formatting only): the only primary source found on CLIC — see SOURCES.md . This is not a publication: a project in progress, presented by its authors. Field Value layout pw43-project permalink /:path/ project_title Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption category Segmentation / Classif"},{"u":"CLIC/2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.html#description","p":"Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Project Description","x":"The CLIC module uses a Mask R-CNN segmentation model to locate and classify impacted canines in CBCT scans. This project extends CLIC by developing a supervised model to automatically classify the severity of root resorption in teeth adjacent to impacted canines. Objective Segment impacted canines using the existing CLIC module. Extract adjacent teeth volumes for analysis. Assemble an annotated dataset of adjacent teeth with clinician‐provided resorption severity scores. Train a classification model to predict root resorption severity from segmented volumes. Integrate and visualize segmentatio"},{"u":"CLIC/2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.html#illustrations","p":"Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Illustrations","x":"Figure 1: Impacted canine segmentation results from CLIC. Image hosted on GitHub, not embedded here so the page stays readable offline: view the image on GitHub ."},{"u":"CLIC/2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.html#references","p":"Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Background and References","x":"No response"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"CLIC segments a CBCT slice by slice with a Mask R-CNN and classifies each detected instance as a buccal, bicortical, or palatal impacted canine — classification and localization come out of the same network pass. Classification and Localization of Impacted Canines. 2D slice-by-slice instance segmentation of a CBCT by a torchvision Mask R-CNN , whose predicted class encodes the intraosseous position of the impacted canine: buccal, bicortical, palatal . The module's name promises two tasks; the code has only one network . There is no separate classification network, no separate localization netw"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#situation","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"Standalone in both directions: no module in the repository calls CLIC, and CLIC calls no other module in the repository. It calls nothing but its own runner: Called When How CLIC/runner/clic_runner.py once per scan CondaSetUpCall.condaRunFilePython(runner, [\"--params_json=…\"], \"clic_env\") release CLIC_model/final_model.pth Download Model button requests.get , written to ~/Documents/CLIC_Models It starts from the raw CBCT. No prior segmentation, no landmarks, no orientation: the module reads a .nii / .nii.gz and outputs a label map. It does not depend on AMASSS , ASO , or ALI in any way. CLIC i"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#fichiers","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs CLIC/CLIC.py widget, 622 lines, of which ~230 are a dark stylesheet and ~50 are conda patches Slicer Python CLIC/runner/clic_runner.py all the processing , 110 lines clic_env conda env, GPU if available CLIC/Resources/UI/CLIC.ui the interface — No Slicer CLI, no .xml , no MedX_Method -like layer: the widget writes a parameters JSON to slicer.app.temporaryPath and launches the runner as a standalone Python script inside the conda environment. The widget imports CondaSetUp at module level ( CLIC/CLIC.py:18 ) without declaring it in parent.dependencies : without SlicerCond"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#entrees","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"3.1 Scan collection _collect_scans() ( CLIC/CLIC.py:316 ) accepts .nii , .nii.gz , .nrrd , .mha , .mhd , and follows a two-step rule: if there are subfolders containing at least one valid file, it returns the list of these subfolders ; otherwise, the list of valid files in the folder. Case 1 produces directory Path s, passed as-is as input_path to the runner, which does nib.load(str(inp)) : immediate failure. The nominal case is therefore a flat folder of .nii.gz . Furthermore the runner only uses nibabel : .nrrd , .mha , .mhd are accepted by the widget's filter and unreadable by the runner. P"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#modele","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"4. The model","x":"The only algorithmic path in CLIC, replayed for every axial slice. The entirety of the clinical decision (buccal / bicortical / palatal) comes out of the \"network\" box — the rest is deterministic arithmetic. 4.1 Architecture _blank_model(nc) ( CLIC/runner/clic_runner.py:44 ), called with nc = 4 : m = maskrcnn_resnet50_fpn(weights=None) m.roi_heads.box_predictor = FastRCNNPredictor(in_f, nc) m.roi_heads.mask_predictor = MaskRCNNPredictor(in_fm, 256, nc) backbone : ResNet-50 + FPN ( _resnet_fpn_extractor , 5 levels); RPN then ROI heads : box head ( FastRCNNPredictor : cls_score 4 classes, bbox_p"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#coeur","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"5. The algorithmic core","x":"There is no non-learned part, apart from thresholding: no ICP , no morphology, no component extraction, no measurement. The entire runner is: load the model, loop over Z, threshold, write. The entire decision is inside the Mask R-CNN ."},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#sorties","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"6. Post-processing and outputs","x":"out_dir = out_root / inp.stem out_path = out_dir / f\"{inp.stem}_{suffix}.nii.gz\" nib.save(nib.Nifti1Image(seg.astype(np.int16), nii.affine, nii.header), out_path) a subfolder per scan , named after the scan; suffix from suffixLineEdit , default \"seg\" ; Path.stem on X.nii.gz is X.nii : the folder is called X.nii and the file X.nii_seg.nii.gz . Ugly but without functional consequence; affine and header are taken from the input volume : the label map overlays without registration. The original header describes a floating-point data type while the data is int16 ; Nifti1Image adjusts the header's d"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#predit-calcule","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"7. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Per-slice min-max normalization computed arithmetic, 2 lines clic_env 800/1333 resizing + ImageNet standardization computed deterministic, internal to torchvision clic_env Detection, classification, mask predicted network : Mask R-CNN ResNet50-FPN, 4 classes clic_env , GPU if available 0.7 / 0.5 thresholds and write-back into the volume computed arithmetic clic_env Colors and legend Slicer widget All the clinical content — is this an impacted canine, and on which side of the cortical bone — comes out of the network alone. There is no post-hoc verification."},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#environnement","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Environment","x":"Item Value Source Conda env clic_env (specific to CLIC) CLIC/CLIC.py:120 Python 3.9 CLIC/CLIC.py:167 Creation numpy<2.0.0 , scipy , nibabel , requests same Then, via pip torch==2.2.0 , torchvision==0.17.0 , torchaudio==2.2.0 from the cu118 index CLIC/CLIC.py:182 Then pip install 'numpy<2.0' a second time CLIC/CLIC.py:204 GPU optional, automatic CPU fallback CLIC/runner/clic_runner.py:73 _ensure_env() ( CLIC/CLIC.py:158 ) is replayed on every click on Predict : both pip install calls run again, even when everything is already in place (pip returns quickly but still queries the network). self._e"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#pieges","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug The runner emits its markers via logger.info , with the formatter '%(name)s - %(levelname)s - (%(filename)s:%(lineno)d) - %(message)s' ( CLIC/runner/clic_runner.py:39 ). The received lines look like CLIC_runner - INFO - (clic_runner.py:60) - [SEG] /path , so the tests ln.startswith(\"[PROGRESS]\") and ln.startswith(\"[SEG]\") ( CLIC/CLIC.py:268 ) are never true . Consequence: the progress bar stays at zero and the produced segmentation is never loaded into Slicer — it is nonetheless correctly written to disk. Minimal fix: parse with \"[SEG]\" in ln and cut from the marker onward. The s"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#litterature","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"10. Literature","x":"No paper covers CLIC. The corresponding work is announced under the title Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption (Tulissi, Cevidanes, Prieto), presented at NA-MIC Project Week 43 (Montreal, 2025) — a project in progress, not a publication. The original code comes from the personal repository ashmoy/maskRcnn , which is also where the test scans cited by the README come from ( MN138.nii , UM06.nii ). Not to be confused with the neighboring work below, which did not produce this code: buccal/palatal localization of impa"},{"u":"CLIC/SOURCES.html","p":"CLIC — sources","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"No publication describes CLIC: the only primary source is a NA-MIC Project Week 43 project page, the rest is bibliographic neighborhood on a related problem. No publication describes CLIC. Checked: nothing under the module's name, nothing under its authors' names, no article, no preprint, no IADR/AADOCR abstract found. The only primary source is a NA-MIC Project Week 43 project page (Montreal, 2025), retrieved into this folder ( 2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.md ), which presents CLIC as an already-existing tool and describes ongoing work that extends it to root-resorption severity. "},{"u":"CLIC/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"CLIC — sources","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.md Tulissi E, Cevidanes L, Prieto J, Bianchi J. \"Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption\". Project page, NA-MIC Project Week 43, Montreal, 2025. project report — only primary source project page 2017_He_Mask_RCNN.pdf He K, Gkioxari G, Dollár P, Girshick R. \"Mask R-CNN\". arXiv :1703.06870, 2017 (ICCV 2017). article — architecture used arXiv:1703.06870 .xml / .txt Kahraman EN, Güldiken İN, Tekin A, Kanbak T, Yılancı H. \"Buccal or palatal? AI-based loc"},{"u":"CLIC/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"CLIC — sources","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Galdi M, Cannatà D, Celentano F, Rizzo L, Rossi D, Bocchino T, Martina S. \"Development of a fully deep learning model to improve the reproducibility of sector classification systems for predicting unerupted maxillary canine likelihood of impaction\". arXiv :2511.20493, 2025. arXiv — freely downloadable, not included here: covers sector-based classification on panoramic radiographs , a different problem — Salmanpour F, Akpınar M. \"Performance of Chat Generative Pretrained Transformer-4.0 in determining labiolingual localization of maxillary impacted canines\". AJODO , 2025. pu"},{"u":"CLIC/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"CLIC — sources","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Neighborhood","x":"This work does not describe CLIC's code . Buccal/palatal localization on panoramic radiographs. Kahraman 2026 ( BMC Medical Imaging ) and Helal 2026 ( Children ) train 2D image-classification CNNs on panoramic radiographs, using CBCT as ground truth. Only two classes, one image per patient. CLIC works directly on the CBCT volume, slice by slice, with three classes. 3D segmentation of impacted canines on CBCT. Swaity 2024 ( Scientific Reports ) and Ünal 2025 ( BMC Oral Health ) segment the impacted canine with a volumetric network ( nnU-Net / 3D CNN). They delineate the tooth; they do not class"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"A language-model (LLM) inference module that reads free-text clinical notes and extracts key–value pairs from them — it is neither a geometric tool nor an image-processing one, despite its place in a suite of dental tools. Scope warning. Unlike AutoCrop3D and AutoMatrix , CNE is not a geometric utility: it touches no volume, no mesh, no coordinate. It is an LLM inference module that reads free-text clinical notes and writes key–value pairs. At the core of the module sits a quantized GGUF model loaded by llama-cpp-python inside Slicer's Python."},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#acronyme","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"0. The acronym","x":"The repository never spells out the acronym in any documentation: the README contains no CNE section (it still documents MedX , which CNE replaces, cf. CMakeLists.txt:42 \"Replaced by CNE\"). The module's help text ( CNE.py:147 ) says only \"This tool helps to create summaries of clinical notes\". Two log lines, and only those, give the expansion: logger.info(\"CNE (Clinical Notes Extraction)\") ( CNE.py:329 and CNE.py:606 ), and the CLI publishes the same name to the Slicer supervisor: <filter-name>Clinical Notes Extraction</filter-name> ( CNE_CLI.py:91 ). CNE = Clinical Notes Extraction , attested"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#situation","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"1. Place in the pipeline","x":"Terminal module. Nothing calls it, and it calls no other module in the repository. Declared in CMakeLists.txt:51 . It replaces the \"Summarization\" half of MedX . MedX/ and MedX_CLI/ are still in the tree but disabled from the build ( CMakeLists.txt:42-44 ); the \"Dashboard\" half of MedX ( dashboard.png + patient_data.csv ) has no equivalent in CNE and therefore disappears from the compiled extension. From the raw clinical note to the extraction file: a single pass, with no chunking, through a GGUF model served locally by llama.cpp . Difference from MedX Summarize MedX CNE Engine transformers + "},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#fichiers","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs CNE.py widget: dependencies, model download, launching the CLI Slicer's Python CNE_CLI.py text extraction + llama.cpp inference Slicer's Python ( SlicerMacroBuildScriptedCLI ) CNE.ui two ctkPathLineEdit widgets in Dirs mode, two TMJ /Ortho radio buttons — Resources/testfiles/ 4 sample notes (2 TMJ, 2 Ortho), stored in the clear in the repository — No conda. The CLI is a scripted CLI , so it is launched by slicer.cli.run in Slicer's Python — it is in that same interpreter that llama-cpp-python is installed and the model is loaded into memory."},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#dependances","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"3. Dependencies, installed on demand","x":"check_dependencies() ( CNE.py:83 ) is called on clicking Apply , not on module load. It tests three imports and, if any are missing, asks for confirmation before slicer.util.pip_install : Import tested Package installed Use llama_cpp llama-cpp-python --extra-index-url https://abetlen.github.io/llama-cpp-python/whl/cpu inference fitz pymupdf PDF text extraction docx python-docx Word text extraction Two points worth knowing: Gotcha install_function ( CNE.py:44 ) forces CC=gcc and CXX=g++ during installation and then restores the original values — this is for the case where pip has to compile lla"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#modeles","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"4. The models","x":"No weights ship in the repository. getModelPath() ( CNE.py:478 ) downloads the GGUF on demand, via urllib.request.urlretrieve with a QProgressDialog , into <Documents>/SlicerDownloads/CNE/model/ . UI mode repo_id File requested Local name n_ctx TMJ dcbia/Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct-TMJ qwen-ft-q4_k_m.gguf Qwen-2.5-7B-TMJ.gguf 6144 Ortho dcbia/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-Ortho model-q4_0.gguf Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Ortho.gguf 2048 Constructed URL: https://huggingface.co/{repo_id}/resolve/main/{fileName} . Confirmed bug The Ortho download is broken. Verified via the Hugging Face API: the repository dcbia/Met"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#entrees","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"5. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"SUPPORTED_EXTENSIONS = (\".txt\", \".pdf\", \".docx\") . The scan is non-recursive : glob.glob(os.path.join(notesFolder_input, f\"*{ext}\")) ( CNE_CLI.py:133 ) — unlike every other module in the repository, which uses iglob(**, recursive=True) . A folder made of per-patient subfolders will yield nothing. Text extraction ( CNE_CLI.py:73 ): Format Extractor Detail .pdf fitz.open then \"\\n\".join(page.get_text() for page in doc) no OCR: a scanned PDF comes out empty .docx docx.Document , paragraph.text tables are lost ( doc.paragraphs does not iterate over them) .txt open(..., encoding=\"utf-8\") a file in c"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#inference","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"6. Inference","x":"A single pass per file, with no chunking ( CNE_CLI.py:192 ): messages = [] if notesType.upper() == \"TMJ\": messages.append({\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": INSTRUCTION_TMJ}) messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": clinical_text}) output = llm.create_chat_completion(messages=messages, max_tokens=500, temperature=0.1) The two modes diverge before the call to the model (system instruction, context size) and then converge in the same llama.cpp call. The Ortho path is highlighted: it is the one whose download fails (§4). Worth noting: Gotcha Ortho mode sends no instruction. Only the raw note goes o"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#post-traitement","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"7. Post-processing the response","x":"The model is expected to answer in JSON; the code does not enforce this (no response_format , no GBNF grammar) and simply fishes out the JSON from the text ( CNE_CLI.py:207 ): start_idx = ai_response.find('{') end_idx = ai_response.rfind('}') + 1 data = json.loads(ai_response[start_idx:end_idx]) if \"extraction\" in data: data = data[\"extraction\"] for key, value in data.items(): formatted_response += f\"{key} : {value}\\n\" So: first opening brace through last closing brace, unwrapping of a possible extraction level, then flattening into key : value lines. If the parse fails or there is no brace at"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#sorties","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"8. Outputs","x":"One text file per input note, in the output folder: Extraction_<note name without extension>.txt Content: key : value lines, or the model's raw response if the parse failed. No CSV, no json, no per-patient aggregation — MedX 's dashboard was not carried over. The output folder is created by os.makedirs(..., exist_ok=True) on the widget side ( CNE.py:578 )."},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#predit-calcule","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"9. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where PDF/Word text extraction computed PyMuPDF / python-docx CLI Typographic normalization computed fixed replacement table, 7 entries CLI Key–value pair extraction predicted LLM, Qwen-2.5-7B-TMJ or Llama-3.1-8B-Ortho, GGUF Q4 CLI, in-process llama_cpp Formatting computed find('{') / json.loads / flattening loop CLI All the clinical content of the output comes from the model. There is no rule, no dictionary of terms, no schema validation: whatever the model writes is what gets saved."},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#environnement","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"10. Environment","x":"Element Value Source Interpreter Slicer's Python, no conda SlicerMacroBuildScriptedCLI , CMakeLists.txt Packages installed on the fly llama-cpp-python (CPU index), pymupdf , python-docx CNE.py:93-100 GPU requested ( n_gpu_layers=-1 ) but the install forces a CPU build §3 Weights ~4.4 GB (TMJ) / ~4.9 GB (Ortho), <Documents>/SlicerDownloads/CNE/model/ CNE.py:500 RAM not constrained in the code; a 7–8 B Q4_K_M model needs roughly 5–6 GB resident on top of Slicer inference"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#pieges","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"11. Gotchas and fragile points","x":"The Ortho model does not download : wrong file name, HTTP 404 (§4). Fix: model-q4_0.gguf → model-q4_k_m.gguf . The test files' output folders do not exist. copyTestFiles() ( CNE.py:409 ) copies input_<type> and output_<type> , but Resources/testfiles/ contains only the two input_ folders. The shutil.copytree of the output folder is skipped with a warning, and the UI ends up with an output path filled in that does not yet exist. Non-recursive scan of the input folder (§5). No length limit before the call: a note that is too long fails instead of being chunked, whereas MedX did chunk (§6). This "},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#litterature","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"12. Literature","x":"No publication is cited in the repository for CNE, and both dcbia/* Hugging Face repositories are published with no model card and no reference. The fine-tuning protocol, the TMJ/Ortho annotation corpus, and the evaluation are not determinable from the repository . What is verifiable: The MedX → CNE lineage, documented only by CMakeLists.txt:42 and the MedX section of README.md:425 , which still describes the old BART pipeline and the vanished dashboard. The base models: Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct , Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct . The fine-tuned repositories: dcbia/Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct-TMJ , dcbia/Meta-"},{"u":"CNE/SOURCES.html","p":"CNE — sources","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Unpublished tool: what the literature search looked for, did not find, and the neighborhood gathered instead. Unpublished tool. After checking, no publication describes CNE, nor the two fine-tuned models it downloads. The acronym Clinical Notes Extraction itself appears nowhere outside the repository. What was searched for, and turned up nothing: Search Result Europe PMC \"Clinical Notes Extraction\" 6 articles, none from the group, none about this code Europe PMC (AUTH:\"Cevidanes L\" OR AUTH:\"Prieto JC\" OR AUTH:\"Bianchi J\") AND (\"large language model\" OR LLM OR Llama OR Qwen OR GPT) 3 articles, "},{"u":"CNE/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"CNE — sources","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"Nothing that describes CNE. The files below fall under the neighborhood (next section) and are listed here because they were downloaded. File Reference Type DOI / ID 2024_Wiest_privacy_preserving_LLM.xml / .txt Wiest IC, Ferber D, Zhu J, van Treeck M, Meyer SK, Juglan R, Carrero ZI, Paech D, Kleesiek J, Ebert MP, Truhn D, Kather JN. Privacy-preserving large language models for structured medical information retrieval. npj Digit Med 2024;7:257. Article (open access) 10.1038/s41746-024-01233-2 — PMC11415382 2026_Panchal_LLM_deidentification_EHR.xml / .txt Panchal O, Chang NW, Zhao ZR, Ramesh Nad"},{"u":"CNE/SOURCES.html#a-consulter","p":"CNE — sources","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"No reference of this kind: there is no paywalled article to flag either."},{"u":"CNE/SOURCES.html#modeles-depots","p":"CNE — sources","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"Models, repositories, engine","x":"Item Where Base models meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct , Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct Fine-tuned repositories (no model card) dcbia/Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct-TMJ , dcbia/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-Ortho Inference engine, GGUF format, quantization Q4_K_M llama-cpp-python , llama.cpp The module SlicerAutomatedDentalTools"},{"u":"CNE/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"CNE — sources","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"Neighborhood","x":"None of these works is about CNE. They share neither the authors, nor the models, nor the corpus. They are gathered here because they describe the same technical gesture — running an open-weight LLM locally , without sending the patient's text to a third-party service, to extract structured fields from it — and they make it possible to assess what CNE does, in the absence of any text about CNE itself. Wiest 2024 (file above): the work closest to CNE's gesture. Open-weight LLMs deployed locally extract structured data from clinical reports, with the privacy argument used to justify the local de"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"Classification of .vtk meshes by a two-branch network (multi-head-attention point cloud + multi-view EfficientNet-B0 rendering), systematically followed by a Grad-CAM pass written as a per-point scalar on the mesh. Dental Oral and Craniofacial Shape Analysis eXplainability and Interpretability. A single user action: pick a data type, an input folder, an output folder. Everything else — which checkpoint, which architecture, how many classes — is inferred from the data type."},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#situation","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Place in the pipeline","x":"Standalone: nothing in the repository calls DOCShapeAXI. The input meshes are assumed to be already segmented and extracted elsewhere ( AMASSS for condyles, airway segmentation, etc.); the module does not produce its own surfaces . Overview: DOCShapeAXI performs neither segmentation nor surface extraction, it classifies meshes that are already ready. Called When How DOCShapeAXI_CLI on every task conda run -n shapeaxi python -m DOCShapeAXI_CLI <8 args> model_path.json on raw.githubusercontent.com at CLI startup, if the .ckpt is missing requests.get then urlretrieve shapeaxi pip package at all t"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#fichiers","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.py widget: conda, model choice, progress bar, ~180 lines of dark-theme styling Slicer's Python DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py prediction + explainability , 307 lines shapeaxi conda env, GPU if available model_path.json 5 checkpoints and their URLs — install_pytorch.py picks the pytorch3d wheel based on the installed torch, then shapeaxi>=2.0.2 conda env Most of the actual work lives in the shapeaxi pip package, outside the repository. The architecture details below were read from the real installation ( shapeaxi/saxi_nets_lightning.py , saxi_layers.py , saxi"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#entrees","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"3.1 The CSV The CLI flat-lists args.input_dir , keeps the .vtk files, and writes <output_dir>/files_<data_type>.csv with the header surf and one file name per line ( csv_edit , DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py:102 ). Paths are relative; SaxiDataset joins them with mount_point = input_dir . data_type contains spaces (\"Mandibular Condyle\"), so the csv is named files_Mandibular Condyle.csv . The csv is regenerated only if it does not already exist ( DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py:282 ): adding meshes to the input folder without emptying the output folder will not get them processed. 3.2 Geometric normalization EvalTransfo"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#modeles","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"4. The models","x":"4.1 The decision table find_model_name() / find_nn_type() ( DOCShapeAXI.py:1012 ) — the keyword tested is a token of the combo label ( self.data_type.split(' ') ): Data type (combo) Token Task model nn num_classes Mandibular Condyle Condyle severity condyles_4_class SaxiMHAFBClassification 4 Nasopharynx Airway Obstruction Airway binary airways_2_class SaxiMHAFBClassification 2 Nasopharynx Airway Obstruction Airway severity airways_4_class SaxiMHAFBClassification 4 Nasopharynx Airway Obstruction Airway regression airways_4_regress SaxiMHAFBRegression 1 Alveolar Bone Defect in Cleft Cleft severi"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#explicabilite","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"5. The explainability mechanism","x":"Note This is the module's most distinctive part, and it runs systematically after prediction — neither an option nor a checkbox ( saxi_gradcam , DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py:126 ). From logit to per-point scalar: saliency is computed on the rendered views then reprojected onto the mesh. a) The targeted layer. target_layer = getattr(model.convnet.module, '_blocks') mv_cam = LayerGradCam(model, target_layer[-1], device_ids=[0]) model.convnet.module is the EfficientNet-B0; _blocks[-1] is its last MBConv block . Pitfall Major consequence: the saliency describes only the multi-view branch . The point-cloud b"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#sorties","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"6. Post-processing and outputs","x":"File Content <output>/<model>.ckpt the downloaded checkpoint, left in place <output>/files_<data_type>.csv the list of .vtk files <output>/files_<data_type>_prediction.csv the input csv plus one <task>_prediction column per task <output>/explainability/<task>/<name>.vtk a copy of the mesh plus N grad_cam_* arrays <log_path> \"<task>,<predict|explainability>,<index>,<num_classes>\" The column accumulation is intentional ( DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py:240 ): if the prediction csv already exists, it is read back in and used as a base, which is why an airway dataset ends up with binary_prediction , severity_p"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#predit-calcule","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"7. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Unit-sphere normalization computed geometric (bbox + scale) shapeaxi Color-encoded normals computed geometric (VTK) shapeaxi 4096-point sampling computed stochastic (pytorch3d) GPU 42-view 224×224 RGB+z render computed pytorch3d rasterization GPU Class / ratio predicted MHAEncoder + EfficientNet-B0 + attention GPU Grad-CAM map predicted derived from the network (Captum, multi-view branch only) GPU Face → vertex reprojection, cross-view max computed geometric GPU/CPU Median filter on the mesh computed geometric, topological neighborhood CPU Pitfall Two sources of randomness a"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#environnement","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Environment","x":"Item Value Source Conda env shapeaxi , shared with ALI /AREG/ASO/FlexReg DOCShapeAXI.py:801 Python 3.12 DOCShapeAXI.py:842 Env creation torch>=2.8,<2.13 , ocnn==2.2.1 , SimpleITK same pytorch3d wheel chosen from torch.__version__ + CUDA, from https://ImageMindAnalytics.github.io/pytorch3d-wheels/simple/ install_pytorch.py:24 shapeaxi shapeaxi>=2.0.2 , installed after pytorch3d install_pytorch.py:186 Patch patch_dentalmodelseg() rewrites saxi_nets.DentalModelSeg → saxi_nets_lightning.DentalModelSeg in the installed package install_pytorch.py:210 GPU not mandatory ( torch.device('cuda' if …) ) b"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#pieges","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug The CLI is written for the shapeaxi 1.0.x API, the environment installs 2.0.2. This is the first thing to check if the module stops working: shapeaxi 1.0.10 shapeaxi 2.0.2 signature forward(self, X_pc, X_views) forward(self, X_mesh) return value x x, x_w, X class-count hparam out_classes num_classes The CLI calls model(X_pc, X_views) and uses the return value as a tensor ( DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py:227 ): on 2.0.2 this is a TypeError (3 positional arguments). And the published checkpoints carry out_classes , which the 2.0.2 __init__ no longer reads — the load_from_checkpoint call would "},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#litterature","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"10. Literature","x":"Three references, not to be confused with one another: 1. The framework. ShapeAXI: Shape Analysis Explainability and Interpretability , Prieto, Miranda, Gurgel, Anchling, Hutin, Barone, Al Turkestani, Aliaga, Yatabe, Bianchi, Cevidanes — Proc. SPIE Medical Imaging, April 2024 , DOI 10.1117/12.3007053 . It reports 79.78% on condyles and 81.58% on cleft severity — precisely two of the three datasets supported here. Code / paper divergence The code diverges from the paper. The paper describes a multi-view render over an icosahedron subdivision processed by a ResNet-18 plus additive attention, wit"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/SOURCES.html","p":"DOCShapeAXI — sources","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for the tool: what is deposited in this folder, what remains to be checked online, and the neighborhood of publications that do not describe the module itself. A partially published tool, and published in separate pieces: no paper describes the Slicer module, but each of the three embedded datasets has its own clinical publication, and the shapeaxi framework has its own as well. Code / paper divergence The code diverges from its reference paper. The 2024 SPIE paper ( 2024_Prieto_ShapeAXI_SPIE ) describes a multi-view render over an icosahedron subdivision, with the images processe"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"DOCShapeAXI — sources","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2024_Prieto_ShapeAXI_SPIE.xml / .txt Prieto JC, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Anchling L, Hutin N, Barone S, Al Turkestani N, Aliaga A, Yatabe M, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L. ShapeAXI: Shape Analysis Explainability and Interpretability. Proc. SPIE 12931, Medical Imaging 2024: Imaging Informatics for Healthcare, Research, and Applications, 1293116, April 2024. Proceedings (full JATS text, NIHMS1990711 author manuscript) 10.1117/12.3007053 — PMC11085013, PMID 38736903 2025_Mattos_adenoid_airway_obstruction.xml / .txt Mattos CT, Dole L, Mota-Júnior SL, Cury-Saramago AA, Bianchi J, "},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"DOCShapeAXI — sources","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"To check online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Dole L, Mattos CT, Bianchi J, Oh H, Evangelista K, Valladares Neto J, Mota-Júnior SL, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC. Enhancing airway obstruction diagnosis with multimodal 3D shape analysis. Int J Comput Assist Radiol Surg 2026;21(3):529–538 (online 14 Oct. 2025). The closest reference to the module , by the author of the original lucieDLE/DOC-ShapeAXI repository: it explicitly presents an \"open-source, automated deep learning tool,\" combines multi-view and point-cloud, and works on 1215 CBCTs from four centers. Paywalled; likely to reach PMC under NIH Public Access. Springer · Pu"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/SOURCES.html#depots","p":"DOCShapeAXI — sources","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"Repositories and software objects","x":"Object Where shapeaxi framework (the pip package actually run) DCBIA-OrthoLab/ShapeAXI Original repository of the module lucieDLE/DOC-ShapeAXI Zenodo software archive for ShapeAXI 10.5281/zenodo.22118309 Captum — LayerGradCam , used in §5 of the module captum.ai"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"DOCShapeAXI — sources","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"Neighborhood","x":"These works do not describe DOCShapeAXI . They are listed here because they set the condyle severity scale that the condyles_4_class dataset reuses without any text documenting it for this checkpoint. Tubau Ribera 2019 and de Dumast 2018 (SPIE + CMIG) , above: the Shape Variation Analyzer lineage, the direct ancestor of the group's condyle classifier. They grade condylar degeneration into 5, 6, or 7 groups depending on the version (normal, near-normal, overgrowth, Degeneration 1–4), on geometric descriptors + heat kernel signature , not on multi-view rendering. None of the three produces 4 cla"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Patient-by-patient registration of Intra Oral Scans, with a registration patch built by hand (palatal butterfly, free curve, or mucogingival band) instead of being predicted by a network — the interactive counterpart to AREG_IOS."},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#situation","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"Nothing in the repository calls FlexReg: it is a terminal tool, driven by the operator. It does, however, call two pretrained networks, and it produces the input to AREG_IOS — both tools read exactly the same point array. Called When Why dentalmodelseg (ShapeAXI / DentalModelSeg ) if the mesh has no Universal_ID array segment and number the crowns ALI_IOS (weights Lower_MG_*.pth ) lower-arch mode only place the 13 mucogingival landmarks Downstream, AREG_IOS.py:191 does vtkMeshTeeth(list_teeth=[1], property=\"Butterfly\") and AREG_IOS.py:205 the same on MGL_ARRAY_NAME : a patch built in FlexReg i"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#fichiers","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs FlexReg/FlexReg.py Slicer widget, 4400 lines: UI, joysticks, previews, full MGL path Slicer's Python FlexReg_CLI/FlexReg_CLI.py the CLI: butterfly, curve, delete, icp, icp_mgl Slicer's Python via slicer.cli.run , CUDA required FlexReg_Method/ ( FlexReg_CLI/FlexReg_Method ) orientation, butterfly, geodesic dilation, ICP same FlexReg_utils/ ( FlexReg/FlexReg_utils ) butterfly preview (numpy, CPU), MGL patch (numpy/scipy), pytorch3d installation Slicer's Python Non-obvious point The CLI does not run inside the conda environment. It goes through slicer.cli.run(slicer.module"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#entrees","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"Pitfall — file conversion ensureVtkInput() ( FlexReg.py:3454 ) converts a .stl to .vtk before any operation. This is not cosmetic: dental_model_seg.py does os.remove(args.stl) and would destroy the source file if it were handed the .stl directly. LPS ↔ RAS flip. The CLI reads the .vtk (LPS), applies Scale(-1,-1,1) to work in RAS like Slicer, and flips back before writing ( FlexReg_CLI.py:39 ). All internal computation is therefore in RAS. Canonical framing — the key preprocessing step, orientation.py : centroids of teeth Universal_ID 3, 5, 12, 14 (UR6, UR4, UL4, UL6); local basis on each side:"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#modeles","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"4. The models","x":"4.1 Crown segmentation — DentalModelSeg predicted checkSegmentation() ( FlexReg.py:3657 ) does not test for the array's presence: it attempts the orientation above and reads the exception. ToothNoExist → the mesh is segmented but one of the 4 reference teeth is missing → message, stop. NoSegmentationSurf → no segmentation array → shapeaxi_conda() . conda run -n shapeaxi python -m CrownSegmentationcli \\ <surf> None <out> 1 latest 0 Universal_ID 0 None <vtk_folder> <dentalmodelseg_path> Arguments in order: surface, input csv, output folder, overwrite=1 , latest model, crownsegmentation=0 , array"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#coeur","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"5. The algorithmic core","x":"5.1 Path A — the Butterfly patch (upper arch) computed make_butterfly.py . No network, purely geometric. a) Four centroids. The operator picks 4 teeth (anterior L/R, posterior L/R); vtkMeanTeeth averages the vertices carrying that Universal_ID . b) Three families of parameters applied to these centroids: Parameter Effect Detail shift_lr , shift_ap translates the whole patch the same vector is added to all 4 centroids; since the landmarks are affine combinations with weights summing to 1, the shift carries through the interpolation and the shape is unchanged adjust (mm) lengthens / shortens off"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#sorties","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"6. Post-processing and outputs","x":"The mesh. Patch type → rewritten into the input file . ICP type → written into path_output as <T2 name without extension><suffix>.vtk , after flipping back to LPS. The transform , the delicate step ( FlexReg_CLI.py:186 ): flip = np.diag([-1, -1, 1, 1]) composed = flip @ matrix_array @ flip # RAS -> LPS by conjugation composed_inv = np.linalg.inv(composed) # ITK convention: fixed -> moving sitk_tfm = sitk.AffineTransform(3) sitk_tfm.SetMatrix(composed_inv[:3, :3].flatten()) sitk_tfm.SetTranslation(composed_inv[:3, 3]) File <T2 name><suffix>.tfm , reusable by the other modules in the repository."},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#predit-calcule","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"7. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Crown segmentation predicted multi-view network ( DentalModelSeg ) conda run -n shapeaxi Canonical orientation computed geometric, 4 centroids CLI Butterfly / curve computed geometric, Bézier + geodesic flood fill CLI, GPU required Butterfly preview computed geometric, point-in-polygon widget, CPU MG landmarks predicted multi-view network ( ALI_IOS , Lower_MG_*.pth ) conda run -n shapeaxi MGL band computed geometric, spline + Dijkstra widget, CPU Registration computed classic rigid ICP (VTK) CLI No part of FlexReg learns anything: the two networks it calls are pretrained and"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#environnement","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Environment","x":"ensureBooted() ( FlexReg.py:181 ) centralizes the check, run once , at the moment an action needs it — not when a scan is displayed. The probes cost a measured 7.6 s: 2.2 s for conda --version , 0.6 s for the environment test, 4.8 s for importing the install module inside it. Item Value Source conda env shapeaxi , shared with ALI , AREG, ASO , DOCShapeAXI FlexReg.py:880 Python 3.12 (forced: prebuilt pytorch3d wheels only exist for cp310–cp313; shapeaxi ≥ 2.0.2 lifted the grpcio pin that used to force 3.9) same Packages at creation torch>=2.8,<2.13 , ocnn==2.2.1 , SimpleITK install_shapeaxi pyt"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#pieges","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"The CLI requires CUDA Dilation , drawPatch , and the final merge of the arrays all call .cuda() with no CPU fallback. The MGL patch, on the other hand, is computed on the widget side and does without it — hence the while args.type != \"icp_mgl\" that skips the merge for the lower arch. The input file gets overwritten In patch mode, and the automatic segmentation overwrites it too ( overwrite=1 ). A .stl handed to dentalmodelseg is simply deleted outright — that's the whole reason ensureVtkInput() exists. Confirmed bug — vtkICP.__call__ vtkICP.__call__ returns source unchanged instead of the tran"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#litterature","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"10. Literature","x":"The published foundation is AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOral Scans (Hutin, Anchling, Cevidanes et al., Lecture Notes in Computer Science, 2023): crown segmentation by DentalModelSeg , initial alignment by shared centroids, palatal ROI segmented by multi-view 3D shape analysis, then ICP. FlexReg is its \"the operator draws the ROI instead of having it predicted\" counterpart. No paper deals specifically with FlexReg or with the MGL variant — the latter appears to be an in-house development that remains unpublished to date. The figures quoted in the code (4.2 mm vs 1.2 mm on 364 predi"},{"u":"FlexReg/SOURCES.html","p":"FlexReg — sources","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"FlexReg's bibliography: what is deposited in this folder, what remains to be read online, and the neighboring work that does not describe this code. FlexReg is not described by any publication. Verification done: nothing in Crossref, OpenAlex, Europe PMC, arXiv, HAL, nor in the forty-three weeks of the NA-MIC project. The name appears publicly only in the README of SlicerAutomatedDentalTools. The MGL variant (mucogingival line) is not published either, and the figures quoted in its code (4.2 mm vs 1.2 mm on 364 predictions) are not sourced anywhere outside the comments. The published foundatio"},{"u":"FlexReg/SOURCES.html#depose","p":"FlexReg — sources","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2021_Boubolo_FlyBy_CNN_surface_segmentation.xml / .txt Boubolo L, Dumont M, Brosset S, Bianchi J, Ruellas A, Gurgel ML, Massaro C, Aliaga-Del Castillo A, Ioshida M, Yatabe MS, Benavides E, Rios H, Soki F, Neiva G, Paniagua B, Cevidanes L, Styner M, Prieto JC. « FlyBy CNN: a 3D surface segmentation framework ». Proc. SPIE 11596, Medical Imaging 2021: Image Processing , 115962B, 2021. SPIE proceedings, full text on PMC (author manuscript) 10.1117/12.2582205 — PMC7983301 2023_Leclercq_DentalModelSeg_intraoral_surfaces.xml / .txt Leclercq M, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Yatabe"},{"u":"FlexReg/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"FlexReg — sources","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Hutin N, Anchling L, Cevidanes L, Miranda F, Curado D, Gurgel M, Barone S, Bianchi J, Al Turkestani N, Ruellas A, Eason M, Mavani K, Prieto JC, Aliaga A. « AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOralScans ». In: Shape in Medical Imaging — ShapeMI 2023, held jointly with MICCAI 2023, Vancouver, October 8, 2023, Lecture Notes in Computer Science vol. 14350, Springer, 2023, p. 223–235. link.springer.com — paywalled, no author's version deposited (Unpaywall closed , nothing in PMC/HAL) 10.1007/978-3-031-46914-5_18 Ioshida M, Muñoz BA, Rios H, Cevidanes L, Aristizabal JF, Rey "},{"u":"FlexReg/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"FlexReg — sources","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighboring work","x":"Related work that does not describe this code . NA-MIC Project Week 39 (Montreal, 2023), « Automatic Registration Intra Oral Scan » (Hutin, Anchling, Cevidanes, Prieto et al.): project page . Describes AREG_IOS (network on the palate + ICP ), never the hand-drawn patch. NA-MIC Project Week 36 (2022), « ALIIOS — Automatic Landmarks Identification for Intra Oral Scans » (Baquero, Gillot, Cevidanes, Prieto): project page . This is the network that supplies the 13 mucogingival landmarks for FlexReg's MGL path. ALI_IOS has no publication of its own ; this page is its only public description and cit"},{"u":"FlexReg/SOURCES.html#rien-trouve","p":"FlexReg — sources","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Searched for, found nothing","x":"Searched the name « FlexReg » alone and combined (Slicer, Cevidanes, DCBIA, intraoral, registration) in Crossref, OpenAlex, Europe PMC, arXiv, HAL/DUMAS, and on the web; went through the complete listing of NA-MIC Project Weeks 36 → 43; searched for IADR/AADOCR abstracts on IOS registration by the mucogingival line. Nothing. The only public trace of FlexReg is the GitHub repository."},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Affine/rigid registration of T1/T2 CBCT scans by driving ITK-SNAP's greedy binary as a subprocess, with no network in the main path and no dedicated conda environment. Registration of T1/T2 CBCT scans by calling ITK-SNAP's greedy binary as a subprocess. No network is trained or called in the main path: the only network the module uses is for \"distant\" initialization, and that is ALI_CBCT , borrowed as-is."},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#situation","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"Nothing in the repository calls GreedyReg. It is a terminal tool, driven by the operator, and it feeds the input of no other module. Called How When GreedyReg_CLI slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.greedyreg_cli, …) every automatic registration, single-case or batch greedy binary subprocess.run from the CLI two invocations per case (registration then resampling) ALI_CBCT slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.ali_cbct, …) \"Distant Registration\" mode only The widget assembles the parameters, then delegates: the CLI launches greedy , and ALI_CBCT is only called on in Distant mode. Verified The Slicer module n"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#fichiers","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs GreedyReg/GreedyReg.py widget, 1704 lines, about 700 of which are Qt UI (rotation wheel, slice overlay, style sheets) Slicer's Python GreedyReg_Method/Logic.py binary download, CLI parameters, pairing, Kabsch , ALI parsing Slicer's Python GreedyReg_CLI/GreedyReg_CLI.py loops over the pairs, builds and launches the two greedy commands python-real , i.e. Slicer's Python GreedyReg_CLI/bin/<platform>/greedy the executable, absent from the repository , downloaded on demand native Note Important point for anyone taking this over: there is no conda environment . The comment at"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#binaire","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"3. The greedy binary: where it comes from","x":"greedyBinaryPath() looks for GreedyReg_CLI/bin/{linux|mac|windows}/greedy[.exe] . If it is missing, a red banner offers downloadGreedyBinary() ( Logic.py:106 ), which fetches an ITK-SNAP 4.2.2 archive from SourceForge and extracts only bin/greedy from it: Platform Source Extraction Linux itksnap-4.2.2-20241202-Linux-x86_64.tar.gz tarfile , member …/bin/greedy , chmod 755 macOS (arm64) itksnap-4.2.2-20241202-MacOS-arm64.tar.gz same Windows itksnap-4.2.2-20241202-win64-AMD64.exe (NSIS installer) 7-Zip if present, otherwise a silent install followed by uninstall The Windows path is the most fragi"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#modes","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"4. The three modes, and what actually sets them apart","x":"Mode What is computed Who computes it Output Manual Alignment nothing: the operator moves a vtkMRMLLinearTransformNode widget matrix in the scene, used as -ia Automatic Registration affine or rigid, by optimizing an intensity metric computed greedy binary, via the CLI registered .nii.gz + .mat Distant Registration closed-form rigid ( SVD /Kabsch) on 3-5 predicted landmarks predicted computed widget + ALI_CBCT only a matrix, to be refined afterward Vocabulary trap The word \"Distant\" does not mean remote computation. The panel is titled \"Distant Registration (Large Misalignment)\": it refers to t"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#commande","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"5. The greedy command line","x":"buildRegistrationCommand() ( GreedyReg_CLI.py:100 ) always produces exactly this, with no option exposed beyond the metric and the number of degrees of freedom: greedy -d 3 -a \\ -m NMI | -m NCC 4x4x4 | -m SSD \\ -i <T1.nii.gz> <T2.nii.gz> \\ -o <ID>_warp.mat \\ -n 100x100x50x25 \\ -e 0.5 \\ -search 100 10 20 \\ -dof 6|12 \\ -ia <init.mat> \\ [-gm <binarized mask>] Flag Value Meaning (greedy docs) -d 3 fixed image dimension -a fixed affine/rigid mode . Greedy's diffeomorphic mode, which is its default and its reason for existing, is never used here -m NMI index 0 (UI default) normalized mutual informat"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#entrees","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"6. Inputs, pairing, masks, initialization","x":"Trap Pairing. ID_PATTERN = ^([A-Za-z]+\\d+) applied to the file name, uppercased ( GreedyReg_CLI.py:37 ). A01_t1.nii.gz → identifier A01 . A pair exists when the same identifier is found in both the T1 folder and the T2 folder; masks ( .nii / .nii.gz ) and inits ( .mat ) are optional and matched by the same identifier. A file named 01_T1.nii.gz (digits first) or Patient-01.nii.gz is never paired, silently . findBatchPairs() in the widget duplicates this logic for the \"Found N pairs\" label; the comment warns that the two implementations must stay in sync. Mask. Exported from a vtkMRMLSegmentatio"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#batch","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Batch mode","x":"Two independent batches, which do not do the same thing. Automatic batch ( GreedyReg.py:1537 ). The widget does not loop: it launches a single GreedyReg_CLI run over the folders, and it is the CLI that iterates ( main() , GreedyReg_CLI.py:141 ), emitting <filter-progress> / <filter-comment> tags read by Slicer's CLI infrastructure. The output folder is the T2 folder itself . No mask is binarized upstream, no init is passed ( initFolder=\"\" ): the batch always starts from the nudged identity. First case that fails → sys.exit(1) → the whole batch stops. Distant batch ( GreedyReg.py:1606 ). Here, "},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#sorties","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Outputs","x":"File Content Where <ID>_registered.nii.gz T2 resampled into the T1 grid single case: temporary folder; batch: T2 folder <ID>_warp.mat 4×4 greedy affine matrix same <ID>_t2_aligned.nii.gz T2 voxels unchanged, affine corrected T2 folder (distant batch) Transform format, and the conversion that does not exist. <ID>_warp.mat is a plain four-line text file, in greedy's own convention: a 4×4 matrix that maps fixed-volume RAS coordinates to moving-volume RAS coordinates ( [ras(moving);1] = M · [ras(fixed);1] , greedy docs, \"Affine mode\" section). The \"Save Transform Matrix\" button does a plain shutil"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#convention","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"9. Initialization convention: very likely inverted","x":"Manual mode writes the Slicer transform node's matrix, as-is, into <ID>_init.mat ( Logic.py:277 ). This matrix is a MatrixTransformToParent applied to the moving volume: it maps the moving volume's own coordinates to world coordinates, i.e. moving → fixed . -ia expects a matrix of the same nature as the one produced by -o , i.e. fixed → moving . Likely trap, unconfirmed The two are inverses of one another. The manual pre-alignment is therefore very likely handed to greedy backwards (transposed rotation, opposite-sign translation). The rest of the repository knows this convention: FlexReg_CLI.p"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#predit-calcule","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"10. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Manual pre-alignment interaction, no inference widget Centering T2 on T1 computed geometric (image centers) widget Rigid/affine registration computed intensity optimization (NMI/NCC/SSD, LBFGS inside greedy) native binary, CPU Resampling computed linear interpolation native binary Landmarks in distant mode predicted network, reinforcement learning ( ALI_CBCT , DNet ) slicer.cli.run(ali_cbct) , GPU if available Rigid from landmarks computed geometric, SVD/Kabsch widget (numpy) Mask binarization computed > 0 thresholding CLI (nibabel)"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#environnement","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"11. Environment","x":"Item Value Source Interpreter Slicer's Python, for the widget and the CLI ( #!/usr/bin/env python-real ) GreedyReg_CLI.py:1 CLI dependency nibabel alone, installed by ensureNibabelInstalled() after confirmation Logic.py:82 Distant mode dependencies itk , dicom2nifti==2.6.2 , pydicom==3.0.2 , monai ( 1.3.2 if Python ≥ 3.10, otherwise 0.7.0 ) Logic.py:336 torch assumed already present via the extension's NNUNet/PyTorch chain, never installed here comment at Logic.py:331 GPU irrelevant for greedy; useful for ALI ( torch.cuda.empty_cache() on the ALI side) — conda / WSL none , unlike most modules "},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#pieges","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"12. Traps and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug The batch writes its outputs into the T2 input folder. A01_registered.nii.gz and A01_t2_aligned.nii.gz both map to the identifier A01 under ID_PATTERN . On a second run, the {id: path} dictionary keeps only one entry per identifier, the one encountered last in os.listdir : the previous run's result can therefore end up being registered again, non-deterministically. There is no exclusion filter on the produced suffixes. Trap The .mat produced is not a Slicer transform ( §8 ): RAS, fixed→moving direction, plain text format. Trap The manual mode's init is likely inverted ( §9 ). Tra"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#litterature","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"13. Literature","x":"The engine is published, the module is not. greedy : a tool by Paul Yushkevich (PICSL, UPenn), bundled with ITK-SNAP. The usual reference for citing it is Venet, Pati, Yushkevich, Bakas, Accurate and Robust Alignment of Variable-Stained Histologic Images Using a General-Purpose Greedy Diffeomorphic Registration Tool (arXiv:1904.11929) . Documentation: greedy.readthedocs.io , code: pyushkevich/greedy . Note: the paper and the tool are about diffeomorphic registration; GreedyReg only uses the affine/rigid mode ( -a ), which is merely its initialization step. ALI_CBCT : Gillot et al., Automatic l"},{"u":"GreedyReg/SOURCES.html","p":"GreedyReg — sources","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"GreedyReg's bibliography: what is deposited in this folder, what is available to read online, and its neighborhood — work that describes the engine or the borrowed components, never this module itself. The module itself is not published anywhere. No paper, no abstract, no Project Week report is about GreedyReg. What is published is the engine it drives — greedy , the tool by Paul Yushkevich (PICSL, University of Pennsylvania, shipped with ITK-SNAP) — and the network it borrows for \"Distant Registration\" mode, ALI_CBCT. Both of these works are neighborhood material: they describe components, no"},{"u":"GreedyReg/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"GreedyReg — sources","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2019_Venet_greedy_diffeomorphic_registration.pdf Venet L., Pati S., Yushkevich P., Bakas S. Accurate and Robust Alignment of Variable-Stained Histologic Images Using a General-Purpose Greedy Diffeomorphic Registration Tool. arXiv:1904.11929, 2019, 3 p. arXiv preprint arXiv:1904.11929 2023_Gillot_ALI_landmark_identification.xml / .txt Gillot M., Miranda F., Baquero B., Ruellas A., Gurgel M., Al Turkestani N., Anchling L., Hutin N., Biggs E., Yatabe M., Paniagua B., Fillion-Robin J.-C., Allemang D., Bianchi J., Cevidanes L., Prieto J.C. Automatic landmark identificat"},{"u":"GreedyReg/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"GreedyReg — sources","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Yushkevich P.A., Pluta J., Wang H., Wisse L.E.M., Das S., Wolk D. IC-P-174: Fast Automatic Segmentation of Hippocampal Subfields and Medial Temporal Lobe Subregions in 3 Tesla and 7 Tesla T2-Weighted MRI. Alzheimer's & Dementia 12(7S_Part_2):P126-P127, 2016. This is the citation the official greedy website asks to be used ( sites.google.com/view/greedyreg/cite ). Conference abstract, paywalled. alz-journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com 10.1016/j.jalz.2016.06.205 Yushkevich P.A., Piven J., Hazlett H.C., Smith R.G., Ho S., Gee J.C., Gerig G. User-guided 3D active contour segmentat"},{"u":"GreedyReg/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"GreedyReg — sources","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighborhood","x":"None of the works below describe GreedyReg . They describe the engine or the borrowed components. The greedy paper (Venet 2019, retrieved) describes what GreedyReg does not use. It presents a two-stage diffeomorphic registration: an initializing affine step, followed by estimating a deformation field. GreedyReg only ever calls the first one: greedy -a with -dof 6 or -dof 12 , and greedy -rf/-rm for resampling. The deformation field, the \"greedy\" in the tool's name, the diffeomorphic NCC metric — none of that is ever executed. Citing this paper to describe GreedyReg would amount to describing a"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Report from the 43 rd NA-MIC Project Week (Montreal, 2025): the first version of the pipeline, coarse alignment by TorchReg followed by fine registration by Elastix on cropped volumes. Investigators Name Affiliation Country Alban Gaydamour University of Michigan USA Lucia Cevidanes University of Michigan USA Gaelle Leroux CPE Lyon France Juan Prieto University of North Carolina USA Steve Pieper Isomics USA Enzo Tulissi University of Michigan USA"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#description","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Project Description","x":"Accurate registration between CBCT and MRI scans offers complementary visualization of osseous and soft tissue structures around the temporomandibular joint ( TMJ ). This project aims to automate the registration process to support more effective diagnosis of temporomandibular degenerative joint disease."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#objective","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Objective","x":"Develop a robust multi-step registration pipeline to align MRI and CBCT images in the TMJ region. Enhance diagnostic accuracy by integrating soft tissue and osseous imaging modalities. Move toward a fully automated workflow that eliminates manual intervention."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#approach","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Approach and Plan","x":"Perform initial coarse alignment using TorchReg to approximate global positioning of the volumes. Automatically crop both CBCT and MRI volumes to isolate the TMJ region of interest. Apply Elastix-based deformable registration to the cropped images for fine alignment. Evaluate registration quality through both visual assessment and quantitative metrics. Plan to implement automated TMJ region localization to eliminate manual cropping. Note This step 3 plan describes deformable registration by Elastix on the cropped images; the MRI2CBCT page describes, in the current code, a rigid registration (I"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#progress","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Progress and Next Steps","x":"Implemented automated global registration using TorchReg. Established an automated cropping protocol focused on the TMJ region to reduce failure modes in deformable registration. Completed fine registration using Elastix on cropped image pairs. Next steps include developing automated tools to segment MRIs and adding an articular disc label to improve interpretability."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#illustrations","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Illustrations","x":"Four figures accompany the original report, hosted on GitHub (not embedded here so this page stays readable offline): Figure 1: Input MRI (grey) and CBCT (beige) — view the image on GitHub Figure 2: Patient after first registration — view the image on GitHub Figure 3: Cropping of the left TMJ — view the image on GitHub Figure 4: Patient after second registration — view the image on GitHub"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#references","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Background and References","x":"TorchReg Elastix Github — SlicerAutomatedDentalTools"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Report from the 45 th NA-MIC Project Week (Boston, 2026): fixes to the existing pipeline and new manual registration tools designed for clinicians. Investigators Name Affiliation Country Eduardo Duarte Caleme University of North Carolina USA Lucia Cevidanes University of North Carolina USA Paul Dumont University of North Carolina USA Alex Buisson University of North Carolina USA Steve Pieper Isomics USA Gaelle Leroux CPE Lyon France Juan Prieto University of North Carolina USA Alban Gaydamour CPE Lyon France Andras Lasso Queen's University Canada"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#repo","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Github Repository","x":"Slicer"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#funding","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Funding Source(s)","x":"(to be added)"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#description","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Project Description","x":"MRI-to-CBCT registration remains a pressing challenge in medical imaging. Simultaneous visualization of hard and soft tissue structures benefits both clinicians and patients in the diagnosis of temporomandibular degenerative joint disease. Challenges remain in accurately registering these two modalities due to differences in intensity distributions that complicate mutual information optimization, as well as the necessity for initial manual alignment, which can prove unintuitive and challenging for clinicians using current 3D Slicer tools."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#objective","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Objective","x":"Fix issues with the current registration pipeline that impede it from producing appropriate outputs. Implement clinician-friendly manual registration tools. Expand options in both fully automated and clinician-in-the-loop workflows for multi modal registration between MRI and CBCT."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#approach","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Approach and Plan","x":"Present the restored MR2CBCT pipeline and validated results to the community. Explore improved Elastix parameter strategies to increase the capture range beyond the current limits. Present, test, and optimize the new clinician-friendly manual registration tools. Discuss validation framework design."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#progress","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Progress and Next Steps","x":"Fixed a critical bug in AREG_MRI.py where process_images() was never called. Fixed naming convention restrictions throughout the pipeline that proved unintuitive for clinicians. Fixed a mask size mismatch crash in apply_mask.py and a left/right label swap in TMJ crop side detection. Implemented clinician-friendly manual approximation tools, featuring options to freely translate the volume by dragging in slice views, rotate by dragging the circle edge, set a custom center of rotation, and center the MRI at the CBCT center of mass for extremely distant cases. This makes registration significantl"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#illustrations","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Illustrations","x":"The original report includes a screenshot, a video, and four images, hosted on GitHub (not embedded here so this page stays readable offline): Screenshot: Screenshot01.jpg (file attached to the report, not redistributed here) Demo video — video on GitHub Initial images — image on GitHub Center MRI on CBCT function — image on GitHub Clinician manual registration — image on GitHub Elastix Refinement — image on GitHub"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#references","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Background and References","x":"The original report lists no separate bibliography section beyond the Github repository cited above and the link to the Slicer pull request cited in \"Progress and Next Steps\"."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Rigid multimodal MRI → CBCT registration of the TMJ by elastix (Mattes mutual information), at the end of five independent preparation steps; the only network segments the condyle on the CBCT to provide an anchor point — it registers nothing by itself. Rigid multimodal MRI → CBCT registration of the TMJ. Six independent steps, each with its own button and CLI, ending in a rigid elastix registration by mutual information. The module's only network is an nnU-Net that segments the condyle on the CBCT : it takes no part in the registration itself, it provides an anchor point and a crop box."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#situation","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"Nothing in the repository calls MRI2CBCT, and MRI2CBCT calls no other module in the extension. It declares itself dependent on SlicerNNUNet ( MRI2CBCT.py:150 ) but never imports it: it is the nnUNetv2_predict executable that gets launched as a subprocess. Called How When 6 repository MRI2CBCT_* CLIs slicer.cli.run one per button nnUNetv2_predict subprocess.run from the CLI Approximate and TMJ Crop slicer.modules.fiducialregistration slicer.cli.runSync end of automatic approximation The comment at the top of MRI2CBCT_CLI_utils/__init__.py claims that resample_images is consumed by AREG_IOSCBCT "},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#fichiers","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.py widget, 2400 lines: UI, orientation / normalization / resample tables, CLI chaining MRI2CBCT_utils/ one Method class per button: path validation, building the parameter dict ManualApprox_MRI2CBCT.py manual approximation (ported from GreedyReg ), entirely on the widget side MRI2CBCT_CLI_utils/ the actual code: orientation, resample, inversion, normalization, masking, segmentation, elastix Everything runs in Slicer's Python , no conda run anywhere, unlike FlexReg /AREG_IOS. install_function() ( MRI2CBCT.py:78 ) does a pip_install inside Slicer itself and is called "},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#pipeline","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"3. Pipeline order, and what is actually mandatory","x":"[MRI orientation/centering] → [L/R crop] → resample → approximation → [TMJ crop] → registration MRI2CBCT's six CLIs, in the order the pipeline chains them. The only network (nnU-Net) is called as a subprocess from two steps (approx and tmj_crop) to segment the condyle on the CBCT; it registers nothing itself. Six independent buttons: nothing in the code controls the order or the provenance of the files. The only hard constraint is the one on registration. Step Status Why MRI orientation / centering effectively mandatory elastix starts with no initial transform and at a single resolution L/R cr"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#orientation","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"4. MRI orientation and centering","x":"MRI2CBCT_ORIENT_CENTER_MRI.py , modify_image_properties . No resampling: a header rewrite. The voxel array is untouched, only Direction , Spacing[2] and Origin change. SetDirection(new_direction) — a 3×3 matrix built from the checkbox table, flattened row by row by getCheckboxValuesOrient ( MRI2CBCT.py:847 ); default (0,0,-1, 1,0,0, 0,-1,0) ( MRI2CBCT.py:866 ), the value commented \"USE THIS DIRECTION FOR MRI\" at the bottom of the CLI. The table enforces a single ±1 per row and per column: only the 48 signed permutation matrices are reachable. Spacing[2] is replaced by acquisition_z_spacing if "},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#cropping","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"5. Cropping","x":"5.1 L/R crop — LR_crop.py Purely geometric, sitk.RegionOfInterest , half and half. MRI : cut along the Z axis ( size[2]//2 ); lower half → _cropLeft , upper half → _cropRight . CBCT and Seg : cut along the X axis, with the two halves swapped if direction[0,0] < 0 , then the names are deliberately crossed (the left half is written to _cropRight and vice versa) under a # TODO: Check why left is right and why right is left ( LR_crop.py:64 ). Outputs in <output>/CBCT , /MRI , /Seg . The left / right suffixes are then read back by the resample step, with a plain in input_path.lower() . Trap Left an"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#segmentation","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"6. The network: condyle segmentation","x":"condyle_segmentation.py , shared word for word between TMJ crop and Approximate. The module's only network. There is no MRI segmentation at all. Element Value Architecture predicted nnU-Net v2, nnUNetResEncUNetXLPlans plan, 3d_fullres config, fold 0 Dataset Dataset001_myseg Weights checkpoint_final.pth , plus dataset.json and plans.json URL github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools/releases/download/TMJ_CROP_MODEL/… ( MRI2CBCT.py:1249 ) Location <Documents>/SlicerDownloads/MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT_CBCT/ML/Dataset001_myseg/nnUNetTrainer__nnUNetResEncUNetXLPlans__3d_fullres/fold_0/ Execution"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#resampling","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Resampling","x":"MRI2CBCT_RESAMPLE_CBCT_MRI.py + resample.py . Six input folders: MRI/CBCT/Seg × T1/T2. create_csv recursively inventories .nii , .nii.gz , .nrrd , .nrrd.gz , .gipl , .gipl.gz and records the size and spacing of each. Table defaults ( MRI2CBCT.py:543 ): 443 × 443 × 119 slices, spacing 0.3 × 0.3 × 0.3 mm , each row can be disabled via \"Keep the same size / spacing as the input scan\" (the string \"None\" is then passed to the CLI). Input resample_size Interpolation Note MRI the table's value linear iso_spacing=True actually serves as the isMRI flag CBCT forced to \"None\" linear only the spacing chan"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#approximation","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Approximation","x":"Two paths coexist in the same UI section, writing into the same <output>/first_approximation/ folder under the same file names. 8.1 Automatic path The MRI2CBCT_APPROX CLI registers nothing : it produces a pair of points and a rotation, and the widget finishes the job (the CLI has no access to Slicer's modules). approximation() ( approximate.py:77 ), per CBCT/MRI pair (folders, or a single file if both inputs are files — \"Scene Volume\" mode): Rotation derived from the headers ( SVD of the affines), translation derived from pairing the condyle centroid with the MRI's center of mass. The network "},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#normalisation","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"9. Normalization","x":"A 4-column × 2-row table (MRI, CBCT): Normalization Min/Max , Percentile Min/Max . Two sets of defaults, DefaultNorm ( MRI2CBCT.py:1157 ): Preset MRI [min, max, p_low, p_high] CBCT [min, max, p_low, p_high] Default 1 (applied on open) [0, 100, 0, 100] [0, 75, 10, 95] Default 2 [0, 100, 10, 95] [0, 100, 10, 95] The two roles are distinct ( normalize_percentile.py ): the percentiles define the intensity window . np.percentile is computed over the whole array, background included — not on the non-zero voxels. On a CBCT mostly made of air, p10 falls in the background and p95 clips the densest stru"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#recalage","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"10. Registration","x":"MRI2CBCT_REG.py chains 6 steps, each in its own subfolder of folder_general : # Step Output 1 MRI intensity inversion a01_MRI_inv/ 2 MRI normalization a2_MRI_inv_norm/percentile=[…]_norm=[…]/ 3 masking the MRI with the CBCT segmentation a3_MRI_inv_norm_mask/… 4 CBCT normalization b2_CBCT_norm/… 5 masking the CBCT with the same segmentation b3_CBCT_norm_mask_l2/… 6 elastix mri=inv+norm[…]+p[…]_cbct=norm[…]+p[…]+mask/ The CBCT segmentation from §6 serves as a mask twice, for the inverted normalized MRI (moving) as well as for the normalized CBCT (fixed). The raw CBCT is never read by elastix: th"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#predit-calcule","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"11. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where MRI orientation / centering computed header rewrite, matrix chosen by hand CLI L/R crop, resample computed median index, sitk.ResampleImageFilter CLI Side detection, half split computed comparison of centers of mass in world space CLI Condyle segmentation predicted nnU-Net v2 3d_fullres ResEnc XL network nnUNetv2_predict subprocess CBCT point, 400³ TMJ box computed centroid of the predicted mask CLI MRI point, rotation computed center of mass + SVD of the affines CLI Approximation translation computed Slicer's fiducialregistration , 1 point widget Manual approximation operat"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#pieges","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"12. Traps and fragile spots","x":"Confirmed bug install_function() always fails. The dependency list ( MRI2CBCT.py:96 ) contains ('nnunet_version', \"==2.8.0\") ; that package does not exist on PyPI ( AMASSS correctly declares ('nnunetv2','2.8.0') ). check_lib_installed therefore always returns False : an install dialog on every action, a pip_install that raises, errorDisplay , and a False return — a value every caller ignores , so the action proceeds anyway. Confirmed bug Elastix's center of rotation is lost. MatrixRetrieval reads TransformParameters but not the CenterOfRotationPoint that the output parameter map also carries; "},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#environnement","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"13. Environment","x":"Element Value Interpreter Slicer's Python, everywhere ( slicer.cli.run , #!/usr/bin/env python-real ) Conda none GPU optional, automatic CPU fallback in nnU-Net ( condyle_segmentation.py:55 ) torch 2.2.0 , index download.pytorch.org/whl/cu118 ( MRI2CBCT.py:117 ) numpy pinned to 1.26.4 ; numexpr==2.9.0 pydicom pinned to 3.0.2 — downgrading it breaks dicomweb-client , highdicom and every DICOM module in Slicer ( MRI2CBCT.py:83 ) dicom2nifti 2.6.2 Registration itk-elastix (0.19.2 in Slicer 5.12) Other einops , nibabel , pandas , scikit-learn , torchreg , SimpleITK , psutil nnU-Net expected via th"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#litterature","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"14. Literature","x":"The corresponding paper is Novel CBCT-MRI Registration Approach for Enhanced Analysis of Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease , Leroux G. et al. , Clinical Image-Based Procedures (CLIP 2024), LNCS vol. 15196, Springer. Its abstract describes the sequence implemented here: automatic orientation, resampling, MRI inversion, normalization, then rigid registration by mutual information. Two divergences between the paper and the current state of the code: the repository's approximation at the time was the torchreg + NMI path of crop_approximation.py , since replaced by nnU-Net condyle + fidu"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/SOURCES.html","p":"MRI2CBCT — sources","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"What the module owes to the literature, what was retrieved in full text, and what remains paywalled or untraceable. Partially published, and published out of step with the code. Two texts do bear on this module: the method paper (Leroux et al. , CLIP 2024, paywalled) and a clinical case series that documents its use (Caleme et al. , Seminars in Orthodontics 2025, retrieved here). Neither describes the version that runs today: the CLIP 2024 paper describes the torchreg + NMI approximation, since replaced by an nnU-Net segmentation of the condyle followed by a single-point fiducialregistration ;"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"MRI2CBCT — sources","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Archived in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2025_Caleme_MR2CBCT_TMJ_case_series.xml / .txt Caleme E.D., Cevidanes L., Mattos C., Miranda F., Gurgel M., Barone S., Gaydamour A., Tulissi E., Claret J., Leroux G., Moro A., Gonçalves J., Ruellas A., Zupelari-Gonçalves P., Morettin-Zupelari M., Hsu N., Wolford L., Prieto J., Bianchi J. Aligning MRI and CBCT for Advanced TMJ Diagnostics: Case Series Using AI-Powered Registration in Dentistry and Orthodontics. Seminars in Orthodontics, July 2025. Journal article — NIHMS author manuscript , freely readable on PMC (retrieved via the NCBI E-utilities; Europe PMC's ful"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"MRI2CBCT — sources","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"To check online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Leroux G., Mattos C.T., Claret J., Caleme E., Barone S., Gurgel M., Miranda F., Gonçalves J.R., Zupelari-Gonçalves P., Morettin-Zupelari M., Wolford L.M., Hsu N.S., Ruellas A.C.O., Bianchi J., Prieto J.C., Cevidanes L. Novel CBCT-MRI Registration Approach for Enhanced Analysis of Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease. In: Drechsler K. et al. (eds), Clinical Image-Based Procedures (CLIP 2024, MICCAI workshop), Lecture Notes in Computer Science vol. 15196, Springer, 2024, p. 63-72. This is the module's method paper. No deposit, no author version: Unpaywall and OpenAlex"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"MRI2CBCT — sources","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighboring work","x":"Related works that do not describe this module . Al-Saleh 2016 and 2017 (retrieved above): a team from the University of Alberta, unaffiliated with DCBIA. They frame the problem — MRI/CBCT registration of the TMJ, its pitfalls, the state of the art before this module — and serve as a point of comparison, not a description of the code. elastix (Klein 2010, retrieved): describes the registration library, not its use here. The module goes through the Python ITKElastix wrapper ( itk.ElastixRegistrationMethod ) with the default rigid parameter map , a single resolution and no initial transform — a "},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Extraction of TMD clinical indicators from free-text notes by a locally run language model (a fine-tuned BART-large-CNN), then aggregation of the cohort into a single figure panel — text from end to end; no medical image ever enters this module, whatever its name might suggest."},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#situation","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"1. Where it sits in the chain","x":"MedX is self-contained in both directions : nothing in the repository calls it, and it calls no other module in the repository. Grepping for MedX outside MedX/ and MedX_CLI/ turns up only the table of contents in README.md and the root CMakeLists.txt . Two independent paths, two tabs, two CLIs: Path Button Executable called Where Summarization SummarizeButton python -m MedX_Summarize conda run -n summaries Dashboard DashboardButton slicer.modules.medx_dashboard Slicer's Python, via slicer.cli.run The Dashboard path consumes the output of the Summarization path (a folder of .txt files), but not"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#fichiers","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs MedX/MedX.py widget, 1552 lines including ~200 of dark stylesheet; conda management, progress bar, download Slicer's Python MedX_Method/summarize.py , dashboard.py path validation, building the parameters dict same MedX_Method/Method.py , Progress.py abstract base classes (copied from ASO /AREG) same MedX_Summarize.py BART inference loop conda env summaries MedX_CLI_utils/utils.py pdf/docx reading, chunking, model loading same MedX_Dashboard.py dashboard entry point Slicer's Python display_figure.py summary parsing → DataFrame → matplotlib figure (689 lines) same dashbo"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#entrees","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"3.1 Grouping by patient process_notes() ( MedX_Summarize.py:67 ) walks the folder non-recursively ( os.listdir , no recursion), keeps only .pdf , .docx , .txt , and groups by file_name.split(\"_\")[0] . Everything before the first underscore is the patient identifier; several notes from the same patient are concatenated into a single text before inference. The progress bar's count, however, uses Method.search() , which is recursive ( glob.iglob(..., recursive=True) , Method.py:115 ). On a tree with subfolders, the denominator counts more files than the CLI actually processes. Pitfall The progres"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#modele","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"4. The model","x":"4.1 What actually gets loaded load_model_and_tokenizer() ( utils.py:22 ): tokenizer = BartTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_path) model = BartForConditionalGeneration.from_pretrained(model_path) Nothing else. No peft , no quantization , despite peft and bitsandbytes being listed among the packages installed in the environment ( MedX.py:849 ) — those two, plus evaluate and scikit-learn , are leftovers from training. 4.2 The weights URL: https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools/releases/download/MedX/BART.zip ( summarize.py:43 ), 1.51 GB. The archive contains a standard Hugging"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#dashboard","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"5. The algorithmic core — the dashboard","x":"No learning here, just parsing and matplotlib. 5.1 Text summary → DataFrame read_summaries() ( display_figure.py:33 ) only reads files ending in _summary.txt , key = split(\"_\")[0] . initialize_key_value_summary() ( dashboard_utils.py:343 ) creates a dict of 56 keys , all typed str , all initialized to \"\" : patient_age , headache_intensity , disc_displacement , muscle_pain_score , sleep_disorder_type , cpap_used , pain_onset_date … This is the module's target schema , and the only place in the repository where it's written down. update_dictionary() ( display_figure.py:69 ) walks the summary lin"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#sorties","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"6. Post-processing and outputs","x":"Path File Content Summarize <output>/<patient_id>_summary.txt free text, chunks separated by 100 dashes Summarize <log_path> an integer: the number of patients processed Dashboard <output>/dashboard.png the 22×14-inch panel Dashboard <output>/patient_data.csv the 56-column DataFrame Dashboard <output>/dashboard_full_dataframe.csv the same DataFrame , written a second time from generate_dashboard_figure The log_path is <tempDirectory>/process.log ( MedX.py:384 ); the widget watches its mtime and reads the first line ( onCondaProcessUpdate , MedX.py:1125 ). If the Load in 3D Slicer box is checke"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#predit-calcule","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"7. What's predicted vs what's computed","x":"Block Nature Where pdf/docx/txt extraction computed deterministic (PyMuPDF, python-docx) env summaries Chunking computed deterministic, regex + section heuristics env summaries Extraction of the 56 indicators predicted network: fine-tuned BART-large-CNN, free-form generation env summaries , GPU if available key: value parsing + merging computed deterministic Slicer's Python Cohort aggregates computed deterministic, regex + pandas Slicer's Python Figure computed matplotlib Slicer's Python Only one network in the whole module, and it's generative : it's the one deciding the output format. The wh"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#environnement","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"8. Environment","x":"Item Value Source Conda env summaries (specific to MedX, not shapeaxi ) MedX.py:1364 Python 3.12 MedX.py:1388 Packages in the env transformers , torch , pymupdf , python-docx , evaluate , scikit-learn , peft , bitsandbytes , matplotlib MedX.py:849 Inside Slicer (dashboard) numpy<2.0.0 , pandas , matplotlib MedX.py:903 Windows conda via WSL ( CondaSetUpCallWsl ), checks libxrender1 , libgl1 / libgl1-mesa-glx , libglx-mesa0 check_lib_wsl check_cli_script() ( MedX.py:1457 ) tests import MedX_Summarize in the env and, if that fails, pushes Slicer's PYTHONPATH into it ( conda env config vars set )."},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#pieges","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Pitfall The Download button downloads nothing. setup() creates <Documents>/SlicerDownloads/MedX ( MedX.py:394 ), then downloadModel calls DownloadUnzip(directory=SlicerDownloadPath, folder_name=SlicerDownloadPath) . os.path.join with an absolute path makes that path absolute, so out_path = the folder already created, and the if not os.path.exists(out_path) ( MedX.py:770 ) skips the whole block. Result: a \"Folder must have model for\" warning and an empty lineEdit . You have to fetch BART.zip by hand. Confirmed bug The result of the environment check is ignored. check_env = self.onCheckRequireme"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#litterature","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"10. Literature","x":"The corresponding paper is AI-Driven Multimodal TMJ Patient Modeling: From Unstructured Notes to Precision Treatment (2025, Springer LNCS). It describes fine-tuning BART and DeepSeek-R1 on 1,813 annotated segments drawn from 500 TMD records , to extract 56 clinical indicators — the exact number of keys in initialize_key_value_summary() — and feed a patient and cohort dashboard. The paper concludes that BART beats DeepSeek on field-extraction accuracy despite a slightly lower ROUGE score; that's consistent with the module shipping a BART. Gaps between the paper and the repository's code: the pa"},{"u":"MedX/SOURCES.html","p":"MedX — sources","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for MedX: what's deposited in this folder, what's left to consult online, and the neighborhood — work that does not describe this text-summarization module. A tool published once, and only partially : a MICCAI 2025 workshop chapter describes the fine-tuning of the summarization model that MedX ships, but the module in the repository implements only a fraction of what the paper presents — the paper works within a multimodal framework (registered MRI and CBCT), MedX only reads text. The chapter is paywalled and not in PMC: nothing specific to MedX's own work could be retrieved here."},{"u":"MedX/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"MedX — sources","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2019_Lewis_BART_denoising_pretraining.pdf Lewis M, Liu Y, Goyal N, Ghazvininejad M, Mohamed A, Levy O, Stoyanov V, Zettlemoyer L. BART: Denoising Sequence-to-Sequence Pre-training for Natural Language Generation, Translation, and Comprehension. arXiv:1910.13461, Oct 29, 2019 (ACL 2020, pp. 7871–7880). Preprint, arXiv arXiv:1910.13461 2025_Caleme_MRI_CBCT_TMJ_registration.xml / .txt Caleme ED, Cevidanes L, Mattos C, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Barone S, Gaydamour A, Tulissi E, Claret J, Leroux G, Moro A, Gonçalves J, Ruellas A, Morettin Zupelari M, Zupelari-Gonçalves P, Hs"},{"u":"MedX/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"MedX — sources","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Gaydamour A, Tulissi E, Mattos C, Teixeira R, Shin M, Hershey A, Kwon A, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Barone S, Aliaga A, Yatabe M, Zupelari P, Zupelari M, Hanauer D, Hsu N, Pieper S, Caleme E, Bianchi J, Goncalves J, Goncalves D, Wolford L, Ruellas A, Prieto J, Li T, Zhu H, Dai R, Styner M, Al Turkestani N, DaSilva AF, Cevidanes L. AI-Driven Multimodal TMJ Patient Modeling: From Unstructured Notes to Precision Treatment. In: Clinical Image-Based Procedures — 14th International Workshop, CLIP 2025, held in conjunction with MICCAI 2025, Daejeon, South Korea, September 23, 2025. Lect"},{"u":"MedX/SOURCES.html#modeles","p":"MedX — sources","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"Models and repositories","x":"Item Where facebook/bart-large-cnn , the fine-tuned upstream model Hugging Face The module SlicerAutomatedDentalTools"},{"u":"MedX/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"MedX — sources","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"Neighborhood","x":"This work does not describe MedX and has no bearing on the module's automatic summarization code. It is included here to situate the clinical problem and the rest of the system that MedX was meant to be a building block of. Caleme 2025 (file above): the imaging half of the CLIP 2025 paper, by a team that overlaps almost exactly with the chapter's — Gaydamour and Tulissi are co-authors on it. It covers the MRI/CBCT registration that the chapter describes as the multimodal side, and nothing about clinical text or summarization. It is the only article indexed in Europe PMC carrying Gaydamour's na"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Detection and replacement of sensitive entities in text documents (DOCX, TXT, PDF, CSV, XML, ODT) via Microsoft Presidio — the module never touches a medical image: this is not a DICOM anonymiser . Vocabulary trap The name suggests a DICOM anonymiser. It is nothing of the sort: MedicalDataAnonymizer de-identifies text documents via Presidio + spaCy . No DICOM tag ( PatientName , PatientID , UID…) is read or modified, and no defacing is ever applied to an image — the extension as a whole contains no defacing tool at all."},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#pas-dicom","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"0. What the module is not","x":"The name suggests a DICOM anonymiser. It is nothing of the sort, and it can be checked with a single command: $ grep -rin \"dicom\\|pydicom\\|deface\" Medical_Data_Anonymizer_Module/ (no result) $ grep -rlni \"deface\" --include=\"*.py\" --include=\"*.md\" . (no result anywhere in the repository) Direct consequences: no DICOM tag is ever erased or replaced : not PatientName (0010,0010), not PatientID (0010,0020), not PatientBirthDate , not StudyDate , not InstitutionName , not the UIDs, not the private tags , not BurnedInAnnotation ; no defacing : the reconstructible face in a CBCT or scanner is never a"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#situation","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"1. Position in the pipeline","x":"An isolated tool, without a CLI. Nothing calls it, and it calls nothing else in the repository. Everything lives in a single 767-line file, ~350 of which are Qt stylesheets: Medical_Data_Anonymizer_Module.py . Each document is reduced to a text string, analysed by Presidio (spaCy NER + rules), then rebuilt after replacement; the mapping table file_mappings.csv is written separately, in the same output folder. Medical_Data_Anonymizer_ModuleLogic and …Test are empty classes ( pass ): all the logic lives in the widget, hence on Slicer's main thread — the interface freezes during processing, only "},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#entrees","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"2. Inputs: text extraction, format by format","x":"Recursive walk of the input folder ( os.walk ); files starting with ~$ (Word lock files) are ignored. Each file is reduced to a single character string before analysis: Format Extraction What is lost along the way .docx python-docx , \"\\n\".join(p.text for p in doc.paragraphs) tables , headers/footers, footnotes, text boxes, comments, file metadata .txt raw UTF-8 read — .pdf pdfplumber , page.extract_text() ; on exception, a second attempt via extract_tables() images, scanned PDFs (no OCR) → empty text .csv csv.reader , each row flattened by \" \".join(row) the column structure .xml ElementTree + "},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#moteur","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"3. The detection engine","x":"No model in the repository, no weights downloaded by the module itself apart from the spaCy model. Two Presidio engines are instantiated on every click of \"Anonymize Files\" ( …py:493 ), with stderr short-circuited by a SuppressStderr class: analyzer = AnalyzerEngine() # default config anonymizer = AnonymizerEngine() AnalyzerEngine() with no argument uses Presidio's default NLP configuration, that is spaCy en_core_web_lg — the only model that install_dependencies() downloads ( spacy.cli.download , …py:411 ). The two families of Presidio recognizers combine before score filtering: spaCy NER (pre"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#entites-methodes","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"4. Entities and replacement methods","x":"Twelve checkboxes, seven checked by default ( …py:90 ): Presidio entity UI label Default PERSON predicted Names (patients, doctors) ✔ PHONE_NUMBER computed Phone Numbers ✔ EMAIL_ADDRESS computed Email Addresses ✔ DATE_TIME predicted Dates and Times ✔ LOCATION predicted Addresses and Locations ✔ US_SSN computed Social Security Numbers ✔ MEDICAL_LICENSE computed Medical License Numbers ✔ US_DRIVER_LICENSE , CREDIT_CARD , US_BANK_NUMBER , IP_ADDRESS , URL computed — ✘ Four methods, in \"Advanced Options\" ( …py:720 ): Method Implementation Result replace (default) operators = {} → passed as None → "},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#sorties","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"5. Outputs and the mapping table","x":"Written flat into the output folder — the input's subfolder hierarchy is not reproduced. Input Output Note x.docx x_anonymized.docx rebuilt document, one paragraph per line, no styling x.txt x_anonymized.txt — x.pdf x_anonymized.pdf regenerated by reportlab , a single Preformatted block x.csv x_anonymized.csv see below x.xml x_anonymized.xml plain text under an .xml extension : no tags remain, and the code comment admits it (\"XML anonymization is complex\") x.odt x_anonymized.txt extension change The CSV case is the most destructive: the file was read by flattening each row with spaces, then it"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#predit-calcule","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"6. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Text extraction computed deterministic, by format library widget, UI thread Detection of PERSON / LOCATION / DATE_TIME predicted network: spaCy NER en_core_web_lg (CNN + embeddings), via Presidio widget, CPU Detection of numbers (SSN, phone, card, license…) computed regular expressions + checksums and context keywords same Replacement computed deterministic ( replace / redact / SHA-256 / mask) presidio-anonymizer Document reconstruction computed deterministic, by format widget No model is trained or bundled in the repository; en_core_web_lg is pulled by spacy.cli.download at"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#limites","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"7. Limits: what is NOT removed","x":"The most useful section for deciding whether a dataset can be shared. Out of scope by construction All images. DICOM, NIfTI, NRRD, meshes: never read, never modified. No header tag is cleaned, no face is erased ( §0 ). File and folder names. Dupont_Jean_2019.docx becomes Dupont_Jean_2019_anonymized.docx . The patient's name survives intact in the file name, and it is copied verbatim into file_mappings.csv . The input hierarchy, often just as identifying ( .../Patients/Dupont_Jean/ ), is flattened — and if two subfolders each contain a file with the same name, the second silently overwrites the"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#environnement","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"8. Environment","x":"Everything runs in Slicer's Python, installed by the \"Install Dependencies\" button ( …py:392 ), with no confirmation and no pinned versions: presidio-analyzer, presidio-anonymizer, pandas, python-docx, pdfplumber, odfpy, lxml, langdetect, reportlab then spacy.cli.download(\"en_core_web_lg\") Notes: spacy is not in the pip_install list: it is only imported ( …py:409 ). It works because presidio-analyzer pulls it in as a dependency; if that import fails, the whole block falls into the except and shows \"Error installing dependencies.\" A Slicer restart is requested after installation (dialog box), w"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#pieges","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"9. Traps and fragile points","x":"Silent failure returning the original text ( §7.9 ). Flattened output folder : name collisions, overwriting with no message. CSV restructured by line.split() , XML degraded to plain text, ODT renamed to .txt . UUID generated but never applied : the \"mapping table\" allows no controlled re-identification. file.replace(\".docx\", \"_anonymized.docx\") : str.replace replaces every occurrence; a name containing the extension twice produces an unexpected result. The output PDF has no pagination (a single Preformatted block): a long document can overflow the frame or raise a reportlab exception, in which"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#references","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"10. Literature and references","x":"No paper covers this module; nor is there an evaluation (recall, precision) in the repository, on any corpus. The building blocks used are documented: Microsoft Presidio — the detection and anonymisation engine, with the exact list of its predefined recognizers and operators ( replace , redact , hash , mask , encrypt — the latter, reversible with a key, is not offered by the module). spaCy en_core_web_lg — the English NER model used by default. HIPAA Safe Harbor, 45 CFR §164.514(b)(2) — the 18 categories of identifiers to remove. Useful as a reading grid for §7 : file names, medical record ide"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/SOURCES.html","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — sources","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"No article describes this module: only its two technical building blocks ( Presidio , spaCy ) and the clinical text de-identification literature (i2b2/n2c2) make up the relevant context. Unpublished No article, abstract, poster, or Project Week report covers this module, and the repository contains no evaluation — no recall, no precision, on any corpus. The only real references are technical: Presidio (Microsoft) and spaCy , its two building blocks, neither of which has a review article. Add to that a neighboring body of work that must be named, since the page establishes that this module de-i"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — sources","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2025_Alrazihi_presidio_philter_evaluation.xml / .txt Alrazihi L.A., Biswas S., George J. Evaluating the accuracy of automated and semi-automated anonymization tools for unstructured health records. Surgical Neurology International 16:313, 2025. Evaluation of Microsoft Presidio and Philter on 200 unstructured clinical documents: the performance measurement this module does not provide. Review article, full JATS text (Europe PMC, CC BY-NC-SA) 10.25259/SNI_459_2025 — PMCID PMC12477974"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — sources","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"The overviews of the clinical text de-identification campaigns are author manuscripts deposited in PMC: free to read, but outside the freely redistributable subset. Links and DOIs, then, rather than files. Reference Where DOI Uzuner Ö., Luo Y., Szolovits P. Evaluating the state-of-the-art in automatic de-identification. JAMIA 14(5):550-563, 2007. The first i2b2 de-identification challenge. PMC1975792 10.1197/jamia.M2444 Stubbs A., Uzuner Ö. Annotating longitudinal clinical narratives for de-identification: The 2014 i2b2/UTHealth corpus. Journal of Biomedical Informatics 58(Suppl):S20-S29, 2015"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — sources","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"Neighboring work","x":"None of these works describes this module . Microsoft Presidio — the detection and anonymisation engine. No paper: the documentation is the authoritative reference, with the exact list of predefined recognizers and operators ( replace , redact , hash , mask , encrypt — the latter, reversible with a key, is not offered by the module). microsoft.github.io/presidio · github.com/microsoft/presidio spaCy — the English NER en_core_web_lg used by default. No paper either; cite the software record: Honnibal M., Montani I., Van Landeghem S., Boyd A. spaCy: Industrial-strength Natural Language Processin"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"NA-MIC Project Week 45 project page (Boston, 2026): the only primary report found on SurgMovPred, a progress report, not a peer-reviewed publication. Investigator Affiliation Country Alexandre Buisson University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Paul Dumont University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Juan Carlos Prieto University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Lucia Cevidanes University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Steve Pieper Isomics USA Mauro I. Dominguez (I added me if that's ok) Independent Argentina Presentation location ( presenter_location ): not provided in the so"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#github","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Github Repository","x":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#financement","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Funding Source(s)","x":"R01DE024450"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#description","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Project Description","x":"Predicting surgical movements and bone displacement vectors in virtual surgical planning software remains an expert-intensive task, requiring surgeons to simulate osteotomies and manually adjust bone segments. Although statistical shape models and deep learning regression networks have been explored to automate this phase, they output dense deformation fields that lack the geometric interpretability needed to guide clinical or surgical decisions. This project introduces a dedicated 3D Slicer module driven by a Machine Learning Stacking model, trained on a robust dataset of 1,496 patients. The "},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#objectif","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Objective","x":"Embed a pre-trained Stacking ML model into the 3D Slicer environment to leverage multi-patient surgical data. Enable seamless parsing of input files (Excel/CSV) to extract patient-specific features. Generate and display precise bone movement recommendations directly within the Slicer interface based on the model's inference."},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#approche","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Approach and Plan","x":"Develop the input file parser (Excel/CSV) to clean and prepare patient data for the model. Integrate the pre-trained Stacking ML model into a Python-based 3D Slicer scripted module. Design a user-friendly Slicer UI that allows clinicians to load patient files, trigger the prediction, and visualize the recommended bone displacements."},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#progres","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Progress and Next Steps","x":"The core Stacking ML model has been successfully trained and validated using a dataset of 1,496 patient cases. During the project week we'll build an interactive UI and backend pipeline within 3D Slicer to handle file inputs and run the model's prediction pipeline. Also, we'll verify the accuracy of the outputs within the Slicer environment and explore intuitive ways to display the predicted movements to the user."},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#illustrations","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Illustrations","x":"Pitfall This project page's illustrations are hosted on GitHub ( github.com/user-attachments/... ) and are therefore not reproducible offline; they are left here as links rather than as broken images. Two of the three screenshots actually show other modules (IOS-CBCT Registration, VFACE ), not SurgMovPred itself — the same copy-paste drift noted in SurgMovPred.py (see the tool page, \"Position in the pipeline\" section). Screenshot (1728×695, alt: \"Screenshot 2026-06-25 at 10 29 58 AM\") — overview of the tool. Tool Demo: demo video . IOS-CBCT Registration tool: screenshot (670×794, alt: \"Screens"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#references","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Background and References","x":"Github link : https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools This work was supported by the National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research (NIDCR) of the National Institutes of Health under Award Number R01DE024450 ."},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SOURCES.html","p":"SurgMovPred — sources","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"SurgMovPred bibliography: no article describes the tool, only a NA-MIC project page constitutes a primary source. No article describes SurgMovPred. But the page can be filled out on one point: there is a NA-MIC Project Week 45 project page (Boston, 2026) devoted exactly to this module — New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery — retrieved in this folder. It names the authors (Alexandre Buisson, Paul Dumont, Juan Carlos Prieto, Lucia Cevidanes, Steve Pieper, UNC Chapel Hill; Mauro I. Dominguez), confirms the model ( Machine Learning Stacking model , Excel/CSV i"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"SurgMovPred — sources","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.md Buisson A, Dumont P, Prieto JC, Cevidanes L, Pieper S, Dominguez MI. \"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery\". Project page, NA-MIC Project Week 45, Boston, 2026. project report — only primary source project page 2023_Cheng_orthognathic_plan_deep_learning.xml / .txt Cheng M, Zhang X, Wang J, Yang Y, Li M, Zhao H, Huang J, Zhang C, Qian D, Yu H. \"Prediction of orthognathic surgery plan from 3D cephalometric analysis via deep learning\". BMC Oral Health , 2023, 23(1), 161. neighboring 10.1"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"SurgMovPred — sources","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Ma Q, Kobayashi E, Fan B, Hara K, Nakagawa K, Masamune K, Sakuma I, Suenaga H. \"Machine-learning-based approach for predicting postoperative skeletal changes for orthognathic surgical planning\". Int J Med Robot Comput Assist Surg , 2022, 18(3), e2379. publisher — paywalled ( oa_status: closed ) 10.1002/rcs.2379 de Oliveira PHJ, Li T, Li H, Gonçalves JR, Santos-Pinto A, Gandini Junior LG, et al. , Cevidanes L, Bianchi J. \"Artificial intelligence as a prediction tool for orthognathic surgery assessment\". Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research , 2024, 27(5), 785-794. PMC11789623"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"SurgMovPred — sources","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Neighboring resources","x":"These works do not describe the SurgMovPred code nor the 112 model packages shipped with it. The exact problem, another team. Ma 2022 ( IJMRCAS ) predicts post-operative skeletal changes from pre-operative measurements, using tabular machine learning. This is the closest formulation to SurgMovPred's, but nothing links this work to the embedded weights. Same goal, deep learning. Cheng 2023 ( BMC Oral Health ) predicts the surgical plan from a 3D cephalometric analysis; Kim 2025 ( Nature Communications ) goes further and synthesizes the post-operative lateral cephalogram via a graph network + di"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"A spreadsheet of cephalometric measurements goes in, a spreadsheet of predicted bone displacements comes out: 112 stacked scikit-learn regressors, one per target, no neural network, no image, and no mesh. Prediction of the surgical movements of an orthognathic surgery from a spreadsheet of cephalometric measurements . No neural network, no image, no mesh: 112 stacked scikit-learn regressors, one per value to predict, applied row by row to an Excel/CSV file."},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#situation","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Position in the pipeline","x":"Isolated tool. Nothing in the repository calls it, and it calls nothing in the repository: it reads no volume, no mesh, no landmarks. Its input is a folder of spreadsheets, its output another spreadsheet. The complete pipeline: deterministic cleanup, then 112 independent regressions, one per target. No return trip into Slicer's 3D scene. Called How SurgMovPred_CLI slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.surgmovpred_cli, …) ( SurgMovPred.py:842 ) The module isn't wired into the MRML scene: no vtkMRMLNode is created, read or transformed. It's a launch interface sitting on top of a pandas script. Pitfall C"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#fichiers","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.py widget: 3 folder selectors, downloading of models and test files, dependency installation, ~400 lines of QSS Slicer's Python SurgMovPred_CLI/SurgMovPred_CLI.py all of the processing: reading, column-name cleanup, predict , writing Slicer's Python (scripted CLI) SurgMovPred/Resources/UI/SurgMovPred.ui 3 fields ( inputFolderLineEdit , modelFolderLineEdit , outputFolderLineEdit ) + 4 buttons — Only three positional parameters ( inputFolder , modelPath , outputFolder , SurgMovPred_CLI.xml ): nothing is configurable — no threshold, no target select"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#entree","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"3. Input and preprocessing","x":"Accepted format. load_data_from_directory() ( SurgMovPred_CLI.py:141 ) collects every *.csv , *.xlsx , *.ods file in the folder (extension in lowercase and uppercase), reads them ( read_csv , read_excel , read_excel(engine='odf') ) and concatenates them ( pd.concat(..., ignore_index=True) ). No recursion into subfolders. For an Excel workbook, only the first sheet is read — the official test file contains seven ( all_data , demographics , Invivo_ceph , surgical_mov , dolp_ceph_T0 , dolp_ceph_T1 , dolp_ceph_total ), and it's all_data that sits in first position. Column-name normalization , clea"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#modeles","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"4. The models","x":"The weights are not in the repository . onDownloadDefaultModel() ( SurgMovPred.py:782 ) downloads and unzips: https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools/releases/download/SurgMovPred/all_models.zip -> Documents/SlicerDownloads/SurgMovPred/Models/all_models/ 706 MB , 112 folders, one stacking_package.pkl of ~13 MB each. load_all_model_packages() does a recursive Path.glob(\"**/stacking_package.pkl\") and indexes by package['target_name'] : pointing the selector at all_models/ loads all 112 models, pointing at a subfolder loads only one. The CLI's help text mentions saved_models/"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#traitement","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"5. The core processing","x":"predict_all_targets() ( SurgMovPred_CLI.py:194 ), in full: for target_name, pack in packages.items(): X_target = df_cleaned[[feature_source[f] for f in pack['features_names']]] X_target.columns = pack['features_names'] X_scaled = pack['scaler'].transform(X_target) predictions_by_target[target_name] = pack['model'].predict( pd.DataFrame(X_scaled, columns=..., index=df_cleaned.index)) No post-correction, no bounding, no inter-target consistency, no confidence interval — the GaussianProcessRegressor can produce a standard deviation, it isn't asked to. Each target is handled independently, with a "},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#sorties","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"6. Outputs","x":"save_results() writes two files, same content, into the output folder: File Content predictions_outputs.xlsx one row per input patient, IDPatient column then one column per predicted target predictions_outputs.csv same index=True in both cases: the pandas index (0, 1, 2…) becomes an unnamed column. Column names are the packages' raw target_name , so with / and + ( Mx_ALL_APoint_P-/A+_Pred ). Pitfall There is no reapplication of the result whatsoever. Nothing builds a matrix, moves a mesh, creates a Slicer node, or opens a view: the prediction stops at the spreadsheet. The question \"how is the "},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#predit-vs-calcule","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"7. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Column-name cleanup, ID detection computed deterministic (regex) CLI Standardization computed learned StandardScaler CLI Prediction of the 112 displacements predicted stacking ElasticNetCV + LightGBM + GP, meta LinearRegression CLI, CPU Consolidation into a single segment transform nonexistent — The dashed boxes do not exist in the code: nothing guarantees a consistent rigid-body movement for an entire bone segment. Pitfall The maxilla's 10 points are predicted by 40 independent models. Nothing guarantees that the predicted displacements are compatible with a rigid-body move"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#environnement","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Environment","x":"Everything runs inside Slicer's Python. check_dependencies() ( SurgMovPred.py:156 ) is called on every click of Apply: Item Value Packages pandas , joblib , openpyxl , scikit-learn , lightgbm Missing from the list odfpy — yet needed by read_excel(engine='odf') for the advertised .ods files numpy slicer.util.pip_install(\"numpy==2.4.0\") unconditional , on every Apply macOS ensure_mac_openmp() downloads libomp.dylib from mac.r-project.org and drops it into Slicer's cli-modules folder, then loads it with RTLD_GLOBAL — LightGBM needs it Compilation CC=gcc / CXX=g++ forced during pip_install , resto"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#pieges","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"pip_install(\"numpy==2.4.0\") on every run. Replacing numpy under a live Slicer session is exactly what breaks scipy's import elsewhere in the extension (Slicer's scipy is compiled against its numpy). By far the most dangerous point in the module, and it's triggered by the main button. Inoperative \"test files\" button : DownloadTestFiles() ( SurgMovPred.py:763 ) does download TestFiles.zip , but only fills in the input/output fields if os.path.exists(.../V_FACE/DefaultList) — a copy-paste leftover from the VFACE module. Without VFACE 's test data, the archive is downloaded and then ignored. .ods "},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#litterature","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"10. Literature","x":"No paper covers SurgMovPred. Neither the repository, nor the release, nor the README cites any reference, and a search on the module's name returns nothing. The module isn't even listed in the extension's README. The published context, not to be confused with a description of this code: Machine-learning-based approach for predicting postoperative skeletal changes for orthognathic surgical planning (PubMed 35132764) — the problem posed is the same (predicting post-operative skeletal displacements from pre-operative measurements), but nothing indicates this is the work behind these weights. Oliv"},{"u":"VFACE/2023_Hutin_NAMIC_PW39_AQ3DC.html","p":"Automatic Quantification 3D Components (AQ3DC)","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"NA-MIC Project Week 39 project report (Montreal, 2023) describing AQ3DC, the Slicer extension for automatic measurement whose logic ( functionaq3dc.py ) VFACE reuses to compute distances and angles. Field Value Project title Automatic Quantification 3D Components Category Quantification and Computation Presentation In person Project link github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/Q3DCExtension Key investigators Nathan Hutin (University of Michigan) · Luc Anchling (University of Michigan, France) · Baptiste Baquero (University of Michigan, France) · Maxime Gillot (University of Michigan, France) · Lucia Cevidan"},{"u":"VFACE/2023_Hutin_NAMIC_PW39_AQ3DC.html#description","p":"Automatic Quantification 3D Components (AQ3DC)","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Project Description","x":"The Automatic Quantification 3D Components(AQ3DC) was developed during Namic-project 37 is now available in Slicer Q3DC extension. The Automatic Quantification 3D Components(AQ3DC) aims to provide a user-friendly automated tool that decrease user time for extraction of quantitative image analysis features. AQ3DC is a Slicer extension to automatically compute lists of measurements selected by users for a single case or a whole study sample, at one or more time points. The current implementation is aimed at automatic computation of 3D components like distances (AP, RL and SI) between points, poi"},{"u":"VFACE/2023_Hutin_NAMIC_PW39_AQ3DC.html#objectives","p":"Automatic Quantification 3D Components (AQ3DC)","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Objectives","x":"Renaming function and variable Create Documentation for developer Find bugs"},{"u":"VFACE/2023_Hutin_NAMIC_PW39_AQ3DC.html#progress","p":"Automatic Quantification 3D Components (AQ3DC)","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Progress and Next Steps","x":"Progress Resolve few issues Ranaming most of the function Next Steps Improve readability of the code Create Documentation for developer"},{"u":"VFACE/2023_Hutin_NAMIC_PW39_AQ3DC.html#illustrations","p":"Automatic Quantification 3D Components (AQ3DC)","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Illustrations","x":"The six original screenshots are hosted on GitHub ( user-images.githubusercontent.com ); they are not brought over here to keep the site readable offline, but the links are kept. 1. Slicer Interface Screenshot from 2022-06-30 18-31-37 (external link) 2. List of measurements exported. Screenshot from 2022-06-30 18-29-01 (external link) 3. Results of the computation for all the list of measurement for a sample of patient. Screenshot from 2022-06-30 19-01-23 (external link) 4. Skeletal measurements signs meaning. skeletal_measurement (external link) 5. Linear measurements signs meaning. linear_me"},{"u":"VFACE/SOURCES.html","p":"VFACE — sources","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for VFACE: what is published, what is left to check online, and the neighboring work that sheds light on the problem without describing this code. VFACE is partially published , and what is published does not describe what the module actually runs. The SPIE Medical Imaging 2026 proceedings paper (doi:10.1117/12.3087010) describes the same upstream chain — standardized preprocessing, mirroring, voxel registration, extraction of anatomical measurements — but stops at an unsupervised clustering step (dimensionality reduction PCA / t-SNE / UMAP, then Spectral Clustering with kNN or RB"},{"u":"VFACE/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"VFACE — sources","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Archived in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2026_Peng_3D_facial_asymmetry_review.xml / .txt Peng J, Qu W, Zhang S, Lin Y. \"Automated assessment of 3D facial asymmetry: a systematic review\". European Journal of Orthodontics , 2026, 48(3), art. cjag012. systematic review (neighboring work) 10.1093/ejo/cjag012 — PMC13207581 2023_Gillot_ALI_landmark_identification.xml / .txt Gillot M, Miranda F, Baquero B, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Anchling L, Hutin N, Biggs E, Yatabe M, Paniagua B, Fillion-Robin JC, Allemang D, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC. \"Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam comput"},{"u":"VFACE/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"VFACE — sources","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"To check online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Sanna A, Ng J, Ruellas A, Aliaga A, Gaydamour A, Tulissi E, Huang Y, Prieto JC, Al Turkestani N, Yatabe M, Bianchi J, Pieper S, Cevidanes L, Li T. \"Automated classification of skeletal facial asymmetry in CBCT using a reproducible 3D Slicer workflow for patient-specific decision support\". In: Gimi BS (ed.), Medical Imaging 2026: Clinical and Biomedical Imaging , Proc. SPIE vol. 13929, art. 139292J. SPIE, 2026. Conference held in Vancouver (Canada), February 15-19, 2026; ISBN 9781510697959. SPIE Digital Library — paywalled ( oa_status: closed at Unpaywall and OpenAlex); the "},{"u":"VFACE/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"VFACE — sources","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Neighboring work","x":"These works do not describe VFACE's code . They shed light on the problem. The mirror method, within this same group. AlHadidi 2011 (DMFR) directly compares the two strategies: mirroring across a mid-sagittal plane defined by landmarks, versus mirroring across an arbitrary plane followed by a voxel registration on the cranial base. Cevidanes 2011 (OOOOE) validates both on simulated data. AlHadidi 2012 adds SPHARM-PDM, Paniagua 2011 adds tensor-based morphometry. It is the second strategy — mirror then voxel registration — that VFACE implements ( AutoMatrix applies Matrix_mirror.tfm , AREG-CBCT"},{"u":"VFACE/SOURCES.html#complement","p":"VFACE — sources","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Addendum: three upstream texts retrieved afterward","x":"NIH author manuscripts return an empty fullTextXML at Europe PMC, which is what had them classified as \"to check\". The efetch route serves them: curl -sL \"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=pmc&id=<number without PMC>\" Three of them are now in this folder, in .xml (JATS) and .txt : File Reference PMCID 2011_Cevidanes_3D_quantification_mandibular_asymmetry Cevidanes L. et al. , Oral Surg Oral Med Oral Pathol Oral Radiol Endod , 2011 PMC3095695 2011_Paniagua_mandibular_asymmetry_tensor_morphometry Paniagua B. et al. , Proc IEEE ISBI , 2011 PMC3892912 2020_Bianchi_3DSlic"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"An orchestrator that learns and segments nothing itself: it chains six other modules to register a CBCT onto its own mirror image, measures the gap, and classifies skeletal facial asymmetry with three LightGBM models. The flow as the lab presents it: ASO orients the scan, ALI_CBCT places the landmarks, AutoMatrix builds the reflection, BatchDentalSeg segments, AReg registers the patient onto their mirror, and the classification comes out at the end. The modules the code actually calls are listed in the rest of this page — they do not match this diagram exactly. Lab diagram — high-resolution PN"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#situation","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"Nothing in the repository calls VFACE: CMakeLists.txt:54 adds it as a terminal module. It does, however, call eight processes, seven of which are other modules of the extension. Everything is built in a single pass by CreateListProcess() ( createlistprocess.py:117 ), which returns a list of dictionaries {Process, Parameter, Module, Display, Review*} ; the widget unwinds it one item at a time. Called Slicer module Role in VFACE MRI2CBCT resample mri2cbct_resample_cbct_mri 0.3 mm isotropic resampling + recentering ASO PRE pre_aso_cbct geometric centering, writes a .tfm ALI-CBCT ali_cbct landmark"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#fichiers","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Lines Role VFACE/VFACE.py 2977 widget: UI, review pauses, batch navigation, sequential execution, cleanup createlistprocess.py 2636 the pipeline plan , plus derive_landmarks , reorganizeStat , postprocess , heatmaps functionaq3dc.py 2484 vendored copy of the AQ3DC module; only AQ3DCLogic is used segmentation_logic.py 1051 drives nnU-Net via SlicerNNUNetLib , VTK exports Measure.py 956 the formulas : Distance , Angle , Diff2Measure review_steps.py 249 catalogue of pauses, and restrictStepToPatients _batch_worker.py 235 one subprocess per mesh pair for the heatmap VFACE_CLI/VFACE_CLI.py 291"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#entrees","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"Input : a folder of T1 CBCTs, one per patient ( .nii , .nii.gz , .nrrd , .nrrd.gz , .gipl , .gipl.gz ). No segmentation, no landmark: VFACE starts from the raw volume. checkInputFolder() ( VFACE.py:1221 ) refuses to start on an empty folder. Patient identity : patientIdFromFileName ( functionaq3dc.py:1369 ) cuts the name on _Scan , _Or , _MAND , _MD , _MAX , _MX , _CB , _lm , _T2 , _T1 , _Cl , . . The same chain is duplicated three times in createlistprocess (marked TIMEPOINT-SUFFIX ): only _T1 / _T2 are recognized , a _T3 silently breaks the pairing. Resampling : spacing [0.3, 0.3, 0.3] , res"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#modeles","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"4. The models called","x":"VFACE contains only one model of its own, the LightGBMs. The others belong to the upstream modules, downloaded by DownloadAllFiles() ( VFACE.py:950 ). Model Origin Nature ALI-CBCT release v0.1-v2.0_models (7 zips: Cranial Base, Lower Bones 1/2, Upper Bones v2, teeth) RL agents, DNet = monai DenseNet ( brain.py:48 ) AMASSS AMASSS_CBCT/AMASSS_Models.zip 3D UNet, label map output DentalSegmentator Dataset111_453CT_v100.zip (SlicerDentalSegmentator v1.0.0-alpha) nnU-Net 3d_fullres, fold 0 sym_asymm.txt , mand_asym.txt , max_asym.txt release VFACE/V_FACE_Models.zip LightGBM , joblib pickles 4.1 ALI"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#mesures","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"5. The algorithmic core — the measurements","x":"5.1 The eight measurement types All instantiated by AQ3DCLogic.createMeasurement ( functionaq3dc.py:1995 ), from the Type of measurement column of the xlsx — matched by exact string equality . Positions are in LPS ( x = left, y = posterior, z = superior). Type (exact string) Inputs Formula ( Measure.py ) Distance between 2 points T1 / T2 P1, P2 at the same time point d = p2 − p1 ; (lr, ap, si) = (−dx, −dy, dz) ; 3D = ‖d‖ ; rounded to 3 decimals Distance between 2 points T1 T2 P1 @T1, P2 @T2 identical, but crossed between the two point sets: this is the asymmetry measurement Distance point line"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#classifieur","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"6. The classifier","x":"VFACE_CLI loads three joblib pickles ( VFACE_CLI.py:62 ). These are lightgbm.sklearn.LGBMClassifier models — not a network, not a hand-written rule tree. Verified The hyperparameters and the number of trees kept below were confirmed by unpickling the three .txt files. Model Hyperparameters Features Trees kept sym_asymm.txt gbdt , objective=binary , num_leaves=31 , learning_rate=0.03 , n_estimators=5000 , class_weight=balanced , random_state=42 , no regularization 14 459 mand_asym.txt same 7 3 max_asym.txt same 7 11 The 5000 estimators announced are never reached (early stopping during training"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#revue","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"7. Manual review and batch navigation","x":"7.1 The pauses Added by commit 2609bc4 . A step in the plan can carry Review* keys; the widget stops at the end of the step, loads the results into the scene, and waits. The catalogue lives in review_steps.py , kept separate from the pipeline so the panel can list the pauses before CreateListProcess has even created a single folder. Id Label Nature Available if t1_landmarks_orientation_max maxillary orientation landmarks editable Full pipeline t1_oriented_max maxillary orientation view only Full pipeline t1_landmarks_orientation_cb cranial base orientation landmarks editable Full pipeline t1_o"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#sorties","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Post-processing and outputs","x":"Folder under OutputFolder Contents Survives cleanup T1 Resample/CBCT 0.3 mm centered volumes no Centered T1 Scans/{CB,MAX} centered volumes + centering .tfm no Oriented T1 Scans/{CB,MAX} <pat>_{CB,MAX}_Or.nii.gz , _transform.tfm , _lm_*.mrk.json no T1 Masks <base>_seg_{CB,MAND,MAX}MASK.nii.gz no T2_Scan/{CB,MAX} or T2 Centered the mirror (or the real T2 in longitudinal mode) no T2_Masks mirrored masks, unused (CMFReg) no Registered Scan/<region>/<pat>_OutReg/ registered scan + _Reg_matrix.tfm no T1 Landmarks/{CB,MAX} ALI .mrk.json and derivatives no Mirrored Landmarks/{CB,MAX} _mir suffix no M"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#predit-calcule","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"9. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Resampling, centering computed geometric MRI2CBCT / ASO Landmarks (orientation and measurements) predicted RL agents, monai DenseNet ALI-CBCT Orientation onto gold standard computed rigid VTK ICP on landmarks ASO SEMI Bone masks predicted 3D UNet AMASSS AMASSS Mirror computed fixed matrix diag(-1,1,1) AutoMatrix Registration computed rigid Elastix, MI, 3 resolutions AREG MAX landmarks computed rigid composition of two .tfm Python, widget Bone surfaces predicted nnU-Net DentalSegmentator Python, widget, CUDA Linear / angular measurements computed geometric, numpy Python, widg"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#pieges","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"10. Traps and fragile spots","x":"derive_landmarks cannot work outside \"Full pipeline\". In \"File already Oriented\" / \"already Registered\" mode, SplitOriented ( createlistprocess.py:1877 ) only copies the .nii* / .nrrd* files into Oriented T1 Scans/ , not the .tfm files . The \"Deriving T1 Landmarks (MAX)\" step then logs missing an orientation transform for every patient, T1 Landmarks/MAX stays empty, so does Measurements_MAX.xlsx , and the classification is missing five features out of fourteen. CMFReg does not change the registration , it only adds a T2_Masks folder that nothing reads (§1). Hardcoded _T1 / _T2 suffixes , three"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#litterature","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"11. Literature","x":"The corresponding published work is \"Automated classification of skeletal facial asymmetry in CBCT using a reproducible 3D Slicer workflow for patient-specific decision support\" , SPIE Medical Imaging 2026, vol. 13929, art. 139292J, doi:10.1117/12.3087010 ( SPIE page ). The public abstract describes 170 Class III patients, and the same upstream chain: standardized preprocessing, mirroring , voxel registration, extraction of anatomical measurements. Code / paper divergence The code diverges from the paper at the last step. The abstract describes an unsupervised approach — dimensionality reducti"},{"u":"glossary.html","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"47 terms the tool pages assume you already know. Defined the way they're used in this repository , not in the abstract. Reference frames and geometry · Registration · Networks and learning · Text and language models · Anatomy and modalities"},{"u":"glossary.html#reperes-et-geometrie","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Reference frames and geometry","x":"direction 3×3 matrix giving the orientation of the voxel grid's axes in physical space. Together with the origin and the spacing, it forms the index-to-millimetre transform. isosurface Surface connecting every point of a volume that shares the same intensity value. AREG_IOSCBCT extracts one at threshold 400 to give its ICP a geometric target. LPS Left–Posterior–Superior: the three axes point toward the patient's left, the back, and up. This is the frame used by DICOM, ITK, and this repository's .vtk files. ALI_CBCT and ALI_IOS both write \"coordinateSystem\": \"LPS\" in their JSON. mesh A surface "},{"u":"glossary.html#recalage","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Registration","x":"affine 12 degrees of freedom: rigid, plus scale and shear. Straight lines stay straight and parallel; distances don't. elastix Image-registration library driven by parameter files. AREG_CBCT and MRI2CBCT call it through itk-elastix for their voxel-to-voxel registration. ICP Iterative Closest Point : for each moving point, find its nearest neighbour in the target, estimate the rigid transform that brings them closer, and repeat. Converges well only if the starting point is already close — hence the systematic landmark pre-alignment. Kabsch The algorithm that solves Procrustes: recenter both poi"},{"u":"glossary.html#reseaux-et-apprentissage","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Networks and learning","x":"reinforcement-learning agents ALI_CBCT doesn't segment: it moves agents through the volume step by step, each one learning to get closer to its landmark. The search stops when an agent starts oscillating, then resumes at a finer resolution. checkpoint A file of trained weights. Reading its hyperparameters is often the only reliable way to know what a model actually does — several code/paper discrepancies in this repository were established that way. cross-encoder A model that takes the query and the document together and outputs a relevance score, unlike embeddings, which encode them separatel"},{"u":"glossary.html#texte-et-modeles-de-langage","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Text and language models","x":"BART Pretrained encoder–decoder model for text generation. MedX uses a large-cnn variant fine-tuned for summarization. GGUF llama.cpp's weight format, designed for CPU inference and quantisation. This is the format both Ollama and CNE require. llama.cpp C++ inference engine for language models, optimised for CPU. Runs under Ollama, and directly under CNE. Ollama Local server that downloads, caches, and serves language models over an HTTP API on port 11434. Decides GPU usage on its own — hence the trap with the snap install. Presidio Microsoft library for detecting and replacing personal data i"},{"u":"glossary.html#anatomie-et-modalites","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Anatomy and modalities","x":"arch The full set of teeth on one jaw. Upper and lower are treated as two separate inputs by nearly every module. CBCT Cone Beam Computed Tomography : a cone-beam scanner, producing a 3D volume. Low dose, high spatial resolution, but intensities are often not calibrated in Hounsfield units — which makes any hardcoded threshold fragile. condyle The head of the mandible, articulating in the temporal fossa. A central structure in asymmetry analyses. FOV Field Of View : the physical extent covered by the acquisition. A \"small FOV\" captures only one region, which changes which models apply. IOS Int"},{"u":"guide/ALI.html","p":"ALI — place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"ALI drops anatomical landmarks onto your scans for you. It works just as well on a CBCT (a volume) as on an intraoral scan (a surface), using two different engines behind a single window. On video ALICBCT 3 min 49 · DCBIA Videos channel ALIIOS 2 min 52 · DCBIA Videos channel"},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#quand","p":"ALI — place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Any time you need landmarks and placing them by hand would take too long: a dozen points on one patient is manageable; on a series of forty, it isn't anymore. ALI also supplies the landmarks for several other modules — if you run an automatic orientation or registration, it may already be running behind the scenes without you having to open it. Two engines, one window The module picks the right engine on its own based on your data: CBCT for a volume, IOS for an intraoral scan. All you do is say what type your files are and which points you want."},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#besoin","p":"ALI — place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of scans — CBCTs as .nii.gz , or intraoral scans as .vtk The group of points you want, checked in the list The trained models, downloaded once What comes out One landmark file per scan and per group, in Slicer's format ( .mrk.json ) Opens straight into the scene, points draggable with the mouse Your original scans are left untouched CBCT — a volume Intraoral scan — a surface Basion Ba Sella S Nasion N Right porion RPo Left porion LPo Right frontozygomatic RFZyg Left frontozygomatic LFZyg C2 vertebra C2 Place the landmarks again landmarks placed A CBCT and an intraoral "},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#marche","p":"ALI — place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Download the models. The \"Download latest models\" button fetches the trained weights. Only needed once, but essential: without them the module has nothing to predict from. Choose the data type. CBCT or intraoral scan. This decides which engine gets used. Point it to the input folder. ALI processes the whole folder in one go; there's no need to run it patient by patient. Check the points you want. They're grouped by region. Only check what you actually need: every point adds computing time. Set an output folder , then launch. A progress bar tracks the patients as they go through. The landmarks "},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#temps","p":"ALI — place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How long it takes","x":"Expect around 15 seconds per point per patient with a graphics card , and up to a minute without one . Over ten points and thirty patients, that gap stops being trivial: roughly an hour versus five. The module runs fine without a GPU, but plan for an overnight run on a large series."},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#coince","p":"ALI — place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"\"Nothing comes out, or the points look wrong\" First check that the models were actually downloaded, then that the data type you checked matches your files. A CBCT processed as an intraoral scan won't produce anything usable. Intraoral scans: teeth need to be identified first On an intraoral scan, the engine aims its measurements tooth by tooth. If your file doesn't have crown identification yet, ALI runs it automatically along the way — expect extra time on the first attempt. DICOM import Use .nii.gz files instead. The module's DICOM path doesn't work in automatic mode. Disk space The temporar"},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#apres","p":"ALI — place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What's next","x":"The landmarks you get are a starting point, not ground truth: open them in Slicer and check them before using them for a measurement or a registration. They can be dragged with the mouse like any other markup."},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#lire-plus","p":"ALI — place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"On ALI itself Gillot et al., Orthod Craniofac Res 2023 — Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography . The paper behind the CBCT path: agents that walk through the volume to the landmark. Reports the measured error, landmark by landmark. Baquero et al., CLIP 2022 (LNCS 13746) — Automatic Landmark Identification on IntraOralScans . The paper behind the mesh path: the surface is photographed from several viewpoints, and each pixel votes for a landmark. Around the method Alansary et al., Medical Image Analysis 2019 — Evaluating reinforcement learning agents for anatomical l"},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html","p":"AMASSS — segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AMASSS automatically segments the anatomical structures of a craniofacial CBCT — mandible, maxilla, cranial base, upper airway, cervical vertebra, skin — each predicted by a network trained for that structure alone. You don't tune contrast or windowing by hand: the module handles that itself. On video AMASSS - Automatic Multi-Anatomical Skull Structure Segmentation 4 min 11 · DCBIA Videos channel AMASSS RC Seg tutorial 5 min 08 · DCBIA Videos channel"},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#quand","p":"AMASSS — segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you need bone, airway, or skin segmentations from a CBCT — for a single patient or for an entire batch at once. AMASSS also runs behind the scenes in two other tools: AREG_CBCT uses it to register two CBCT scans of the same patient, and VFACE uses it for its asymmetry analysis. If you use either of those, part of AMASSS's work is already being done for you, without opening this module."},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#structures","p":"AMASSS — segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it can segment","x":"Six structures have a trained model available in the published bundle: Reset view Raw CBCT — threshold only Drag to rotate · scroll to zoom · click a structure Mandible MAND label 1 The lower jaw, and the only bone of the skull that moves. Carries the lower teeth. Maxilla MAX label 4 The upper jaw, fused to the rest of the skull. Carries the upper teeth. Cranial base CB label 2 The floor of the braincase. It stops changing early, which is why registration uses it as the stable reference. Upper airway UAW label 3 Not bone: the column of air from the nasal cavity down to the pharynx. Segmenting "},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#besoin","p":"AMASSS — segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide A CBCT, or a folder of several CBCT scans, in .nii , .nii.gz , .nrrd , or .nrrd.gz The trained models, to download once The structures you want, checked in the list What comes out One binary mask per structure, or a single merged map where each structure carries its own value If requested, 3D surface(s) ( .vtk ) colored by structure, ready to display Your original scans are left untouched"},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#marche","p":"AMASSS — segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Step by step","x":"Download the models. The \"Download latest models\" button opens a GitHub release page in your browser: unlike AREG_CBCT or VFACE, AMASSS does not download anything on its own — you fetch and install the archive yourself. Point it at the input. A single CBCT or an entire folder; AMASSS finds scans even inside subfolders. Check the structures you want from the list. Choose the output format. Separate segmentations, merged, or both; also check surface generation if you want .vtk meshes ready to display in the scene. Set an output folder and run with the \"Predict\" button. This is when dependencies "},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#temps","p":"AMASSS — segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How long it takes","x":"AMASSS runs without a graphics card, but that's more theoretical than practical: on a batch of scans, the image-resampling step alone (without a GPU) costs roughly seven times the cost of the network itself. With a GPU, expect a reasonable computation per structure and per scan; without one, a batch of several patients quickly becomes impractical. One thing works in your favor: models are loaded once per structure, not once per scan — processing an entire folder at once costs little more than processing a single CBCT, structure by structure."},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#coince","p":"AMASSS — segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's stuck","x":"\"Small FOV\" changes nothing about the computation The checkbox that switches to a \"high definition\" list (teeth, root canal, and mandibular canal included) only changes which checkboxes are shown on screen. It does not reduce the field of view or the resolution of the computation — don't expect it to run faster or produce a finer result. Root canal is checked by default, with no model \"Root canal\" is checked by default in the structure list, but the published model bundle has no model for it. Checking it produces neither an error nor a segmentation: the failure is silent, the structure is simp"},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#apres","p":"AMASSS — segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"And after","x":"No automatic cleanup is applied to the segmentations produced — no hole filling, no keeping only the largest connected component: what you get is the network's raw output. Check it before using it for a measurement or a registration. For a single-file input with surface generation checked, the .vtk meshes are loaded directly into the scene once the computation finishes."},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#lire-plus","p":"AMASSS — segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"The paper below describes the UNETR version of the module. What runs today is a switch to nnU-Net that nobody has published: citing it to describe AMASSS as it runs means describing an architecture that is no longer there. On AMASSS itself Gillot et al., PLOS ONE 2022 — Automatic multi-anatomical skull structure segmentation of cone-beam computed tomography scans using 3D UNETR . The structures segmented, the training data and the measured accuracy. Open access. Around the method Isensee et al., Nature Methods 2021 — nnU-Net: a self-configuring method for deep learning-based biomedical image s"},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html","p":"AREG_CBCT — register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AREG_CBCT superimposes two CBCT scans of the same patient taken at two different times (T1 and T2), anchored on a bony region that's assumed not to have changed — the cranial base, the mandible, or the maxilla. The registration itself is a classic geometric computation, no neural network involved: reproducible, but entirely dependent on picking a good anchor. On video Automated Registration for CBCT (ARegCBCT) - Tutorial 13 min 59 · DCBIA Videos channel"},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#quand","p":"AREG_CBCT — register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you need to compare two CBCT scans of the same patient — before/after treatment, growth follow-up, post-surgical check — and they need to share the same spatial frame to be overlaid or measured. AREG_CBCT only does this voxel-to-voxel registration on a stable bony structure: your two scans must already belong to the same patient and represent two distinct time points."},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#modes","p":"AREG_CBCT — register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Three modes, one registration","x":"All three modes end up computing the same registration; they only differ in what happens beforehand. Semi-Automated — you supply the reference-region masks on T1 yourself. The most direct route if that work is already done. Fully-Automated — AREG_CBCT computes those masks for you. Only reliable when your T1 scans are already oriented consistently with each other. Orientation & Registration — AREG_CBCT starts by centering T1, placing landmarks on it and orienting it to a common reference, before computing masks and registering. The most complete mode, for T1 scans with no consistent orientation"},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#besoin","p":"AREG_CBCT — register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"Cranial base Mandible Maxilla Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom Register again the registration will only look inside — mean surface gap inside the mask: Two time points of the same patient, superimposed. Pick the structure you want to superimpose on: the green region is the only thing the registration looks at. Notice that the three give different results — the translation along one axis is 5.08 mm on the cranial base but 1.33 on the mandible. That is the whole point: you superimpose on something stable in order to measure how much the rest has moved. What to provide A baseline CBCT (T"},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#marche","p":"AREG_CBCT — register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Pick a mode. Semi-Automated, Fully-Automated, or Orientation & Registration — see above. Point it to the T1 and T2 folders. Check that each pair of files shares the same patient id, followed by _T1 or _T2 . In Semi-Automated, set the T1 masks folder. Without it, there's nothing to register against. Check the reference region(s). Cranial Base, Mandible, Maxilla — each one triggers its own registration. Download the models the first time you run it , then, if you want, check under \"Review and manual adjustment\" the steps you'd like to inspect before the run continues. Set an output folder and la"},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#temps","p":"AREG_CBCT — register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How long it takes","x":"The registration itself runs on CPU, no graphics card needed, and on a single thread to stay reproducible from one run to the next — a choice that favors stability of the result over speed. The steps around it (automatic mask segmentation, orientation) are much faster with a GPU. The paper describing the method reports under five minutes per patient for the whole chain; without a GPU for the upstream steps, budget more time on a large series."},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#coince","p":"AREG_CBCT — register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"A patient goes missing, with no error message T1/T2 pairing only works with the _T1 and _T2 suffixes in the file name. Any other convention ( _T3 , _baseline , a date…) keeps AREG_CBCT from recognizing the pair, and that patient is silently dropped — nothing is shown to flag it. If a patient is missing from the output, the first thing to check is that patient's file names. Avoid mask keywords in a scan's file name AREG_CBCT recognizes a mask file by keywords in its name ( seg , mask , pred , or the region's abbreviation — cb , mand , max ). The first file that matches is used as-is, with no ch"},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#apres","p":"AREG_CBCT — register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What's next","x":"The registered CBCT and its matrix are directly reusable — in AutoMatrix, in the chain's analysis tools, or for a simple visual overlay in Slicer. As with any automatic registration, a quick visual check before using it for a measurement remains good practice. Also note that the matrix doesn't account for any centering applied upstream to T2: to bring the follow-up scan all the way back to its original frame, you need to chain that step together with the AREG_CBCT matrix, not use one without the other."},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#lire-plus","p":"AREG_CBCT — register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"On AREG_CBCT itself Anchling et al., LNCS 14242, 2023 — Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans . The module's paper: orientation then voxel-based registration on a region of reference, with the error measured on a patient series. free full text . Around the method Ruellas et al., PLOS ONE 2016 — 3D Mandibular Superimposition: Comparison of Regions of Reference for Voxel-Based Registration . Which mandibular region to take as a reference, and why the choice changes the result. This is the decision you make in the interface. Ruellas et al., AJODO 2016 — Com"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html","p":"AREG_IOS — register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AREG_IOS superimposes two intraoral scans of the same patient taken at two different times — before and after treatment, say — without you having to draw the stable region by hand. It processes a whole batch of patients at once, not one scan at a time."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#quand","p":"AREG_IOS — register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you want to compare T1 and T2 of the same arch — measuring tooth movement, an arch change under treatment — and drawing the stable region by hand, scan after scan, would take too long across your series. If you only have one patient and would rather keep control over the registration region yourself, FlexReg is the interactive tool for that; AREG_IOS is its automatic, batch counterpart. Two modes, one per arch Butterfly (upper arch): the module predicts the stable palatal patch itself, with a network, and uses it as the registration anchor. MGL (lower arch): there's no palate down the"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#besoin","p":"AREG_IOS — register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide Two folders of scans, T1 and T2, in .vtk , .vtp , .stl or .obj Filenames that literally contain T1 or T2 , plus an arch marker (Upper/Lower, U/L, Maxilla/Mandible): matching is done on these words The palatal patch model, downloaded once, for Butterfly mode For MGL: a band width in mm, or your own mucogingival landmarks if you already have them What comes out Per patient: the T1 scan with the registration region marked on it, and the T2 scan registered onto T1 In Butterfly, if the lower arch is provided as a pair, it automatically follows the upper arch's registration A transform ma"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#mode","p":"AREG_IOS — register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Automatic or semi-automatic","x":"Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom Register again the two time points, still apart the Butterfly patch, predicted on the palate — rigid ICP, on the patch alone registered — mean gap on the patch Two time points of the same arch. The green area is the only thing the registration uses — a patch on the palate, chosen because it changes little between visits. Everything else follows along. The motion here is small (4.66°) because ASO has already brought both scans into a common frame upstream; AREG_IOS is the fine pass, not the coarse one. This is the second choice to make, independent of wh"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#marche","p":"AREG_IOS — register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Step by step","x":"Pick the regime. Auto for raw scans, semi-auto if they're already segmented and oriented. Pick the arch. Butterfly for the upper arch, MGL for the lower. Point to the two input folders , T1 and T2. AREG_IOS pairs up patients on its own from the filenames. For MGL , set the band width (0 to 20 mm), or point to a folder of mucogingival landmarks if you already have them — otherwise they're placed automatically. For Butterfly , download the palatal patch model if you haven't already. Point to an output folder that already exists , then run. A progress bar tracks patients as they complete."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#temps","p":"AREG_IOS — register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Time and hardware","x":"Butterfly mode needs a compatible graphics card : the palatal patch prediction has no CPU fallback, unlike some other tools in the suite. Without a GPU, this mode simply won't run — use MGL on machines that don't have one. MGL mode doesn't call that network at all; it relies on landmark placement and a geometric band construction instead, both lighter. Either way, processing runs folder by folder, so total time mostly scales with how many patients are in the batch."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#coince","p":"AREG_IOS — register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something goes wrong","x":"No compatible graphics card In Butterfly mode, the palatal patch inference requires a CUDA GPU, full stop. On a machine without a compatible GPU, this mode fails outright. Use MGL on those machines, or process the upper arch elsewhere. File naming: order matters Put the arch marker before the timepoint marker in the filename ( P001_Upper_T1.vtk ), not after ( P001_T1_Upper.vtk ): in the wrong order, one of the output matrices won't be found — with no blocking error, the run finishes but an expected file is simply missing. Also avoid the same words (Upper, Lower, U, L, Mx, Md…) in parent folder"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#apres","p":"AREG_IOS — register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What happens next","x":"The registered T2 scan overlays directly onto T1 in the scene: that's your basis for measuring change between the two timepoints. The marked region on T1 (palatal patch or mucogingival band) is only a registration aid, not a segmentation to interpret on its own — and as noted above, check it before trusting the result."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#lire-plus","p":"AREG_IOS — register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"On AREG_IOS itself Hutin et al., ShapeMI 2023 (LNCS 14350) — AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOralScans . The module's paper: a network isolates the stable palatal region, then an ICP registers the two scans on it. Around the method Vasilakos et al., Scientific Reports 2017 — Assessment of different techniques for 3D superimposition of serial digital maxillary dental casts on palatal structures . How the various palatal regions compare as superimposition references. Aliaga-Del Castillo et al., Orthod Craniofac Res 2022 — Comparison and reproducibility of three methods for maxillary dig"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AREG_IOSCBCT registers an intraoral scan (IOS) onto the CBCT of the same patient, taken at the same visit. It has nothing to do with AREG_IOS, which compares two scans from two different visits: here the two inputs come from different sensors — optical and volumetric — that have to be brought into agreement. On video Automated registration of intra oral scan with CBCT using an accurate and quick workflow 45 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#quand","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you want the surface detail of an intraoral scan — crowns, contact points — and the bone anatomy of a CBCT in the same frame: for a visual check, a combined measurement, or simply to overlay both images from one visit. The module does the alignment; there is nothing to draw or register by hand. Three ways to get there Depending on what you already have — nothing, raw scans, or landmarks already placed — the module offers three modes that don't ask for the same input files. See \"Three modes\" below before you start."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#besoin","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide Intraoral scans in .vtk only — no other format is read CBCT in .nii.gz only — same rule File names carrying the patient id, a timepoint ( T0 , T1 or T2 ), and the arch ( U for upper, L for lower) as standalone tokens, not buried inside another word Depending on the mode: landmarks already computed, or nothing beyond the raw scans What comes out One registered scan per arch and per patient, in .vtk One landmark file per arch, in Slicer's format ( .mrk.json ), carrying the registered scan's coordinates Normalized output names — they may no longer look exactly like your input names"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#modes","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Three modes, which one to pick","x":"Lower arch Upper arch Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom Register again the CBCT surface, marching cubes at a hard-coded threshold of ALI places the same six occlusal points on each modality paired by label, never by position — rigid pre-alignment, vtkLandmarkTransform then ICP, inside a 1 mm window — not replayed here Your intraoral scan and your CBCT start in unrelated positions. ALI places the same six occlusal points on each, they are matched up by name, and the scan is brought onto the CBCT. The numbers are real: the average mismatch drops from 24.94 mm to 2.88 mm on the lower arch."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#marche","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Pick the mode that matches what you already have — see the section above. Check your file names before launching anything. A file named P001_T1_U_IOS.vtk is recognized; a timepoint outside T0–T2, or an arch letter glued to the rest of the name, is not. Point the module to the folders the chosen mode asks for — IOS, CBCT, and where relevant the landmark folders. Set an output folder , then launch. Open the log when it's done if the number of registered patients is lower than expected — that is where skipped patients are reported."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#coince","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's wrong","x":"No matrix is written Registration produces surfaces that are already transformed, not a matrix file. Unlike AREG_IOS or AREG_CBCT, you cannot reopen the result and nudge it with the mouse: if the alignment is off, the fix is to rerun — with different landmarks or a different setting — not to correct it by hand. One scanned arch means the patient is dropped Every patient must provide both arches, scan and landmarks included. If even one is missing — say, only one arch was ever scanned — the patient disappears from the result with no visible error, only a line in the log. Only T0, T1 and T2 are "},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#apres","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What happens next","x":"The registered scans open directly in Slicer, next to the original CBCT. Since no matrix is kept, the only check available is visual — inspect the overlay before using it for a measurement. Also check the log for any line saying the pre-alignment was skipped: that would mean a batch came out without the first registration step, and nothing else would have told you."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#lire-plus","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT — place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"No publication describes this module's multimodal path. The AReg IOS paper covers registering one intra-oral scan onto another over time, not matching a mesh with a CBCT. Around the method Zheng et al., Clinical Oral Investigations 2025 — Automatic multimodal registration of cone-beam computed tomography and intraoral scans: a systematic review and meta-analysis . The state of the art on the problem this module tackles, with the errors reported by published methods. The best entry point. Kim et al., Bioengineering 2023 — Novel Procedure for Automatic Registration between Cone-Beam Computed Tom"},{"u":"guide/ASO.html","p":"ASO — orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"ASO rotates a scan to bring it into the frame of a reference patient: it does not compare two time points against each other, and it does not segment anything. It handles both a CBCT (a volume) and an intraoral scan (a surface), with two different engines behind a single window. On video Automated Standardized Orientation (ASOCBCT) - Tutorial 7 min 09 · DCBIA Videos channel"},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#quand","p":"ASO — orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Before most registrations, really. A registration assumes its two inputs already start in a reasonably consistent orientation; without that, it has no correct starting point. AREG_IOS, for instance, looks directly for the transform file that ASO produces to build its own matrix — if your scans have never been oriented by ASO, there is nothing for it to find. Run ASO whenever you are preparing scans for a registration, for superimposing a longitudinal series, or simply to bring a whole cohort into the same frame."},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#mode","p":"ASO — orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Choosing a mode","x":"Two data types, two ways of working You first tell ASO what kind of files you have: CBCT for a volume, IOS for an intraoral scan. For each, two modes are available. Fully-Automated asks only for your raw scans: ASO places the landmarks it needs itself (via a network, for a CBCT) or segments the teeth (for a scan) before orienting. Semi-Automated skips that prediction step and works directly on landmarks or teeth you have already identified — faster, but it assumes that preparatory work already exists. CBCT semi-automatic: don't run it on its own On a CBCT, the semi-automatic mode needs a trans"},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#besoin","p":"ASO — orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide CBCT : the volume as .nii.gz (DICOM is accepted but less reliable — prefer .nii.gz when you can). In semi-automatic mode, also your own landmarks already placed and the transform file described above Intraoral scan (IOS) : the mesh, in a common format (converted to .vtk along the way). In semi-automatic mode, the 3 or 4 reference teeth to check on the arch; in automatic mode, nothing more — tooth segmentation runs on its own if it hasn't already been done The reference patient (the \"gold\"): a dataset downloaded once, which every orientation targets What comes out The reoriented scan"},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#marche","p":"ASO — orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Step by step","x":"Download the reference patient and the models. A one-time step: this is the target every one of your scans will orient itself to. Choose the data type and the mode. CBCT or IOS, then Fully-Automated or Semi-Automated depending on what you already have on hand. Point to the input folder. ASO processes the whole folder in one run. For an intraoral scan in semi-automatic mode, check the reference teeth (3 or 4) that the orientation will line up on. For a CBCT in semi-automatic mode, make sure your landmarks cover at least 4 points shared with the gold: the interface accepts 3 checked, but orienta"},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#coince","p":"ASO — orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something goes wrong","x":"Your CBCT landmark files are gone In semi-automatic mode, ASO starts by merging every landmark file it finds in your input folder into a single file — then deletes the originals . Keep a copy of your .mrk.json files elsewhere before running a job, especially if the input folder is also where you keep your only copy of those landmarks. Occlusion: name your files Upper / Lower unambiguously Occlusion mode pairs the upper and lower arches by file name ( Upper / _U_ or Lower / _L_ ). A file whose name carries none of these markers is silently treated as a lower arch — with no error message. Check "},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#apres","p":"ASO — orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"And after","x":"A scan oriented by ASO is not guaranteed to be anatomically perfect: the orientation is a geometric estimate, not ground truth. Open the result in Slicer and compare it visually against the reference patient before feeding it into a registration. That downstream use is exactly why the transform file is produced: the next modules use it to rebuild the full matrix, from the raw scan through to the registered result."},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#lire-plus","p":"ASO — orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"On ASO itself Anchling et al., LNCS 14242, 2023 — Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans . The ASO/AREG family paper: how the automatic orientation is obtained, and how far it departs from a manual one. free full text . Around the method Ruellas et al., AJODO 2016 — Common 3-dimensional coordinate system for assessment of directional changes . The coordinate system the automatic orientation aims to reproduce. It is what defines “correctly oriented”. Gillot et al., Orthod Craniofac Res 2023 — Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomograp"},{"u":"guide/Agent.html","p":"Agent — let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"Agent is a conversational assistant that runs entirely on your machine. You describe what you want to do, in plain language, and it proposes a tool from the extension already filled in with its parameters — or it just answers you, depending on the mode you pick. On video AI Agent directly in 3D Slicer to help you learn dental and cranofacial imaging 30 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel Read this before you try it As the repository stands today, Agent cannot locate the tools it wants to launch . Unless you configure it manually, any attempt to run a tool fails. The mode that only answers, wit"},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#quand","p":"Agent — let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Use it when you don't want to hunt down which module in the extension matches what you need, or build a command line by hand: you describe the task — \"orient these CBCTs\", \"segment these scans\" — and Agent proposes a tool with the parameters it understood from your request. It also works as a plain consultant, in \"Ask\" mode, for questions that don't need anything run."},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#installer","p":"Agent — let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What to install first","x":"Agent relies on a language-model server running locally on your machine, Ollama . If it isn't already present, the module offers to download and start it for you. Either way, expect the first launch to download several gigabytes — the language model alone is a bit over 5 GB — before you can ask your first question. Nothing to install by hand on the Slicer side The required Python packages install themselves automatically on first launch, inside Slicer's own Python."},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#besoin","p":"Agent — let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A request written in plain language, in English or French The mode you want: Agent (Automated) to actually run something, Ask (Interactive) to just talk If relevant, the folders involved, dropped into the drop zone What comes out In Agent mode: a chosen tool, its parameters, a confirmation dialog, then execution if you approve In Ask mode: a free-text reply, nothing is launched If the tool fails, up to two automatic repair attempts, each with its own confirmation"},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#marche","p":"Agent — let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Make sure Ollama is running. If needed, let Agent install and start it for you, and wait out the model download on first launch. Pick a mode. \"Agent (Automated)\" to actually launch a tool, \"Ask (Interactive)\" to only get an answer. Describe what you want , in plain language, the way you'd explain it to a colleague. Drop the relevant folders into the drop zone if your request involves specific data — Agent will try to guess which is which. Send the message. In Agent mode, a tool and its parameters are proposed; in Ask mode, the answer comes straight back."},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#confirmation","p":"Agent — let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Reading the confirmation dialog","x":"In Agent mode, nothing runs without a \"Yes\" from you, in a dialog that lists every parameter it settled on, as key=value pairs. This is the only point where you can catch a wrongly guessed folder or a value that doesn't match what you meant, before the tool actually starts. Read every line, carefully For most tools, the mapping between what Agent believes it's passing and what the tool actually expects is off. A run can therefore start with wrong values without any error warning you beforehand — the confirmation dialog is your only real check. Once you approve, Slicer's interface freezes until"},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#coince","p":"Agent — let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"\"Nothing launches, or the error makes no sense\" As the repository stands, Agent can't find the scripts it wants to run on its own: unless you tell it yourself where they are, execution fails before it even starts. The automatic repair step that follows can't fix this — the problem isn't in the parameters, it's in the script's path. A run starting with wrong values See above: always check the confirmation dialog before approving. A parameter mismatch usually shows up only by eye, in that dialog — not in an error message. Everything is slow without a graphics card If the model server is running "},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#lire-plus","p":"Agent — let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"No publication: the module is work in progress, described by a single NA-MIC project report. On Agent itself NA-MIC Project Week 45, Boston 2026 — AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools . The project report: what the agent is meant to do, and how far the work got. Around the method Qwen Team, arXiv 2025 — Qwen3 Technical Report . The model that decides which tool to send you to and extracts its parameters. Reimers & Gurevych, EMNLP 2019 — Sentence-BERT: Sentence Embeddings using Siamese BERT-Networks . How a question is compared against the indexed documentation: the step that decides what th"},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html","p":"AutoCrop3D — crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AutoCrop3D batch-crops a folder of volumes to a shared region of interest, or to a separate region per patient. No network, no model to download: it's a geometric crop, near-instant, that never touches your voxel values."},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#quand","p":"AutoCrop3D — crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Any time a series is too large or too inconsistent in field of view to process as is: run it before a segmenter (AMASSS, BatchDentalSeg) to shrink what needs processing, or after one to keep only the useful region. AutoCrop3D can also generate a VTK mesh from a segmentation you already produced, for a quick preview."},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#roi","p":"AutoCrop3D — crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Preparing the region of interest","x":"The region to crop comes from a single Slicer ROI file ( .mrk.json ) and nothing else: place a Markups > ROI box in the scene on a representative patient, adjust it, and save it. Two ways to use it: one ROI applied to every volume in the folder, or one ROI per patient — in that case, name each ROI file with the same prefix as its matching volumes, so the pairing works (see below). The ROI must be an axis-aligned box, saved as-is The fast crop path completely ignores the orientation and coordinate system declared in the ROI file: it only reads the center and size, and assumes a box aligned with"},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#besoin","p":"AutoCrop3D — crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of volumes — .nii.gz , .nii , .nrrd.gz , .nrrd , .gipl.gz , or .gipl An ROI file ( .mrk.json ), or a folder with one ROI per patient An output suffix (\"cropped\" by default) What comes out One cropped volume per input, mirroring the input folder structure Optional: a VTK mesh per cropped segmentation No transform file — the geometry lives entirely in the volume itself"},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#marche","p":"AutoCrop3D — crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Place and save your ROI. One box for the whole series, or one per patient, named to match its volumes. Point it to the input volume folder. AutoCrop3D processes the whole folder in one go. Set the ROI file or folder. A single file gets applied to everyone; several get matched per patient. Choose an output suffix , and if you need it, \"Keep the same size as input\" to preserve the full original geometry, with everything outside the ROI set to zero. Rotated ROI only: check \"Use module Crop Volume for tilted images\" — read the warning below before you do. Set an output folder , then run it."},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#preserve","p":"AutoCrop3D — crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What's preserved","x":"This is the whole point of the tool: the default crop path never resamples. It just extracts a block of voxels and recomputes the volume's origin; spacing, orientation and voxel values stay bit-for-bit identical to the original. In practice, a registration matrix computed on the full volume stays valid, unchanged, on the cropped volume, because the physical coordinates never moved — you can crop after registering without recomputing anything."},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#coince","p":"AutoCrop3D — crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"A folder with exactly one ROI file → error If you're using per-patient ROIs but the folder holds only a single .mrk.json file, the module errors out instead of applying it to every volume. In that case, point it directly at that file as a single shared ROI rather than at the folder containing it. Per-patient ROI: matching breaks on an underscore Volume names and ROI names aren't split the same way when they're compared. Patient IDs without an underscore ( MA0001_Scan.nii.gz / MA0001_ROI.mrk.json ) match fine; an underscore inside the ID itself breaks the pairing and that patient gets skipped, "},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#lire-plus","p":"AutoCrop3D — crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"A utility module: it has no publication of its own and needs none. It applies in batch the cropping Slicer already knows how to do. Around the method Documentation 3D Slicer — Crop Volume . The underlying Slicer module: what the interpolation, sampling factor and isotropy you see here actually mean. Barone et al., Orthod Craniofac Res 2025 — Deep Learning-Based Three-Dimensional Analysis Reveals Distinct Patterns of Condylar Remodelling After Orthognathic Surgery in Skeletal Class III Patients . A lab study where this batch cropping is a preparation step — an example of what the module is good"},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html","p":"AutoMatrix — apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AutoMatrix applies one or more ITK transformation matrices to an entire folder of volumes and landmark files in a single pass. No neural network, no GPU: it's a batch tool, not a computation step — the matrix itself comes from a registration you ran somewhere else."},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#quand","p":"AutoMatrix — apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"You ran a registration module (AREG, ASO, FlexReg…) and got a transformation matrix ( .tfm ) out of it. Now you want to apply that same matrix to other files for the same patient — a segmentation, a second volume, a set of landmarks — without opening each one in Slicer by hand. Or you ran that registration on a whole series of patients and want to process the entire folder in one go. That's what AutoMatrix is for: it doesn't register anything itself, it replays a transformation you already computed. One matrix, one motion Applied to a volume, the matrix moves its content. Applied to a landmark"},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#appariement","p":"AutoMatrix — apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How files and matrices get matched","x":"AutoMatrix never asks you to point out by hand which matrix goes with which patient. It guesses the pairing by comparing file names: it strips a list of known keywords ( _Seg , _Or , _MAND , _CB , a timestamp like _T1 , _T2 …) from the scan's name, and a slightly different list from the matrix's name, until it's left with a patient identifier on each side. If the two identifiers match, the file and the matrix are paired. The catch: those two keyword lists aren't the same. The rule that works reliably: start every file name with the patient identifier, then an underscore, then whatever else you"},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#besoin","p":"AutoMatrix — apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of volumes as .nii , .nii.gz or .nrrd , and/or Slicer landmark files ( .mrk.json ) An ITK matrix ( .tfm , .h5 or .mat ), or a folder of matrices named following the same rule as the scans Check this if your volumes are segmentations: the dedicated box, so labels don't get smoothed out by interpolation Optional: a reference file to force the output grid (size, orientation) What comes out A transformed copy of each matched file, laid out in the same folder structure as the input Your original files are never modified In a landmark file, only the points marked as defined "},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#marche","p":"AutoMatrix — apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Point it to the input folder. Scans and/or landmark files to transform; the whole folder is processed in one go. Point it to the matrix, or a folder of matrices. Check that the names follow the patient + suffix rule above, or point straight at a single file to apply it to everyone. Check the segmentation box if your volumes are labelmaps. Give it a reference file if you want to force the output grid; otherwise each file keeps its own input volume's grid, and anything pushed outside the field of view by the transform is lost. Pick an output suffix , and decide whether the matrix name should be "},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#miroir","p":"AutoMatrix — apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Flipping left/right without hunting for a matrix","x":"If all you need is a left/right mirror, there's no need to build your own matrix: the Mirror mode downloads a ready-made reflection matrix and fills it into the matrix field for you, with its own output suffix ( _mir ). The mirror isn't centered on your patient The reflection happens across the sagittal plane of the scene's origin, not the patient's own sagittal plane. If your patient isn't already centered in the scene, the result will come out both mirrored and shifted — plan on a registration step afterward to correct the offset."},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#coince","p":"AutoMatrix — apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"Some advertised formats no longer work The interface still offers meshes ( .vtk , .vtp , .stl , .off , .obj ) as input scans for intraoral surfaces, and matrices as .npy — both get counted in the progress bar, but neither produces any output: each file of that kind is simply skipped, with a line in the error log. A .txt matrix only works if it's an actual ITK transform file, not a plain 4×4 array. A failed match gives no warning If a scan's name and a matrix's name don't reduce to the same patient identifier (see above), that file is simply never transformed — no error message, no visible warn"},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#lire-plus","p":"AutoMatrix — apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"No publication, and nothing to publish: the module applies transformation matrices to files, in batch. What matters is knowing what a matrix is in Slicer and which coordinate frame it is in. Around the method Documentation 3D Slicer — Transforms . What a linear transform is in Slicer, how it is applied and how it is inverted. Documentation 3D Slicer — Coordinate systems . RAS, LPS, and why a correct matrix applied in the wrong frame flips your patient. The tool's full bibliography — what was found, and what was looked for without success — is in its sources . How it actually works → The Atlas "},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html","p":"BatchDentalSeg — segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"BatchDentalSeg segments your dento-maxillo-facial CT and CBCT volumes assembly-line style: point it at a folder, and it applies the same set of labels to every scan, one at a time. The choice that matters here isn't a network setting — it's the model . Each one segments different structures."},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#quand","p":"BatchDentalSeg — segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you have several CT or CBCT scans that need the same anatomical structures segmented — skull, mandible, teeth, mandibular canal, or a tooth-by-tooth breakdown. BatchDentalSeg is a standalone tool: no other module in the repository calls it, and it doesn't prepare anything for a downstream module. It's an endpoint, not a step in a pipeline. One extension to install first BatchDentalSeg relies on the external Slicer extension NNUnet to run the computation. It doesn't install itself: get it through the Extensions Manager, then restart Slicer. Without it, the module shows a message and re"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#modele","p":"BatchDentalSeg — segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Choosing a model","x":"Four models are offered in the dropdown. They share the same engine and the same window, but they don't segment the same thing: Model What it segments Built for DentalSegmentator 5 structures: upper skull (maxilla included), mandible, upper teeth, lower teeth, mandibular canal Permanent dentition (adult) PediatricDentalsegmentator The same 5 structures, same breakdown Mixed dentition (child) — only the training data differs NasoMaxillaDentSeg 6 structures: the maxilla is detached from the skull and output as its own structure When you need the maxilla isolated from the rest of the facial mass "},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#besoin","p":"BatchDentalSeg — segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide A folder of scans in .nii , .nii.gz , .gipl , or .gipl.gz — no NRRD, and subfolders aren't scanned The model you want, picked from the menu An output folder What comes out Always: <name>_Segmentation.nii.gz , the label map on the original scan's geometry Optionally: STL/OBJ meshes, VTK per structure or merged, binary NIfTI per structure, glTF Your original scans are left untouched"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#marche","p":"BatchDentalSeg — segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Install the NNUnet extension if you haven't already, and restart Slicer. Pick a model from the dropdown, based on which structures you need (table above). The first time you use a given model, its weights are downloaded automatically. Point it at the input folder. BatchDentalSeg processes the whole folder at once; scans that already have a segmentation can be skipped automatically (checked by default). Check the output formats you need beyond the label map, which is always written. Set the output folder and the device (GPU or CPU), then run. A queue works through the scans one at a time with a"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#memoire","p":"BatchDentalSeg — segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Memory and time","x":"A full-mouth CBCT can demand a lot of RAM during inference — the module's own notes cite a real case of a scan that filled 114 of a machine's 125 GB and got killed by the system. Three lines of defense are in place: before starting, a scan whose estimated memory need exceeds your budget (85% of free RAM by default) is marked failed without ever starting ; during the run, a confirmed overrun on two consecutive checks stops the scan cleanly instead of letting the system lock up; a Free memory button lets you reclaim memory by hand if any processes are left hanging after an abrupt stop. If a scan"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#coince","p":"BatchDentalSeg — segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something goes wrong","x":"Switching models later can wipe the others' weights Re-downloading or updating DentalSegmentator's weights clears the entire models folder, including anything already downloaded for PediatricDentalsegmentator, NasoMaxillaDentSeg, and UniversalLabDentalsegmentator. If you switch between models, expect to re-download them. Your files aren't being picked up Only .nii , .nii.gz , .gipl , and .gipl.gz are recognized, and only at the top level of the chosen folder — a scan in NRRD or sitting in a subfolder is silently ignored. Two different folders containing a scan with the same base name will over"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#resultat","p":"BatchDentalSeg — segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"And after","x":"The label map is always written, whatever other formats you checked: open it in Slicer to check the segmentation before using it for a measurement or an export. With UniversalLabDentalsegmentator, the network sometimes mixes up left and right; a dedicated button corrects the laterality using the central incisors, without touching the original result. If a mesh export fails, the scan is still marked done — the label map is what counts."},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#lire-plus","p":"BatchDentalSeg — segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"The original model is published and documented. The three variants added later — pediatric, universal labelling , naso-maxillary — are not: no training set, no evaluation. On BatchDentalSeg itself Dot et al., Journal of Dentistry 2024 — DentalSegmentator: Robust open source deep learning-based CT and CBCT image segmentation . The model this module runs: the five structures segmented, the training data, and the measured accuracy. Free to read. Around the method Isensee et al., Nature Methods 2021 — nnU-Net: a self-configuring method for deep learning-based biomedical image segmentation . The fr"},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html","p":"CLIC — find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"CLIC scans a CBCT slice by slice and flags impacted canines, telling you for each one where it sits relative to the bone: buccal , bicortical , or palatal . You start from the raw volume — no prior segmentation or landmarking needed. The progress bar doesn't move — that's expected While the tool runs, the progress bar stays at zero and nothing shows up in the Slicer scene, even after processing has finished. This is not a crash: the result file is written to disk in the output folder regardless. You have to go find it and load it yourself — see below where it lands."},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#quand","p":"CLIC — find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"When you have a CBCT with one or more impacted canines and want to know, without inspecting each one by eye, which side of the cortical bone it sits on. CLIC has no dependency on other modules — you don't need to have run AMASSS, ASO, or ALI first."},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#besoin","p":"CLIC — find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"Provide A folder of CBCT scans in .nii or .nii.gz The trained model, downloaded once An output folder, chosen explicitly You get One subfolder per scan, holding a .nii.gz label map Three possible voxel values: buccal, bicortical, palatal Directly overlayable on the source scan, no registration needed Accepted formats that don't actually work The file picker also lists .nrrd , .mha , and .mhd . Don't use them: processing can only read .nii / .nii.gz , and fails on the other formats regardless of what the picker allowed. Linux only The module has no working path on Windows, whatever the README s"},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#marche","p":"CLIC — find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Download the model. The dedicated button fetches the trained network. Do this once. Point it at the input folder. A flat folder of .nii.gz files — not a folder of subfolders. Choose an output folder. This step is mandatory: without one, processing fails before it even starts. Run. The first run also sets up the computing environment, so it takes longer than later ones. Wait it out , even if nothing moves on screen. Processing goes scan by scan with no visual feedback in between."},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#resultat","p":"CLIC — find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Where to find the result","x":"Once processing is done, open the output folder you chose: it holds one subfolder per processed scan, and inside each, a .nii.gz file carrying the suffix shown in the interface ( seg by default). Load that file into Slicer yourself as a segmentation volume — it's already aligned to the source scan, no registration required. Values 1, 2, and 3 correspond to buccal, bicortical, and palatal respectively."},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#coince","p":"CLIC — find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If it gets stuck","x":"Cancel does nothing It stops neither the running computation nor the underlying process. If you genuinely need to stop, close Slicer. The first run needs internet access Building the network downloads generic pretrained weights before the trained model is applied on top. Without a connection on the first attempt, processing fails. Two model files in the folder With more than one model file in the models folder, whichever name sorts first alphabetically gets used, with no warning. Keep a single .pth file in that folder to avoid any ambiguity."},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#lire-plus","p":"CLIC — find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"The module has no publication yet: its only public description is a NA-MIC project page. On CLIC itself NA-MIC Project Week 43, Montréal 2025 — Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption . The project report: the goal, the data, and where the work stood at that point. Around the method He et al., ICCV 2017 — Mask R-CNN . The architecture the module runs: detect objects and produce their masks at the same time. Swaity et al., Scientific Reports 2023 — Deep learning driven segmentation of maxillary impacted canine on cone "},{"u":"guide/CNE.html","p":"CNE — extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"The name doesn't say it: CNE doesn't touch any image , volume, or mesh. It's a reading tool — it takes free-text clinical notes and pulls structured information out of them, as key/value pairs, using a language model (LLM) that runs locally inside Slicer. On video CNE (Clinical notes extraction) to summarize your notes and extract common data elements 34 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel"},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#quand","p":"CNE — extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Any time you have a batch of free-text notes — TMJ or Ortho visits — and want the key facts (symptoms, diagnoses) pulled out quickly instead of reading each one by hand. CNE processes a whole folder of notes at once, using a different model depending on whether the notes are TMJ or Ortho. Nothing leaves your machine The model runs inside Slicer's own Python, on your computer. Notes aren't sent to any online service during extraction — the only step that touches the network is the one-time model download."},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#besoin","p":"CNE — extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of notes, placed directly inside it (no subfolders) — .txt , .pdf , or .docx The note type: TMJ or Ortho , each with its own model The matching model, downloaded once (a few GB) What comes out One text file per note, Extraction_<note name>.txt , in the output folder key : value lines summarizing the note No summary table, no CSV: one file per note, to be checked one at a time"},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#marche","p":"CNE — extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Choose the note type. TMJ or Ortho: this decides which model gets used and how long a note it can take in one go. Download the matching model. On the first click on \"Apply\", the module installs whatever is missing (after you confirm), then fetches the model from Hugging Face. Only needed once per note type. Point it to the notes folder. Files must sit directly at the root of the chosen folder, not sorted into per-patient subfolders. Set an output folder. Launch, then wait. Notes are processed one after another; expect several tens of seconds per note."},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#temps","p":"CNE — extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How long it takes, how much memory","x":"Even though the module mentions using a graphics card, the install only sets up CPU packages: in practice, extraction always runs on CPU . Expect roughly a minute per note depending on its length; on a batch of several dozen notes, it's better to start the run and come back later. The loaded model needs about 5 to 6 GB of RAM on top of what Slicer already uses, and its download is several gigabytes, done once per note type."},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#relire","p":"CNE — extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Check the result","x":"All the clinical content in the output file comes from the model: there's no rule, no term dictionary, no schema check behind it. The module expects a JSON reply but doesn't enforce it technically — it just looks for the first opening brace and the last closing brace in the response. If the model's output isn't well formed, its raw text lands in the file as-is, with no indication that formatting failed. Check every file it produces before relying on it."},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#coince","p":"CNE — extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"The Ortho model won't download The file the module asks for the Ortho model doesn't exist at the address listed on Hugging Face: the download fails with \"Download failed or was cancelled\", with no further explanation. TMJ mode downloads fine. If you pick Ortho today, expect this failure — it isn't something on your end. Notes sorted into per-patient subfolders CNE only looks at files sitting directly in the folder you chose, not at anything inside subfolders. A folder organized with one subfolder per patient will produce no output at all, with no error message. Scanned PDF PDF text extraction "},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#lire-plus","p":"CNE — extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"The module has no publication, and the models it runs are lab fine-tunes uploaded without a model card: no described training set, no evaluation. The readings below give what is generally known about how reliable this kind of extraction is. Around the method Wiest et al., npj Digital Medicine 2024 — Privacy-preserving large language models for structured medical information retrieval . Exactly the intended use — pulling structured fields out of a report with a locally-run model — and what it actually achieves. Panchal et al., BMJ Health & Care Informatics 2026 — Benchmarking large language mod"},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html","p":"DOCShapeAXI — classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"DOCShapeAXI takes already-segmented meshes and sorts them into a category — a severity stage, a presence or absence of obstruction, or a continuous ratio depending on the case. For every shape it classifies, it also writes a map directly onto the mesh showing where the network looked most closely to reach its decision."},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#quand","p":"DOCShapeAXI — classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Reach for it whenever you have 3D shapes already extracted — condyles, airways, cleft bone defects — and want an automatic classification instead of a case-by-case visual read, with a map thrown in that gives you a lead on the model's reasoning. DOCShapeAXI does not segment anything itself: the .vtk meshes you feed it must already exist, produced by another tool (AMASSS for condyles, an airway segmentation, and so on). Three shape families, one window The data type you pick is the only thing that decides which model runs and which categories come out: Mandibular Condyle — a severity stage out "},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#besoin","p":"DOCShapeAXI — classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide A folder of already-segmented .vtk meshes, all of the same shape type The data type picked from the list — condyle, airway, or cleft An output folder, created ahead of time before you launch What comes out A csv listing every mesh with its prediction, one column per task A copy of each mesh, per task, carrying the explainability map as a per-point scalar Your original meshes are left untouched"},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#marche","p":"DOCShapeAXI — classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Pick the data type. Mandibular condyle, airway obstruction, or cleft bone defect. This choice alone decides which model gets downloaded and which categories come back. Point to the input folder. It should hold only already-segmented .vtk meshes of the type you just picked. Create and point to an output folder. On the first run against that folder, the matching trained model is downloaded automatically (roughly 70 MB). Launch. For airway, three passes chain automatically — obstruction, severity, ratio; for the other two types, a single pass is enough. Every mesh then gets its explainability map"},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#lire","p":"DOCShapeAXI — classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Reading the result","x":"The prediction csv adds one column per task to your list of meshes: a class (an integer) for a severity stage or a binary obstruction call, a number between 0 and 100 for the airway's continuous ratio. No confidence score comes with the class — DOCShapeAXI only reports the category it settled on, not how close the call was between two neighboring classes. For the explainability map, open the mesh produced in the explainability subfolder for that task. In Slicer's Models module, under the Scalars tab, set Active Scalar to the array named after the class you're interested in (one array per class"},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#coince","p":"DOCShapeAXI — classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's stuck","x":"The output folder has to exist before you launch If the folder you point to doesn't exist yet, the model download is simply skipped, and the error only surfaces later, when the model tries to load. Create the output folder yourself before starting. Adding files mid-way has no effect The list of meshes to process is only rebuilt if it doesn't already exist in the output folder. If you drop more meshes into the input folder without changing the output folder, they simply won't be processed. Disk space The downloaded model isn't cached: every new output folder re-downloads it in full. Across seve"},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#lire-plus","p":"DOCShapeAXI — classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"On DOCShapeAXI itself Prieto et al., SPIE Medical Imaging 2024 — ShapeAXI: Shape Analysis Explainability and Interpretability . The framework this module drives: classify a 3D shape, then show which part of the surface drove the decision. Around the method Mattos et al., Journal of Dentistry 2025 — Explainable artificial intelligence to quantify adenoid hypertrophy and airway obstruction . The same framework applied to the airway: a complete example of what the explanation maps produce, and how to read them. Miranda et al., Scientific Reports 2023 — Interpretable artificial intelligence for cl"},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html","p":"FlexReg — register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"FlexReg registers two intraoral scans of the same patient taken at different times. Instead of letting a network guess which area hasn't moved, you mark it yourself, with the mouse, on the upper or lower arch. On video Discover the new features of FlexReg 1 min 52 · DCBIA Videos channel"},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#quand","p":"FlexReg — register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Reach for FlexReg when AREG_IOS — the automatic version — can't find a good registration area on its own: a partially edentulous arch, an unusual palate shape, or simply a case where you'd rather keep control over what counts as the fixed reference. FlexReg is the \"draw it yourself\" version of the same registration: both tools produce and read the same kind of landmark, so you can start in one and continue in the other. Two ways to build the stable area On an upper arch, the area is anchored on the palate: pick four reference teeth and FlexReg builds a butterfly-shaped patch around them, or dr"},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#besoin","p":"FlexReg — register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide Two scans of the same patient, as .vtk or .stl ( .stl is converted automatically) A \"moving\" scan (the more recent one) and a \"fixed\" scan (the reference) Teeth identified crown by crown — if not, FlexReg runs the segmentation itself, at the cost of a wait on that first pass A graphics card (GPU) — required to build the patch What comes out A registered scan in .vtk , saved next to the original A transformation matrix ( .tfm ) reusable by the other tools in the repository A preview shown in the scene before anything is saved"},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#marche","p":"FlexReg — register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Load both scans. Mark which one is moving (the one being aligned) and which one is the fixed reference. Check the tooth identification. If the arch has no segmentation array yet, FlexReg computes one on its own the first time — expect extra time on that run only. Build the stable area. On an upper arch, pick four reference teeth and shape the butterfly patch with the sliders (position, length, width), or draw its outline freehand. On a lower arch, let FlexReg place the mucogingival landmarks, then adjust the band's height around them. Preview. The preview button runs the registration into a te"},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#temps","p":"FlexReg — register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How long it takes","x":"Building the patch and running the registration take a few seconds per pair of scans on a GPU, once the area is drawn. The slow step is crown segmentation on the first pass over an unidentified arch — that wait happens once per scan and isn't repeated afterward."},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#coince","p":"FlexReg — register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If it gets stuck","x":"A graphics card is mandatory Building the patch and running the registration only work with a compatible GPU. Without one, these steps fail outright — this isn't a performance nicety, it's a hard requirement of the module. \"The registration only looks at the teeth I checked\" FlexReg aligns only the area you marked out — never the whole arch. If the result looks off away from that area, that's expected: the rest of the mesh simply follows the motion computed on the patch, it isn't registered independently. Mucogingival landmarks can be unreliable On the lower arch, some automatically placed poi"},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#apres","p":"FlexReg — register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What happens next","x":"The registered scan and its transformation matrix are ready to use directly in AREG_IOS and in the other tools in the repository that overlay scans across time. As with any automatic registration, a quick visual check before using it for a measurement is still good practice."},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#lire-plus","p":"FlexReg — register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"No publication describes FlexReg: neither the region you draw by hand nor the registration that follows. The readings below explain the approach, not the module. Around the method Hutin et al., ShapeMI 2023 — AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOralScans . The published half of the family: a network finds the stable palatal region on its own, where FlexReg leaves you to draw it. Vasilakos et al., Scientific Reports 2017 — Assessment of different techniques for 3D superimposition of serial digital maxillary dental casts on palatal structures . Compares the palatal regions usable as a refer"},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html","p":"GreedyReg — register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"GreedyReg aligns two CBCT scans of the same patient (a T1 and a T2) by driving the ITK-SNAP greedy engine. No neural network in the main computation, nothing to install: the result comes from a classic intensity-based optimization, not a prediction."},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#quand","p":"GreedyReg — register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"To register two CBCT scans of the same modality — a before/after follow-up, for instance — when you want fine control over the computation: which metric, how many degrees of freedom, optionally a mask to focus the optimization on one region. It is an end-of-chain tool: nothing else in SADT consumes its result. If you need the resulting transform to be reused elsewhere in Slicer, this is not the right module — its matrix stays specific to greedy and is not a Slicer transform file. Three modes, one real registration Only Automatic Registration actually performs an intensity-based registration. M"},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#besoin","p":"GreedyReg — register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide Two folders of CBCT scans in .nii.gz : one for the fixed volume (T1), one for the moving volume (T2) File names starting with letters then digits, for example A01_T1.nii.gz — that is what lets GreedyReg pair a T1 with its T2 for the same patient; any other naming is never paired, with no warning Optional: a mask (segmentation or labelmap), in the space of the fixed volume Optional: an initialization matrix, or a manual pre-alignment done inside the module What comes out The T2 resampled into the T1 grid, as .nii.gz , plus the affine matrix found In Distant mode: only a starting matr"},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#modes","p":"GreedyReg — register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"The three modes, and which one to pick","x":"Manual Alignment. You move the moving volume by hand (sliders, or the \"Center T2 on T1\" button for a quick centering). Useful when the two CBCT scans are already close and only a rough adjustment is missing before running the actual computation. Automatic Registration. The only mode that actually registers: the greedy engine optimizes a rigid or affine transform from image intensities. This is where you pick the metric (NMI by default, for heterogeneous intensities; NCC for a local correlation; SSD if both volumes share the same modality) and the degrees of freedom (rigid or full affine). Dist"},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#marche","p":"GreedyReg — register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Split your volumes. One folder for the fixed CBCT scans (T1), one for the moving ones (T2), with the same patient identifier in each file name. Add a mask if needed. It must be in the space of the fixed volume: that is where the optimization will look for its gradient. Pre-align if the misalignment is large. By hand in Manual mode for a small gap, via Distant Registration for a large one. Switch to Automatic Registration. Pick the metric and the degrees of freedom (rigid or affine), then run. For a series , point directly at the full T1/T2 folders: batch processing chains the pairs matched by "},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#coince","p":"GreedyReg — register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something goes wrong","x":"\"Save Registered Volume\" does nothing in Distant mode This mode does not produce a registered volume, only a starting matrix: the button always answers \"No result to save!\". Use \"Save Transform Matrix\" instead, or go straight to Automatic Registration to get a volume worth saving. Misleading checkboxes in Distant mode Checking several structures (cranial base, mandible, maxilla) does not combine them. Only one region is actually used, chosen by a fixed priority order (cranial base first, then mandible, then maxilla), regardless of which boxes you checked. In batch, one failure stops everything"},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#apres","p":"GreedyReg — register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"And after","x":"Always check the result by eye, overlaying the registered volume on the fixed CBCT in Slicer, before using it for a measurement. And keep in mind that the exported matrix is a plain greedy-format file, not a Slicer or ITK transform: reusing it outside this module needs a conversion that GreedyReg does not perform itself."},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#lire-plus","p":"GreedyReg — register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"On GreedyReg itself Venet et al., arXiv 2019 — Accurate and Robust Alignment of Variable-Stained Histologic Images Using a General-Purpose Greedy Diffeomorphic Registration Tool . The paper for greedy , the engine this module merely drives. Short, and it is the citation its authors ask for. Around the method Yushkevich et al., NeuroImage 2006 — User-guided 3D active contour segmentation of anatomical structures: Significantly improved efficiency and reliability . The ITK-SNAP reference, which greedy came out of and whose vocabulary the module borrows. Documentation de greedy — greedy — command"},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html","p":"MRI2CBCT — align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"MRI2CBCT registers an MRI onto a CBCT of the temporomandibular joint (TMJ), so you can look at both exams in the same frame. It's a long pipeline — six independent steps, one per tab — but each one is short and sets up the next."},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#quand","p":"MRI2CBCT — align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you have, for the same patient, an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ and you want them superimposed: bone shows up better on the CBCT, soft tissue (disc, capsule) better on the MRI, and registering the two lets you look at both at once instead of comparing them from memory. This isn't a one-click tool: plan on going through all six tabs, in order, for each patient. Six tabs, not one button Each tab has its own button and writes its files to its own folder; nothing in the module stops you from running them out of order or skipping one. Two tabs are genuinely optional (the left/right crop and t"},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#besoin","p":"MRI2CBCT — align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what it gives back","x":"To provide A CBCT and an MRI (T1 and/or T2) of the same patient A segmentation of the condyle on the CBCT — you can supply it, or let the module produce it itself during the joint-crop step Files named <ID>_CBCT… , <ID>_MR… or <ID>_MRI… , as .nii.gz — this shared identifier is what ties a patient's files together from one step to the next What comes out The MRI registered onto the CBCT, and the matrix of the applied transformation Two volumes you can now overlay in the same scene — not a merged file One folder per intermediate step, kept or deleted through a checkbox"},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#marche","p":"MRI2CBCT — align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Tab by tab","x":"MRI orientation / centering. Rewrites the header of the MRI volume (the voxels themselves don't move) to a fixed convention, and recenters the origin to match. If you check \"Bilateral MRI\", it also fixes the inter-slice spacing that this kind of acquisition often loses when converted to NIfTI. Mandatory in practice: the final registration has no starting transform and no search margin, so the MRI and CBCT need to already sit in comparable orientations before you get there. Left/right crop (optional). Useful if your acquisition covers both TMJs in a single volume: splits the CBCT, MRI and segme"},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#coince","p":"MRI2CBCT — align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If it gets stuck","x":"An install prompt keeps coming back Automatic dependency installation fails every time (a misspelled package name that doesn't exist). You'll see an installation error before almost every button, but the action carries on anyway regardless. If the rest of your setup is already in place, that changes nothing; but if condyle segmentation later fails without a clear message, this is the likely cause — the real dependency was never installed on its own, and you'll need to install it yourself. Two paths that crash the module In manual approximation, \"Confirm & Save Alignment\" crashes if the MRI vol"},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#apres","p":"MRI2CBCT — align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Afterward","x":"The original CBCT is never modified: only the MRI is moved and then registered. Open both volumes together in Slicer to visually check the overlay before using it for any measurement. If the final registration can't find a good alignment, the problem is almost always a too-rough starting approximation (step 4) rather than the registration itself — redo that step before trying again."},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#lire-plus","p":"MRI2CBCT — align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"On MRI2CBCT itself Leroux et al., CLIP 2024 (LNCS 15196) — Novel CBCT-MRI Registration Approach for Enhanced Analysis of Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease . The module's paper: the MRI ↔ CBCT registration approach on the temporomandibular joint. Gaydamour et al., CLIP 2025 (LNCS 16126) — AI-Driven Multimodal TMJ Patient Modeling: From Unstructured Notes to Precision Treatment . The follow-up: this registration put back into the full patient analysis pipeline. Around the method Al-Saleh et al., J Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2016 — MRI and CBCT image registration of temporomandibular j"},{"u":"guide/MedX.html","p":"MedX — summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"Despite the name, MedX never looks at an image: it reads written clinical reports ( .pdf , .docx , .txt ), has a local language model turn each one into a structured summary, and can then roll a whole cohort of those summaries into a single page of figures. Two distinct steps, worth knowing before you start."},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#quand","p":"MedX — summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"When you have a pile of free-text reports — clinical exam, radiographic exam, consultation notes — and want the clinical indicators out of them (age, pain, sleep, mouth opening, and so on) without reading each one by hand. Once you have those summaries, the second half of MedX lets you see the whole cohort at a glance: age breakdowns, associated disorders, average scores. Two independent paths MedX has two tabs, two buttons, and nothing chains them together automatically. Summarize reads your documents and writes one summary file per patient. Dashboard reads those summaries back and produces t"},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#besoin","p":"MedX — summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"Summarize path What to provide A flat folder of reports as .pdf , .docx , or .txt (no subfolders) File names where everything before the first \"_\" is the patient ID — several files sharing that ID are grouped and summarized together The summarization model, downloaded once What comes out One <patient-id>_summary.txt file per patient, in free text Your source documents are left untouched Dashboard path What to provide The summary folder produced by the Summarize path (files must end in _summary.txt ) What comes out A dashboard.png image: age, sleep, joint and muscle pain, mouth opening, across "},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#marche","p":"MedX — summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Summarize the reports Download the model. Only needed once; without it, the Summarize path has nothing to work from. Point it to the folder of reports , named with a patient ID before the first \"_\". Set an output folder , then launch. A progress bar tracks patients as they're processed. Read through the summaries you get before relying on them — see below. Build the dashboard Switch to the Dashboard tab. This doesn't happen on its own after summarization. Set the summary folder from the previous step as input. Set an output folder , then launch. On the first run, Slicer may ask to install a co"},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#relecture","p":"MedX — summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Reading the result","x":"Each report's summary comes from a generative model: it writes free text, not a guaranteed form. The key: value format the dashboard expects is a habit the model learned, not an enforced rule — a malformed line is simply dropped, and silently missing from the dashboard. The model also samples part of its output: running the summary again on the same document doesn't produce the exact same text twice. For both reasons, a summary MedX generates is a starting point to read through, not a validated report — don't use it as-is for a clinical decision."},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#coince","p":"MedX — summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"The model download button does nothing Clicking the download button creates the expected folder but never puts a file in it: the field stays empty and a warning message appears. Get the model archive yourself from the GitHub releases page of the SlicerAutomatedDentalTools repository (the BART.zip file, under the \"MedX\" release), unzip it, then point the module at that folder. The dashboard progress bar never moves Unlike the Summarize path, the Dashboard progress bar stays still for the whole run. That isn't a sign it's stuck: let it run and wait for dashboard.png to show up in the output fold"},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#lire-plus","p":"MedX — summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"On MedX itself Gaydamour et al., CLIP 2025 (LNCS 16126) — AI-Driven Multimodal TMJ Patient Modeling: From Unstructured Notes to Precision Treatment . The paper for the pipeline MedX is the text link in: going from free reports to usable data. Around the method Lewis et al., ACL 2020 — BART: Denoising Sequence-to-Sequence Pre-training for Natural Language Generation, Translation, and Comprehension . The upstream model MedX fine-tunes. Explains what the summariser can do — and that it rephrases, so it can invent. Bianchi et al., Seminars in Orthodontics 2021 — Decision Support Systems in Temporo"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"MedicalDataAnonymizer finds and replaces personal data — names, dates, addresses, ID numbers — inside text documents ( .docx , .txt , .pdf , .csv , .xml , .odt ). It is not a DICOM anonymiser : it never reads, modifies, or erases a single medical image. On video Medical Data Anonymizer: Your AI model who anonymize all your medical text files 41 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel This does not anonymise images Despite the name, no DICOM tag is touched — not the patient's name, birth date, study identifier, or institution — and no face is removed from a CBCT or scanner volume. If images are wha"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#quand","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"For a first pass over a batch of English-language reports, letters, or notes that need the most visible mentions stripped before you share or export them — not to declare a dataset de-identified in one step. The output still needs a read-through, and it doesn't substitute for renaming files or handling the images separately."},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#besoin","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of text documents: .docx , .txt , .pdf , .csv , .xml , or .odt The dependencies installed once (a dedicated button), with Slicer restarted afterward The categories of data to detect, checked in the list — seven are checked by default (names, phone numbers, emails, dates, addresses, US social security numbers, medical license numbers) What comes out A copy of each document, suffixed _anonymized , in the output folder A file_mappings.csv file listing original and output file names — don't hand this out together with the documents Your original files are left untouched, b"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#marche","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Install the dependencies (once), using the dedicated button. The module downloads Presidio and an English text-analysis model; Slicer then asks to be restarted. Point it to the source folder. It's walked in full, subfolders included; there's no need to process documents one at a time. Check the categories to detect. Only add what you actually need beyond the seven checked by default (credit card, IP address, URL, US driver's license…). Pick a replacement method in \"Advanced Options\": tag replacement ( <PERSON> , the default), redaction, masking with asterisks, or hashing. A hash stays the same"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#verifier","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Check the result","x":"Before sharing anything, reopen every _anonymized file and compare it against the original: that's the only way to catch a silent failure (see above). Then rename the files yourself — the patient's name survives in the output file name — and don't attach file_mappings.csv to anything sent outside your team: it lists original file names, not a usable re-identification key."},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#limites","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What the detection misses","x":"The language is fixed to English. A folder in French or any other language runs through the same model, with no warning, and recall drops sharply. The default confidence threshold drops the least certain hits — typically rare or non-Anglo-Saxon names. Hospital identifiers — medical record number, accession number, study ID, bed number — aren't searched for by any of the categories on offer. Non-US ID numbers — a French social security number, an NHS number, a passport — aren't covered; only US formats are recognised. Content that couldn't be extracted from a file (tables in a .docx , attribute"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#coince","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"Dependency installation fails It needs internet access to download Presidio and the language model. If installation errors out, check the connection and try again; Slicer will need restarting once it succeeds either way. Two files with the same name, only one comes out The output folder is written flat: the input's subfolder structure isn't reproduced, and if two subfolders each hold a file with the same name, the second one silently overwrites the first. Rename ahead of time if that's a risk. A .csv comes out unreadable It's the most fragile format here: columns aren't preserved as-is. For ta"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#lire-plus","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer — de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"No publication: the module assembles existing pieces (Presidio, spaCy). What is worth reading is what those pieces let through — automatic anonymisation is never complete. Around the method Alrazihi et al., Surgical Neurology International 2025 — Evaluating the accuracy of automated and semi-automated anonymization tools for unstructured health records . Evaluates Presidio itself on free-text records: the share of identifying data that survives the pass. Stubbs & Uzuner, J Biomed Inform 2015 — Annotating longitudinal clinical narratives for de-identification: The 2014 i2b2/UTHealth corpus . Th"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html","p":"SurgMovPred — estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"SurgMovPred doesn't look at your volumes or your meshes: it takes a spreadsheet of cephalometric measurements and turns it into a numeric estimate of the bone displacements expected after orthognathic surgery — how far a point such as Pogonion or the posterior nasal spine should move, along the three axes of space. No imaging, no neural network: 112 small, independent statistical models, one per predicted value, applied row by row to your table. On video Surgical Movement Prediction: Machine learning model to help surgery preparation 28 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#quand","p":"SurgMovPred — estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Use it once you already have, per patient, a set of cephalometric measurements — the pre-surgical state and the intended occlusal outcome — and you want a quick numeric estimate of the corresponding bone displacements, across a whole batch of patients at once rather than one at a time. It's a numeric complement to your own surgical planning, not a 3D visualization tool. A spreadsheet-only tool SurgMovPred doesn't read any volume, surface, or Slicer landmark, and it doesn't touch your 3D scene: nothing is created or displayed there. Everything happens on disk — a folder of spreadsheets in, a fo"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#format","p":"SurgMovPred — estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"The input spreadsheet format","x":"This is where most people get stuck with this module: what it expects on input is quite specific, and nothing warns you clearly when it isn't met. A folder, not a single file. Every .csv , .xlsx , and .ods file inside is read and stacked into one big table, with no check that their columns actually match. Subfolders aren't scanned. Only one sheet per workbook. For an Excel or .ods file, only the first sheet is read. If your workbook has several sheets (demographics, raw measurements…), make sure the one with per-patient measurements comes first. Column headers get lightly cleaned up automatica"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#besoin","p":"SurgMovPred — estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of spreadsheets ( .csv , .xlsx , or .ods ) in the format described above The trained models, downloaded once (dedicated button, about 700 MB) An output folder What comes out Two identical spreadsheets ( .xlsx and .csv ) in the output folder One row per input patient, one column per predicted displacement value Your input spreadsheets and your Slicer scene are left untouched"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#marche","p":"SurgMovPred — estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Download the models. Dedicated button, about 700 MB, only needed once but required before the first run. Prepare your input folder following the format above — one consolidated spreadsheet or several files, either way, as long as the expected columns are there. Set the three folders : input, downloaded models, output. Run it. There's nothing to tune: no threshold, no target selection, no skeletal-class option. All 112 values are predicted at once, for every row of the assembled table. Collect the two output spreadsheets from the folder you chose."},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#lire","p":"SurgMovPred — estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to read the result — and not read too much into it","x":"Each output column is a predicted displacement value for a given point on the maxilla, the mandible, or the genioplasty segment, expressed along three components of the cephalometric frame (front/back, left/right, up/down), plus a fourth \"Total\" column that's most likely an overall magnitude. The unit is written nowhere in the tool; it's probably millimeters, but that's an inference, not a stated certainty. Each point is predicted on its own, not the whole segment The 112 output values come from 112 independent models, one per value. Nothing guarantees that the ten points predicted for the max"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#coince","p":"SurgMovPred — estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"Clicking Apply reinstalls a dependency every time Clicking Apply always forces a reinstall of numpy, even when everything was already working. That makes the run slower, and swapping out a core numerical library inside an already-running Slicer session can unsettle other tools that depend on it. If another module starts misbehaving after you've used SurgMovPred, restart Slicer before looking any further. A missing column in the output isn't a crash If one of the expected values is missing from the result, nothing errors out visibly: the model behind it was simply skipped because one of the col"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#lire-plus","p":"SurgMovPred — estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"No publication describes the module. Its only public description is a NA-MIC project report, and the models it runs are evaluated nowhere. On SurgMovPred itself NA-MIC Project Week 45, Boston 2026 — New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery . The project report: the intent, the starting data, and the state of the work. Around the method de Oliveira et al., Orthod Craniofac Res 2024 — Artificial intelligence as a prediction tool for orthognathic surgery assessment . The same lab on the same question, but published and evaluated. Read it before trusting a predict"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html","p":"VFACE — classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"VFACE measures and classifies skeletal facial asymmetry from a single CBCT per patient. You don't need a second scan for comparison: the module builds its own \"mirror\" of the patient and compares the patient against that reflection. On video Classify facial Asymmetry and longitudinal studies, here is VFACE the new tool who do both 33 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel The flow as the lab presents it: ASO orients the scan, ALI_CBCT places the landmarks, AutoMatrix builds the reflection, BatchDentalSeg segments, AReg registers the patient onto their mirror, and the classification comes out at t"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html#quand","p":"VFACE — classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you want to objectify a facial asymmetry on a CBCT — quantify it rather than eyeball it — and possibly find out whether it involves the mandible, the maxilla, or both. VFACE starts from a raw volume: no segmentation or landmarks to prepare beforehand, it handles all of that itself. The heaviest module in the extension VFACE is not a standalone tool: it's an orchestrator that chains, one after another, six other modules of the extension (resampling, orientation, bone segmentation, registration, dental surface segmentation, classification). On a batch of patients, expect the full run to"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html#besoin","p":"VFACE — classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide A folder of raw T1 CBCTs, one per patient ( .nii , .nii.gz , .nrrd , .nrrd.gz , .gipl , .gipl.gz ) No prior segmentation or landmarks: the module starts from the volume alone The trained models (ALI, AMASSS, DentalSegmentator, the VFACE classifiers), to download once An output folder, on a drive with room to spare What comes out A per-patient table of measurements (skeletal distances and angles) A final classification file stating whether the patient is symmetric or asymmetric, and whether the asymmetry involves the mandible and/or the maxilla Optionally, bone surfaces and color dis"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html#marche","p":"VFACE — classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Download the models. A dedicated button fetches all the weights needed by the chained modules. Only needed once. Point to the input folder containing your T1 CBCTs, and to an output folder. Choose the analysis mode. For an asymmetry study (the common case), VFACE builds the patient's mirror and produces the classification. A \"longitudinal studies\" mode exists to compare two real time points of the same patient, but it doesn't produce a classification in that case. Choose what you want as output. Measurements and classification (quantitative mode), surfaces and distance maps (visualization mode"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html#lire","p":"VFACE — classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to read the result","x":"Before the table, the picture. This is the measurement the classifier is fed from — the patient against their own mirror image, and the gap that is left after superimposing on one structure. Cranial base Maxilla Mandible Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom Mirror again 0 mm 8 mm+ 0. t1_landmarks_orientation_max — ALI searches ANS IF PNS UL6O UR1O UR6O 1. t1_oriented_max — semi_aso_cbct orients on the maxilla 2. t1_landmarks_orientation_cb — ALI places Ba LPo N RPo S LOr ROr 3. t1_oriented_cb — the mid-sagittal plane now lies on x = 0 4. t1_masks — AMASSS segments cranial base, maxilla, ma"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html#lire-plus","p":"VFACE — classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Further reading","x":"On VFACE itself Buisson, poster IADR 2026 — Automated Classification of Facial Asymmetry, Identification of Regional Asymmetry Patterns . The poster presented at the 104th IADR General Session, San Diego. The UNC Adams School of Dentistry round-up gives its title and author; the poster itself is not deposited anywhere public, and the IADR archives are not freely indexed. SPIE Medical Imaging 2026 — Automated classification of skeletal facial asymmetry in CBCT using a reproducible 3D Slicer workflow . The only publication describing the pipeline. Paywalled, and to be read knowing it describes a"},{"u":"guide/index.html","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"What each tool does, what to feed it, what it returns — without going into the code. For the internal mechanisms, switch to the Atlas."},{"u":"guide/index.html#pour-qui","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Who this guide is for","x":"Anyone who opens SlicerAutomatedDentalTools in 3D Slicer and wants to use it: clinicians, researchers, students. It assumes no ability to read code and no idea what a neural network is. If what you want is the inner workings — which model is called, when, on what data — you want the Atlas instead: both sections cover the same 20 modules, each from the opposite end."},{"u":"guide/index.html#pourquoi","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Why it exists","x":"The extension gathers 20 modules written by different teams at different times. Their documentation amounts, depending on the case, to a few lines of README, a project page, or nothing at all. Several behave differently from what their publication describes: the paper documents a version the code no longer runs. This guide was written by reading each module's code, not its documentation. So it says what the tool actually does when you click, including when that is inconvenient: 79 warnings and 4 bugs are flagged at the exact point where you are likely to hit them. Nothing is fixed here — the p"},{"u":"guide/index.html#plan","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What every page contains","x":"They all follow the same plan, so you know where to look without rereading: When to use it — which problem the tool answers, and the cases where it is of no help. What you need, what you get — the expected inputs, their format, and what comes out at the end. Step by step — the steps in order, field by field. When it goes wrong — the common failures and what causes them. Further reading — the publications behind the tool where there are any, and the pointer to the Atlas page."},{"u":"guide/index.html#commencer","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Where to start","x":"If you know which tool you are after, take it from the list below. Otherwise start from what you have to do: Compare two scans of the same patient over time → Registration Isolate structures in a CBCT → Segmentation Place anatomical landmarks, or put a scan back upright → Landmarks & orientation Measure, classify or predict from a shape → Analysis Crop or apply a matrix, in batch → Utilities Extract or anonymise clinical text → Text & language"},{"u":"guide/index.html#registration","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Registration","x":"FlexReg Register two intraoral scans by picking the stable area yourself AREG_IOS Register two scans of the same patient, with nothing to draw AREG_CBCT Superimpose two CBCT scans of the same patient taken at two dates AREG_IOSCBCT Place an intraoral scan into the frame of a CBCT GreedyReg Register two CBCT scans using the ITK-SNAP engine MRI2CBCT Align an MRI and a CBCT of the temporomandibular joint"},{"u":"guide/index.html#segmentation","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Segmentation","x":"AMASSS Segment mandible, maxilla and other structures from a CBCT BatchDentalSeg Segment teeth and bone across a series of scans in one run"},{"u":"guide/index.html#landmarks","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Landmarks & orientation","x":"ALI Place anatomical landmarks automatically ASO Bring scans into a shared orientation"},{"u":"guide/index.html#analysis","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Analysis","x":"VFACE Measure and classify a patient's facial asymmetry DOCShapeAXI Classify 3D shapes and see what drove the decision CLIC Find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT SurgMovPred Estimate the expected bone movement after surgery"},{"u":"guide/index.html#utilities","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Utilities","x":"AutoCrop3D Crop a series of volumes to the same region AutoMatrix Apply registration matrices across a whole series"},{"u":"guide/index.html#text","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Text & language","x":"CNE Pull structured information out of clinical notes MedX Summarise clinical reports and build a dashboard from them MedicalDataAnonymizer Strip personal data from a text document Agent Ask in plain language which tool to run"},{"u":"index.html","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools — how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"One page per tool, organised around the pipeline : what is computed, what is predicted by a network, when the model gets called, what it actually does, what the preprocessing is, and what comes out. This is not a user manual. Code links point to the ~/Projects/UNC/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools repository."},{"u":"index.html#lecture","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools — how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"Where to start","x":"From the bottom of the chain to the top. The first two steps are the ones everything else depends on. # Page Why this one first 1 FlexReg The simplest, everything is explicit. Introduces the LPS / RAS conventions, crown segmentation, and patch-based ICP . 2 ALI and AMASSS The two landmark providers everything else depends on. 3 ASO Orientation, a prerequisite for most registrations. 4 AREG_IOS then AREG_CBCT The registrations themselves. 5 VFACE Highest in the chain: it stacks all the others."},{"u":"index.html#briques","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools — how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"The three shared building blocks","x":"Almost every module rests on the same three components. Understanding them once means not re-reading the same mechanics across five pages. Who calls what, found by grepping slicer.modules.* . A filled dot means \"actually launches\". ALI sits in the path of all six modules — it's the building block to understand first. Details in ALI and AMASSS ."},{"u":"index.html#fiches","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools — how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"All the pages","x":"Registration FlexReg IOS registration with a hand-drawn patch AREG_IOS IOS registration with a predicted patch — the automatic FlexReg AREG_CBCT T1/T2 CBCT registration AREG_IOSCBCT multimodal IOS ↔ CBCT registration GreedyReg ITK-SNAP-style CBCT registration, Greedy engine MRI2CBCT multimodal MRI ↔ CBCT registration Segmentation AMASSS craniofacial structure segmentation on CBCT BatchDentalSeg DentalSegmentator and variants, in batch Landmarks and orientation ALI automatic landmark placement, CBCT and IOS ASO automatic scan orientation, CBCT and IOS Analysis and classification VFACE facial as"},{"u":"index.html#carte","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools — how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"Call map","x":"Found by grepping slicer.modules.* and conda run -m <module> invocations. An arrow means \"actually launches\". Agent ──────────────► Agent_CLI ──► (routes to any module) VFACE ──────────────► AMASSS_CLI ├────────────► ALI_CBCT ├────────────► AREG_CBCT ├────────────► Automatrix_CLI └────────────► VFACE_CLI AREG ───────────────► AREG_CBCT / AREG_IOS / AREG_IOSCBCT ├────────────► AMASSS_CLI ├────────────► ALI_CBCT / ALI_IOS └────────────► dentalmodelseg (CrownSegmentationcli) ASO ────────────────► PRE_ASO_CBCT / SEMI_ASO_CBCT ├────────────► PRE_ASO_IOS / SEMI_ASO_IOS ├────────────► ALI_CBCT └─────"},{"u":"index.html#divergences","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools — how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"When the paper doesn't describe the code","x":"A frequent case here, flagged at the top of the relevant SOURCES pages. Tool The paper describes The code does AMASSS UNETR MONAI , 128³ crop nnU-Net v2 PlainConvUNet VFACE unsupervised UMAP + spectral clustering 3 supervised binary LightGBM models DOCShapeAXI ResNet-18, condyles as binary dual-branch 4096 points + 42 views, 4-class checkpoint AREG_CBCT two passes, SimpleElastix one pass, itk-elastix"},{"u":"index.html#constats","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools — how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"What the reading turned up","x":"Found while documenting, not while hunting for bugs. The \"verified\" column distinguishes what was reproduced or confirmed directly from the code from what a page reports and still needs checking. Where Symptom Verified MedicalDataAnonymizer anonymize_text_presidio does return text on any exception: the _anonymized file holds the untouched text, and the UI shows \"Complete!\" yes, read line by line CLIC the [SEG] / [PROGRESS] markers are emitted through logger.info with a prefix, the reader tests startswith : the segmentation is never loaded into Slicer yes BatchDentalSeg NasoMaxillaDentSeg: the "},{"u":"index.html#conda","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools — how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"Shared conda environment","x":"Coupling worth knowing ALI , AREG , ASO , DOCShapeAXI and FlexReg share the same conda environment shapeaxi , on Python 3.12 . A change to the Python version breaks all of them at once. Details in FlexReg , Environment section."},{"u":"index.html#organisation","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools — how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"Folder layout","x":"One folder per tool. Each one holds: <Tool>.html — the pipeline, read from the code; SOURCES.html — the bibliography: what's deposited here, what's meant to be read online, and the \"neighbourhood\" (related work that does not describe the code); the retrieved documents, as PDF where the publisher allows it, otherwise as .xml (JATS, the full text) with a readable .txt alongside it. The original .md files are kept next to the HTML pages. 29 PDFs and 54 full texts, all verified: the PDFs are paginated, the XML files carry a text body. Nothing was obtained by working around a paywall — arXiv, PLOS,"}];